stay isolated; this avoids the shared-queue contention that would otherwise turn a per-scenario expectedAttempts assertion into a flake under concurrent goroutines. Forward-applicable to every BE-039x chaos-timeout sibling whose invariant is `client classifies X dependency as typed *yerr.Error with Code=Y under fault Z` (BE-0394 chaos Postgres disconnects, future chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit suites). The fixture-table closed-set member's per-scenario knobs — name, method, expectedAttempts, expectedCode, expectedDependency, call closure — are the reusable shape; the BE-0394 Postgres-disconnect sibling extends this by swapping the fake-Dokploy `TimeoutFault` for a fakepg.DisconnectFault and the typed-client surface for the store layer's pgx pool, keeping every other knob.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, load-smoke instance (BE-0392 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_load_smoke_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/load_smoke_test.go's new canonical pair TestLoadSmokeContractCoversCoreEndpoints + TestLoadSmokeContractRunsBurstWithStableEnvelopes + SECURITY.md "## Load Smoke Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: load smoke tests (BE-0392)" subsection; FOURTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0391 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestLoadSmoke ./...`, CI step name `Load smoke tests`, verify.sh prefix #17 inserted between BE-0390's #16 and the optionals — optionals renumbered #17→#18 govulncheck, #18→#19 staticcheck, #19→#20 golangci-lint, #20→#21 goreleaser in the SAME edit; TENTH consecutive required-step insertion with optional renumbering since BE-0380's #6). Two BE-0392-specific extensions to the template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing subject is an HTTP handler under burst load: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × DETERMINISTIC DUAL — NO POSTGRES MEMBER` — unlike BE-0389/BE-0390 whose canonical pair splits across deterministic-fixture-only + Postgres-required substrates, BE-0392's BOTH pair members are deterministic. The bootstrap surface (`/healthz`, `/readyz`, `/version`) is the one part of the public HTTP API that needs no backing infrastructure — no Postgres, no fake Dokploy fixtures, no quota counters mutated, no scope-resolution chain. The closed-set member walks an in-memory endpoint table; the burst member spins the test handler in-process via httptest.NewServer and fires 16 workers × 32 iterations = 512 concurrent requests per endpoint. Neither member calls t.Skip on missing env vars — the gate trips on every developer machine and every CI runner without setup. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a stateless HTTP surface or a pure-in-process invariant (BE-0407 desired-state goldens against in-memory reconciler, BE-0398 tenant isolation matrix at the policy layer alone). Critical implication: the BE-0389/BE-0390 dual-substrate shape is NOT mandatory for the canonical-pair template — when both invariants are deterministic, the pair can stay deterministic-only and the SECURITY.md section's "skips when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset" language is OMITTED (BE-0392's section instead explicitly says "no Postgres, no live Dokploy server"). The shared `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` opt-in-external substring stays in the substring set as the cross-suite escape hatch reference, but does NOT bind to any code path the canonical pair member exercises. (2) `BURST OBSERVATION COLLECTION SHAPE — channel buffered to total request count, observation struct includes endpoint + status + schema + ok + requestID + bodyBad + headerBad, then post-burst aggregation in main goroutine` — the burst test must avoid testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged); instead each goroutine pushes a typed `observation{}` onto a buffered channel sized to `len(endpoints) * workers * iterations` and the main goroutine drains the channel after wg.Wait()+close. Aggregation builds (a) a per-endpoint count map asserted against the expected burst total, (b) a per-request-id map for uniqueness assertion, (c) per-observation envelope/status/redaction assertions. Failure messages quote the offending endpoint AND the observed request_id (or "<missing>" sentinel + a synthetic "<request-construction-failed:...>" / "<request-do-failed:...>" id when the round-trip itself failed) so the operator correlates against logs without re-running. The burst total (16 × 32 × 3 = 1536 requests) completes in ~600ms on a developer laptop — safely under the flaky-test threshold. Forward-applicable to BE-0393 (chaos Dokploy timeouts — same channel+aggregation shape, but with a fault-injection step before each iteration), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — same shape but with t.Skip on YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — same shape but each iteration writes the same Idempotency-Key and asserts every response after the first is the cached envelope). The closed-set bootstrap endpoint table includes a `wantStatus` field per entry so a future endpoint that legitimately returns 503 under some condition can be added without breaking the burst member's status assertion. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0390's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0392 substring count = 17; matches BE-0389's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite UMBRELLA-shape adaptation, race-detector instance (BE-0391 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_race_detector_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Race Detector" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: race detector tests (BE-0391)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the umbrella shape — first was BE-0379's `go test ./...` — and THIRTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0390 verification-suite mega-pattern overall, canonical command `go test -race ./...`, CI step name `Race detector`, verify.sh prefix #5 STABLE since BE-0379). Three BE-0391-specific extensions to the template, all forward-applicable to ANY future umbrella verification story whose canonical command is a flag (`-race`, `-msan`, `-asan`, `-cover`) or an unfiltered `./...` rather than a `-run` filter: (1) `NO CANONICAL TEST-FUNCTION PAIR` — the `-race` flag wraps every `Test*` function the suite runs at the Go runtime layer, so there is no function-name prefix to pin (unlike BE-0383..BE-0390 whose `-run TestXxx` filter binds to a specific prefix). The umbrella shape mirrors BE-0379's `go test ./...` gate, but BE-0379 had `allowedPackagesWithoutUnitTests` + `findPackagesMissingTests` as its analog "closed-set" surface (every package MUST have a *_test.go file OR be in the exemption set); BE-0391 has NEITHER closed-set surface because every package's tests are AUTOMATICALLY in scope when `-race` is set — there is nothing for the test to enumerate. The static test pins exactly 5 surfaces (no canonical-pair surface = 5 not 6): ci.yml step, verify.sh step, CONTRIBUTING entry, SECURITY row + section, PRD command(s). Forward-applicable to any future flag-style umbrella gate (race detector variants, msan, asan, coverage thresholds). (2) `TWO-ENTRY PRD PIN — `requiredLocalCommands` AND `requiredBackendCommands`` — unlike every prior verification-suite story (BE-0379..BE-0390) that pinned a single PRD command entry (BE-0379 in requiredLocalCommands, BE-0380..BE-0390 in requiredBackendCommands), BE-0391 pins BOTH: `go test -race ./...` in requiredLocalCommands AND `go test -race ./internal/controlplane/...` in requiredBackendCommands. The umbrella command is the canonical gate; the backend-narrowed command is the faster mid-iteration variant an agent can run without paying the full umbrella cost. Both are part of the contract because each serves a different agent workflow (final pre-commit gate vs. mid-iteration check). The two-entry pin is forward-applicable to ANY future verification gate whose canonical command has a naturally faster narrow variant (e.g. a future `go test -msan ./...` gate would also pin a `go test -msan ./internal/controlplane/...` faster variant). The matcher uses a shared `containsString([]string, string) bool` helper (declared in verification_suite_race_detector_static_test.go) instead of inlining the loop — this helper is now a reusable release_test package primitive future PRD-array matchers can call. (3) `STABLE PRE-EXISTING NUMERIC SLOT — NO RENUMBER NEEDED` — unlike BE-0380..BE-0390 each of which inserted a NEW required step into verify.sh and renumbered the trailing optionals in the same edit (NINE consecutive insertions from BE-0380's #6 to BE-0390's #16), BE-0391's slot #5 was STABLE since BE-0379's foundation work — verify.sh, CONTRIBUTING.md, and ci.yml already carried the race-detector step / header / entry. The BE-0391 work was purely DEFENSIVE: it added the static test that PINS those pre-existing surfaces so a future regression (rename, renumber, deletion) trips the gate. Critical implication: when a verification story's surfaces ALREADY EXIST at story start (because they were authored as foundation work before the verification-suite mega-pattern landed), the implementation is JUST the new static test + new SECURITY.md section + new AGENTS.md subsection + PRD flag flip — no ci.yml / verify.sh / CONTRIBUTING.md edits at all. Forward-applicable to any future verification story whose canonical command predates the mega-pattern (BE-0379's `go test ./...`, BE-0391's `go test -race ./...`, future `go vet ./...` / `gofmt -l .` umbrellas if those ever get verification-suite stories). The SECURITY.md substring set adapts: race-detector substring set has 11 entries (vs BE-0389's 17) because the umbrella gate has fewer per-suite-specific literals to pin — no canonical function pair to quote, no `-run` filter pivot, no per-suite env var beyond the shared `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`. The 11 substrings anchor on the umbrella contract pivots: `data race`, `go test -race`, `Every push and PR`, `deterministic`, `request_id`, `fake Dokploy`, `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`, `redacted`, `yalla.output.v1`, `yalla.error.v1`, and the self-locating file path. NOT applicable to focused `-run`-filter verification stories (BE-0392 load smoke, BE-0393 chaos Dokploy timeouts, BE-0394 chaos Postgres disconnects, BE-0395 idempotency replay, etc.) — those follow the BE-0389/BE-0390 canonical-pair shape; the umbrella shape applies ONLY when the canonical command itself is a runtime-flag or unfiltered umbrella.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, migrations-from-empty-DB instance (BE-0389 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_migrations_empty_db_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrate_test.go's new canonical pair TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles + TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase + SECURITY.md "## Migration Tests From Empty DB" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: migration tests from empty DB (BE-0389)" subsection; ELEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0388 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...`, CI step name `Migration tests from empty DB`, verify.sh prefix #15). Four BE-0389-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × POSTGRES-REQUIRED DUAL` — unlike every prior verification-suite story whose canonical pair pinned two invariants on the same execution substrate, BE-0389's pair splits across two SUBSTRATES: TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles runs on the embed.FS alone (no Postgres dependency, runs on every developer machine and every CI runner deterministically); TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase requires a real Postgres (creates a throwaway database via crypto/rand-named DSN, runs the embedded ladder, asserts schema_migrations is clean, skips with t.Skip when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset). The deterministic member is the FRONT LINE — it trips on every machine on any closed-set coverage regression (new NNNN_*.up.sql file the loader misses, numbered file renamed to non-numbered form, version gap). The Postgres member is the DEPTH gate — it asserts the runtime contract that no migration can fail on an empty DB. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose contract spans deterministic-fixture-only AND requires-infrastructure invariants: BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety — deterministic Reversible check + Postgres Down test), BE-0392 (load smoke — deterministic harness contract + Postgres load run), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — deterministic injection-point coverage + Postgres reconnect test). The Postgres member MUST use t.Skip on missing env var so the suite stays green on machines without Postgres — never t.Fatal, never silently pass. (2) `CLOSED-SET COVERAGE WALKS embed.FS, NOT RUNTIME.CALLER` — unlike BE-0388 (which read its own source via runtime.Caller to enumerate FuzzXxx targets), BE-0389's closed-set coverage walks the embedded migrations directory via fs.ReadDir(embeddedMigrations, migrationsDir) and parses each filename against a regex that MIRRORS the loader's grammar (`^(\d{4,})_([a-z0-9_]+)\.(up|down)\.sql$`). The matcher then cross-references against LoadMigrations(embeddedMigrations) to assert closed-set parity (same count, same versions in order, same names, non-empty bodies, 64-char checksums). The duplication of the filename grammar is INTENTIONAL — a future grammar change in the loader (e.g. permissive `.sql` form) MUST update both the loader AND the test in the same edit. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set surface is a directory tree (BE-0390 down-script coverage, BE-0398 tenant-isolation test coverage per repository method, BE-0407 desired-state goldens). (3) `SUBSTRING SET INCLUDES BOTH "schema_migrations" AND "NNNN_"` — the matcher binds to TWO load-bearing literals the migrations-from-empty-DB contract pivots on: `schema_migrations` (the ledger table — every applied migration MUST surface as one clean row) and `NNNN_` (the canonical filename grammar — closed-set coverage pivots on the four-digit-zero-padded version prefix). The substring set also includes `YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL` (the deterministic-skip env var) AND `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` (the shared opt-in-external escape hatch) so the matcher pins BOTH the per-suite skip contract and the cross-suite external contract. Substring count = 17 (matches BE-0388's count). The full FuzzXxx-style explicit-listing rule from BE-0388 carries over: when the matcher's substring set names the canonical function pair, the SECURITY.md section MUST quote each full function name verbatim — TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles AND TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase — so a reader sees the gate from the doc alone. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — NINE INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#15)` — inserting required step #15 between #14 (fuzz validator tests) and the optionals renumbered 15→16/16→17/17→18/18→19 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was EIGHT consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0388). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0388's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list canonical function pair verbatim), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: when the PRD's `-run` filter is NOT already in verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (BE-0389's `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...` was not), the implementation MUST add it via a Node fs round-trip (JSON.parse + JSON.stringify) to preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; direct text Edit can drift the formatting. Forward-applicable to BE-0390..BE-0399 sibling stories.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, fuzz-validators instance (BE-0388 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_fuzz_validators_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/validate/fuzz_test.go's new canonical pair TestFuzzValidatorContractCoversExpectedValidators + TestFuzzValidatorContractSeedCorpusRejectsHostileInputs + SECURITY.md "## Fuzz Validator Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: fuzz tests for validators (BE-0388)" subsection; TENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0387 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestFuzzValidator ./...`, CI step name `Fuzz validator tests`, verify.sh prefix #14). Four BE-0388-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR WRAPS FuzzXxx TARGETS, NOT INDIVIDUAL VALIDATORS` — unlike BE-0386 (one fake, dual invariants) and BE-0387 (one redactor, dual invariants), BE-0388's load-bearing surface is a SET of nine FuzzXxx targets (FuzzName, FuzzPath, FuzzDomain, FuzzEnvVarName, FuzzEnvVarValue, FuzzDecodeJSON, FuzzImageRef, FuzzURL, FuzzGitBranch). The canonical-pair design splits the set into two orthogonal invariants: TestFuzzValidatorContractCoversExpectedValidators pins the CLOSED-SET COVERAGE — every public validator MUST have a FuzzXxx target, asserted by reading fuzz_test.go's own source via runtime.Caller and matching against `fuzzValidatorExpectedTargets` (the nine literal `func FuzzXxx(f *testing.F)` declarations). A new validator added to the public surface without a Fuzz target trips this matcher with the exact missing name. TestFuzzValidatorContractSeedCorpusRejectsHostileInputs pins the RUNTIME NO-PANIC invariant — the wrapper drives every validator through the shared `fuzzSeeds` corpus under a `recover()` guard so a panic surfaces with the offending validator name AND the seed index (the actionable-failure contract). Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing surface is a SET of targets sharing one invariant + a closed-set coverage rule: BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — set of disconnect injection points), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — set of idempotency-protected endpoints), BE-0398 (tenant isolation — set of customer-data queries). (2) `Fuzz-PREFIX TARGETS RUN UNDER -run TestFuzzValidator AND -fuzz=FuzzXxx` — Go's fuzz tests (signature `func FuzzXxx(f *testing.F)`) run their seed corpus deterministically when invoked with `go test -run FuzzXxx` AND drive the randomised engine when invoked with `go test -fuzz=FuzzXxx`. The BE-0388 design KEEPS the existing FuzzXxx targets unchanged (they continue to support the optional `-fuzz=` driver for soak runs) AND adds a TestFuzzValidator-prefixed wrapper pair that binds to the `-run TestFuzzValidator ./...` filter on every `go test ./...` invocation. The wrapper does NOT replace the FuzzXxx targets — it complements them by adding a deterministic, name-anchored Test* binding to the canonical filter. The randomised driver remains an opt-in escape hatch documented in SECURITY.md (`-fuzz=Fuzz<Name>`); CI runs the wrapper, soak runs drive `-fuzz=`. (3) `SUBSTRING SET ANCHORS THE STRING "no panic" VERBATIM, NOT HYPHENATED FORMS` — first CI run failed because the SECURITY.md text used "no-panic" (hyphenated, sentence-internal) while the matcher pinned "no panic" (space-separated, quoted). The fix wrapped the canonical phrase in quotes in the SECURITY section ("the wrapper pins the 'no panic' invariant") so both the matcher and the human-readable doc land on the same literal. Forward-applicable pattern: when pinning a multi-word invariant as a closed substring, prefer quoting the invariant in the doc so the matcher binds to the literal a reviewer is most likely to write. Also: when the matcher's substring set contains both bare ("FuzzName") AND prefix-form ("FuzzDecodeJSON") names, the section MUST list the full FuzzXxx target set explicitly — handing the reader a sample like "(`Name`, `Path`, ...)" doesn't satisfy a matcher that pins on the literal "FuzzName" substring. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — EIGHT INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#14)` — inserting required step #14 between #13 (redaction tests) and the optionals renumbered 14→15/15→16/16→17/17→18 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was SEVEN consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0387). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0387's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list FULL target-name set explicitly when pinning a multi-target surface), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, fake-Dokploy-contract instance (BE-0386 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_fake_dokploy_contract_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake/dokployfake_test.go's new canonical pair TestFakeDokployContractDeterministicHierarchyIDs + TestFakeDokployContractRecordedRequestsRedactCredentials + SECURITY.md "## Fake Dokploy Contract Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: fake Dokploy contract tests (BE-0386)" subsection; EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383/BE-0384/BE-0385 verification-suite mega-pattern, confirms the BE-0385 closing-line prediction held verbatim — canonical command `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`, CI step name `Fake Dokploy contract tests`, verify.sh prefix #12). Four BE-0386-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL FILE = CONTRACT-OWNING PACKAGE, NOT A CONSUMER` — unlike BE-0385 which pinned `internal/controlplane/worker/queue_test.go` (the worker's lease test) and BE-0384 which pinned the quota test in the quota package, BE-0386's canonical file is `internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake/dokployfake_test.go` — the FAKE's own contract tests, not a downstream consumer's tests against the fake. The rationale: a fake-X contract-test story should bind to the package whose contract is being asserted (the fake itself), not to a caller that happens to use the fake. The worker test in `internal/controlplane/worker/dokployfake_test.go` (TestWorkerRunnerProvisionsThroughFakeDokploy, TestWorkerRunnerSurfacesFakeDokployFaults) is downstream evidence, NOT the canonical contract. Forward-applicable: any future verification story whose subject is a deterministic test fixture (BE-0392 load smoke harness, BE-0393 chaos test harness, BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal) should pin the fixture's own contract tests, not a consumer's. (2) `-RUN FILTER PRE-EXISTING IN PRD BUT MATCHED ZERO TESTS` — sibling of BE-0382's `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` but the OPPOSITE shape: the PRD's `verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands` already contained `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...` before BE-0386 landed, BUT the `TestFakeDokploy` prefix matched ZERO existing tests anywhere in the repo (the dokployfake package's tests are TestCreateHierarchy…, TestTwoServers…, TestRequestRecordingRedactsCredentials, etc. — none start with TestFakeDokploy). The named CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING gates would have silently passed against zero tests. BE-0386 adds value by (a) CREATING the canonical TestFakeDokploy* pair so the `-run` filter binds to real assertions, (b) wiring CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY/AGENTS surfaces as named gates, (c) adding the static defence that pins both halves. Forward-applicable: when picking up ANY verification-suite story whose PRD `-run` filter exists, FIRST run `go test -run <Prefix> ./...` — if it shows `no tests to run` everywhere, the story MUST add canonical tests under the prefix BEFORE wiring the gates, otherwise the gates pass vacuously. (3) `CANONICAL-PAIR DUAL-INVARIANT NAMING — DETERMINISM × RECORDER-REDACTION` — TestFakeDokployContractDeterministicHierarchyIDs pins the per-server deterministic ID allocator (two independent dokployfake.New() servers driven through the same org/project/env/app/deployment chain mint byte-identical org_1/proj_1/env_1/app_1/dep_1, with per-server request counts isolated to 5/5); TestFakeDokployContractRecordedRequestsRedactCredentials pins the recorder hot path that swaps the Authorization header for output.Sentinel AND scans the body for the bearer literal (3 recorded requests across happy-path create / GET read-back / failure-path POST whose body deliberately echoes the bearer — each MUST have AuthHeader==Sentinel, Headers[Authorization]==Sentinel, no body retains the bearer literal). The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `dokployfake.New` (constructor — deletion breaks every worker test), `Server.Requests` (recorder seam), `output.Sentinel` (the redaction substitute — a different sentinel would silently fork the redaction contract), AND `TestLiveDokploySmoke` (the AC2 opt-in-external escape hatch's test name) so auditors verify the contract from documentation alone. Substring count raised to 15 (vs BE-0385's 13). (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — SEVEN INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#12)` — inserting required step #12 between #11 (job worker lease) and the optionals renumbered 12→13/13→14/14→15/15→16 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). Now SEVEN consecutive required-step insertions with optional renumbering; the next contributor adding required step #13 (BE-0387 redaction tests, canonical `go test -run TestRedaction ./...` if PRD names that filter) should follow the same edit shape AND prepare for an optional-band renumber. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0385's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-15-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers (which seam each canonical-pair function pins). Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, job-worker-lease instance (BE-0385 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_job_worker_lease_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/worker/queue_test.go's new canonical pair TestJobWorkerLeaseExclusivityNeverDoubleClaims + TestJobWorkerLeaseReleasedOnShutdownIsRetryable + SECURITY.md "## Job Worker Lease Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: job worker lease tests (BE-0385)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383/BE-0384 verification-suite mega-pattern). Three BE-0385-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER NEWLY ADDED TO PRD` — unlike BE-0383/BE-0384 where verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands already carried the `-run` filter, BE-0385's `go test -run TestJobWorkerLease ./...` was NOT present in the array before the story landed; the implementation adds it via a small JSON edit (preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; use Node fs to round-trip parse/serialize so existing key order stays stable). (2) `CANONICAL-PAIR DUAL-INVARIANT NAMING — EXCLUSIVITY × SHUTDOWN-SAFETY` — the canonical-funcs pair encodes the TWO load-bearing engine invariants: TestJobWorkerLeaseExclusivityNeverDoubleClaims pins the SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED claim in StoreClaimer.Claim (two concurrent goroutines race the same one-job queue; exactly one returns a non-nil Lease and the other returns (nil, nil); the surviving lease runs the job to success and the row carries no stale lease afterward); TestJobWorkerLeaseReleasedOnShutdownIsRetryable pins the Loop.Run shutdown path that invokes Lease.Release against a context detached from the cancelled loop context (a Loop.Run past a runner that blocks until cancellation, cancel the loop while the lease is in-flight, assert the persisted job is back to retrying with no stale lease owner and next_run_at claimable immediately). The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (the claim) AND `Lease.Release` (the shutdown seam) so an auditor verifies both invariants from the documented contract alone. Forward-applicable to ANY future worker-verification story whose invariant has a "hot path concurrency × shutdown safety" dual: BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract tests can mirror this exclusivity × fault-injection pattern). (3) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK CONTINUES` — inserting required step #11 between #10 (quota concurrency) and the optionals renumbered 11→12/12→13/13→14/14→15 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The static test pins `# 11. Required: job worker lease tests` but NOT the optional headers — those shift on every required insertion. Established by BE-0380's renumber pattern; this is now SIX consecutive required-step insertions (#6→#11) with optional renumbering, suggesting the next contributor adding a required step #12 (BE-0386 fake Dokploy) should follow the same edit shape: insert the new `# 12. Required` block AND renumber the four optional headers in the same Edit call. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0385 uses `go test -run TestJobWorkerLease ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0386 will use `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0385: "Job worker lease tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber trailing optionals in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (per-suite invariants; preserve the closed-set discipline so the matcher fails loudly), (e) AGENTS.md subsection (mirrors the SECURITY.md section but speaks to maintainers; explains the canonical-funcs pair invariants and which seam each pins).
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, quota-concurrency instance (BE-0384 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_quota_concurrency_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/quota/quota_test.go's new canonical pair TestQuotaConcurrencyHardLimitNeverOverallocates + TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation + SECURITY.md "## Quota Concurrency Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: quota concurrency tests (BE-0384)" subsection; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383 verification-suite mega-pattern). Three BE-0384-specific extensions to the template: (1) `RUN-FILTER PRE-EXISTING IN PRD` — the PRD's `verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands` already contained `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...` before the story landed (same as BE-0383's policy-matrix entry; established by BE-0382's `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` sub-pattern). BE-0384 adds value via (a) wiring CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY/AGENTS surfaces (the gate didn't exist as a named gate before), (b) ADDING canonical TestQuotaConcurrency* tests (the existing TestCheckerReserveConcurrentRespectsLimit / TestCheckerReserveTenantIsolation siblings stay; the new pair encodes the FOR UPDATE-lock and organization_id-predicate invariants under the load-bearing prefix), (c) pinning the existence of both via the static test. (2) `CANONICAL-PAIR INVARIANT-NAMING` — the canonical-funcs pair encodes the TWO load-bearing engine invariants: TestQuotaConcurrencyHardLimitNeverOverallocates pins the FOR UPDATE lock on the per-organization-per-resource usage counter row (parallel writers against ONE tenant — exactly limit succeed, the rest get yerr.CodeQuotaExceeded, the database row count agrees); TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation pins the organization_id predicate carried through every counter/sum/insert (parallel writers across TWO tenants — each respects its own limit AND the two tenants' active reservations sum to the two limits, never collapse into one bucket). The cross-tenant sum-check is the load-bearing defence-in-depth: per-tenant counts could pass while a regression silently dropped one tenant's rows entirely. The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `FOR UPDATE` (the lock) and `organization_id` (the predicate) so an auditor verifies the invariant from the documented contract alone, without opening the test file. Forward-applicable to ANY future concurrency-verification story whose invariant has a "single-tenant hot path × cross-tenant isolation path" dual: BE-0386 (fake Dokploy), BE-0398 (tenant isolation), BE-0407 (service desired-state goldens). (3) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK CONTINUES` — inserting required step #10 between #9 (policy matrix) and the optionals renumbered 10→11/11→12/12→13/13→14 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The static test pins `# 10. Required: quota concurrency tests` but NOT the optional headers — those shift on every required insertion. Established by BE-0380's renumber pattern. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0384 uses `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0385 will use `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0384: "Quota concurrency tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber trailing optionals in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-entry substring set covering scope / determinism / actionable-failures (engine-specific tokens: FOR UPDATE, organization_id, CodeQuotaExceeded) / opt-in-external / envelope-pinning / canonical-pair / redaction / CI-cadence / self-locating gate filename, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry pre-existing OR newly inserted, (f) canonical reference test file path + canonical-pair func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: in TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation's per-org goroutine closure, do NOT alias `t` (the *testing.T) to a per-tenant tally bucket — `t := results[orgID]` SHADOWS the outer testing.T, silently breaking `t.Errorf` inside the closure. Used `bucket := results[orgID]` to keep the testing.T accessible for actionable diagnostics. Forward-applicable to any verification-suite story whose parallel-goroutine test groups results per-loop-var.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, policy-matrix instance (BE-0383 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_policy_matrix_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/policy_test.go's TestPolicyMatrix + the NEW sibling TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant + SECURITY.md "## Policy Matrix Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: policy matrix tests (BE-0383)" subsection; FIFTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern). Three BE-0383-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER WITHOUT PACKAGE NARROWING` — unlike BE-0381/BE-0382 which used `go test ./internal/controlplane/<pkg>/...` (package-narrowed) as the canonical command, BE-0383 uses the PRD's `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` form unchanged (filter-narrowed across the entire module). The CI step, verify.sh step, CONTRIBUTING entry, SECURITY row, and PRD entry ALL agree on the exact same `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` literal — there's NO package-narrowed variant. This keeps the gate stable across hypothetical future packages that may declare a TestPolicyMatrix… test (e.g. an httpapi-layer policy-matrix integration test). The static test's canonical-command constant is the single command echoed verbatim across all five external surfaces. (2) `CROSS-TENANT MATRIX TWIN` — the canonical-funcs list is a PAIR (TestPolicyMatrix + TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant), not a triple (BE-0382) or a single (hypothetical), because the policy engine has TWO load-bearing matrix invariants: in-tenant decisions (Decide's main path: ReasonAllowedSelf / ReasonAllowedByRole / ReasonDeniedNoCapability per `builtinRoleCaps[role].has(required)`) and cross-tenant decisions (Decide's cross-tenant clause: ReasonAllowedSelf for CapSelf, ReasonAllowedBySupport for support's CapSupport bridging CapRead/CapSupport only, ReasonDeniedCrossTenant for everything else). The two matrices are STRUCTURALLY IDENTICAL (same role × action cross-product, same engine, same fixtures) but differ in resource scope (resourceIn(orgA) vs resourceIn(orgB)) and in the expected-verdict switch. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant has a "happy path × foreign-scope path" dual: BE-0398 (tenant isolation), BE-0404 (break-glass), BE-0407 (service desired-state goldens). The cross-tenant variant's switch MUST encode the engine's exact cross-tenant clause — guessing it from intuition (e.g. assuming Support is always allowed cross-tenant) fails the matrix because the engine ALSO checks the required cap via `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)`. Verify by tracing engine.Decide BEFORE writing the test's expected-verdict switch. (3) `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` — unlike BE-0382 where the `-run TestOpenAPI` filter previously matched zero tests (the existing openapi tests were `TestBuild…` / `TestOperation…`), BE-0383's `-run TestPolicyMatrix` filter ALREADY matched the existing `TestPolicyMatrix` function in internal/controlplane/policy/policy_test.go. The story still adds value by (a) wiring the dedicated CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY surfaces (the gate did not exist as a named gate before), (b) enriching the canonical test set with the cross-tenant matrix twin so the canonical-funcs pair is load-bearing, and (c) pinning the existence of both via the static test. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0383 uses `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0384 will use `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0383: "Policy matrix tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (BE-0383 inserted `# 9. Required: policy matrix tests` between BE-0382's `# 8` and the previous `# 9. Optional: govulncheck`, renumbering optionals 9→10/10→11/11→12/12→13 in the same edit — established by BE-0380's renumber pattern), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-entry substring set covering scope (internal/controlplane/policy) / determinism (deterministic fixtures) / actionable-failures (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) / exceptions (CapSelf, CapSupport) / opt-in-external (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY) / envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1) / matrix-pair (TestPolicyMatrix, TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant) / redaction (redacted) / CI-cadence (Every push and PR) / self-locating gate filename (verification_suite_policy_matrix_static_test.go), (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry already present unchanged (BE-0383 did NOT add — the existing entry satisfies the matcher), (f) canonical reference test file path + matrix-pair func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, OpenAPI-conformance instance (BE-0382 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_openapi_conformance_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/openapi/openapi_conformance_test.go + SECURITY.md "## OpenAPI Schema Conformance Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: OpenAPI schema conformance tests (BE-0382)" subsection; FOURTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern). Two BE-0382-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER PREFIX CONTRACT` — when the PRD already carries a `-run TestXxx ./...` filter for the suite (BE-0382 inherited `-run TestOpenAPI ./...` from the PRD's verificationLoop array unchanged; that filter previously matched zero tests because the existing openapi tests are `TestBuild…` / `TestOperation…` not `TestOpenAPI…`), the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy the `-run` filter prefix or the named gate silently matches zero tests. The static test pins this with a dedicated `<subject>PrefixLocksTheRunFilterContract` analyzer subtest that walks the canonical-funcs list and asserts every entry begins with `func TestOpenAPI` (or whatever the suite's filter prefix is). Forward-applicable to BE-0383 (`-run TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (`-run TestQuotaConcurrency`), and BE-0386 (`-run TestFakeDokploy`); NOT applicable to BE-0385 (no `-run` filter — package-level only). When picking up such a sibling story FIRST check whether the PRD's `-run` filter currently matches any test (`go test -run <PrefixPattern> ./...` shows `no tests to run` everywhere) — if so, the story MUST add tests that satisfy the prefix, not just wire the dedicated step. (2) `VALUE-TYPE STRUCT FIELD SHAPE` — when the canonical conformance test reads a struct field that's a value type (not a pointer), the test MUST NOT guard with `field == nil` (compile error against the value type); the empty-struct sentinel is `field.SubField == ""` instead. This is a per-package shape detail; the canonical fix is to read the type once at write time, not to introduce a pointer indirection or a package-internal nil sentinel that does not exist today. Worked instance: openapi.Parameter.Schema is a `Schema` value not `*Schema`, so the conformance test checks `param.Schema.Type != "string"` directly. The six surfaces are: (a) .github/workflows/ci.yml dedicated `OpenAPI schema conformance tests` step running `go test ./internal/controlplane/openapi/...`, (b) scripts/verify.sh `# 8. Required: OpenAPI schema conformance tests` header + literal command (BE-0381's prior steps 8/9/10/11 — govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser — renumbered to 9/10/11/12 in the same edit), (c) CONTRIBUTING.md `8. \`go test ./internal/controlplane/openapi/...\`` entry under `## Required Checks Before Every Commit`, (d) SECURITY.md verification-gates table row + dedicated `## OpenAPI Schema Conformance Tests` section with a 13-entry closed substring set covering scope (internal/controlplane/openapi) / determinism (deterministic fixtures) / actionable-failures (operationId) / authorization (x-required-action) / opt-in-external (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY) / envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1) / conformance-triple (TestOpenAPIConformance, TestOpenAPIEnvelopesReferenceStableSchemaVersions, TestOpenAPIExamplesAreRedacted) / redaction / CI-cadence (Every push and PR) / self-locating gate filename, (e) ralph/prd.json verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands entry inserted immediately after the httpapi entry (so the two adjacent commands are the two package-level subsets that follow the umbrella `./internal/controlplane/...` walker), (f) canonical reference conformance test file `internal/controlplane/openapi/openapi_conformance_test.go` + load-bearing conformance triple covering (i) end-to-end document conformance (3.1.0 identity, both response rows per operation, public empty security, authenticated ApiKeyAuth + x-required-action, required-string-in-path parameters), (ii) envelope schema_version enum pinning to `output.SuccessSchema` / `yerr.SchemaVersion`, (iii) example redaction via `output.Sentinel` with negative assertion against the credential-shaped `yalla_sk_<env>_<value>` pattern; the self-check `TestVerificationSuiteOpenAPIConformanceAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` drives every matcher with 11 synthetic known-good AND known-bad fixtures. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber the trailing optional steps in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 11-13 substring set covering every AC, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry (insert near siblings in the array; keep the umbrella entry FIRST), (f) canonical reference test file path + closed conformance-triple func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, repository-integration-tests instance (BE-0380 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_repo_integration_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Repository Integration Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: repository integration tests (BE-0380)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379 verification-suite single-file template — applies to verification stories whose subject is a SUBSET of `go test ./...` (a specific package path, a specific `-run` filter) rather than the umbrella suite). EIGHT load-bearing rules: (1) SUBSET-OF-UMBRELLA REDUNDANCY IS DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH, NOT CHURN — when the canonical command (`go test ./internal/controlplane/store/...`) is a strict subset of an already-gated umbrella command (`go test ./...`), the new CI step / verify.sh step / CONTRIBUTING entry are NOT redundant: a future narrowing of the umbrella (e.g. `go test ./internal/...` minus store, a "skip slow tests" flag) would silently de-gate the subject while leaving the umbrella green; the dedicated step still fires AND surfaces the regression as a fast, named, targeted failure rather than buried inside the umbrella log. Cost: one extra ~30s parallel CI step. (2) VERIFY.SH RENUMBERING SAFETY — the verify.sh numeric prefixes 6-9 (optional steps after BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` and the bare `# 5. Required: race detector`) were not pinned by any existing test; inserting a new `# 6. Required: <subject>` between current 5 and current 6 and renumbering optionals 6→7, 7→8, 8→9, 9→10 was a safe edit. BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` pin is preserved verbatim. Future verification-suite subset stories adding a required step should follow the same pattern OR agree on a fixed prefix range like `# 6.X.` to avoid further renumbering as more stories land. (3) PRD HAS TWO COMMAND ARRAYS — verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands (umbrella `go test ./...`, BE-0379) and verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (per-package paths and per-`-run` filters, BE-0380 and later). Subset stories add to requiredBackendCommands; the JSON decode in the test MUST target that field. The two arrays must NEVER be confused — a regression where BE-0380's command lands in requiredLocalCommands instead of requiredBackendCommands would silently weaken the contract because the matcher decodes the wrong field. (4) PREFIX-TOLERANT FUNCTION MATCHER — the `func TestMigrations` matcher is intentionally prefix-tolerant (matches `func TestMigrationsForward` too) because the PRD's `-run TestMigrations` filter is itself a prefix match. Document the gap in a self-check subtest with a stricter fixture (one with NO `func TestMigrations*` declaration at all) so a future maintainer who wants exact-name pinning knows to switch to a regex anchored on `\(`. (5) ELEVEN-SUBSTRING SECURITY SECTION — the section MUST name every load-bearing AC fact: package boundary (`internal/controlplane/store`), determinism (`deterministic fixtures`), isolation (`isolated migrated`), bootstrap function (`TestMigrations`), actionable-failure tokens (BOTH `request_id` AND `resource_id` because HTTP-facing tests use the former and row-level tests use the latter — separating them keeps both contracts visible), opt-in escape hatch (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`), deterministic fixture (`fake Dokploy`), redaction (`redacted`), CI cadence (`Every push and PR`), self-locating gate file name. Each substring is distinct and semantically anchored so Markdown line wrap cannot break the matcher. (6) SELF-CHECK PARITY WITH BE-0379 — each per-surface matcher gets BOTH a "fires on missing" AND "passes on complete" synthetic subtest. The CI workflow self-check uses a fixture containing the umbrella step but NOT the new dedicated step, asserting both halves of the matcher fail; the complete fixture adds the dedicated step and asserts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, HTTP-handler-contract-tests instance (BE-0381 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_handler_contract_tests_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## HTTP Handler Contract Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: HTTP handler contract tests (BE-0381)" subsection; THIRD worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern: the static-test file pins SIX surfaces in one place — (1) .github/workflows/ci.yml dedicated `HTTP handler contract tests` step running `go test ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`, (2) scripts/verify.sh `# 7. Required: HTTP handler contract tests` header + literal command, (3) CONTRIBUTING.md `7. \`go test ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...\`` entry under `## Required Checks Before Every Commit`, (4) SECURITY.md verification-gates table row + dedicated `## HTTP Handler Contract Tests` section with a closed substring set covering scope/determinism/actionable-failures/contract-triple/envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1)/redaction/CI-cadence, (5) ralph/prd.json verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands entry, (6) the canonical reference contract test file `internal/controlplane/httpapi/me_contract_test.go` AND its load-bearing contract triple (TestMeServerWritesResponseDataOnlyToResponseWriter, TestMeRequestLogRedactsBearerToken, TestMeErrorEnvelopeDoesNotLeakDependencyCause) so deleting the canonical example or renaming any of the three silently breaks the convention every other *_contract_test.go follows; the self-check `TestVerificationSuiteHandlerContractAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` drives every matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad fixtures so over-tightening and under-tightening are both caught at the package-internal API; REUSES the shared constants requiredCIWorkflowPath, requiredVerifyShPath, requiredContributingPath, requiredContributingSection, requiredSecurityPath, requiredPRDPath defined in verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go; when inserting a new numbered verify.sh step, ALSO renumber subsequent optional steps in the same edit because the numeric prefix is part of the contract shared between verify.sh and the static-test constants — skipping the renumber breaks the BE-0380 contract-prefix matcher).
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, unit-tests-for-every-package instance (BE-0379 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go + internal/credentials/keyring_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Unit Test Suite" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: unit tests for every package (BE-0379)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the verification-suite single-file template called out in the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose subject is the suite itself rather than a specific security control). NINE load-bearing rules: (1) SINGLE FILE pins ALL surfaces of the meta-contract so a future contributor changing the CI step name, the verify.sh step header, the CONTRIBUTING numeric prefix, the SECURITY row, or the PRD verification-loop array fails ONE test, not five. The single-file shape is the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern carried forward to the verification-suite family. (2) CLOSED EXEMPT SET WITH RATIONALE — every exemption in allowedPackagesWithoutUnitTests MUST carry a rationale that points to where coverage actually lives, not just a "thin entrypoint" hand-wave. The rationale is what makes a future review decide whether to keep, extend, or remove the entry; without it the set drifts. Current exempt set: cmd/yalla (Cobra entrypoint, behaviour in internal/cli), cmd/yalla-api (process wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/*), cmd/yalla-worker (loop wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/worker), internal/controlplane/jobs (doc.go placeholder). (3) STALE-EXEMPTION GUARD (TestVerificationSuiteExemptionSetIsTight) — assert every exempt dir (a) exists and (b) still lacks tests. Otherwise a package that gains tests silently keeps its exemption and the discipline rots. (4) IGNORED-PREFIX WALKER — the package coverage walker MUST skip `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `outputs/`, `tmp/`, `bin/`, `vendor/` via prefix-with-trailing-slash check (the trailing slash is what prevents a hypothetical `output/` package from being accidentally skipped). (5) PURE FUNCTION SPLIT — findPackagesMissingTests takes (root, exempt, ignored) and is called once by the runtime test against the real repo AND multiple times by the self-check with synthetic trees built via t.TempDir + mkFile helper. The self-check is what proves the walker doesn't false-positive on test-only dirs and doesn't false-negative on ignored subtrees. (6) CASE-SENSITIVE SUBSTRING CONSTANTS — `requiredSecuritySectionSubstrings` are matched verbatim against the doc, so a substring like "every Go package" will NOT match a sentence-start "Every Go package". When the doc text naturally starts a sentence with the substring, use the capitalised form in the constant. The first run caught this: the test failed with `missing required substring "every Go package"`; the fix aligned the constant with the doc, not the doc with the constant. The self-check fixture (synthetic SECURITY.md inside TestVerificationSuiteUnitTestsAnalyzerDetectsRegressions) must be updated in the same edit — if the synthetic fixture starts to fail, the test diagnostic explicitly says "fix the fixture, not the test" to prevent the wrong direction of fix. (7) NON-MAIN, NON-DOC-ONLY PACKAGE WITHOUT TESTS IS NEVER EXEMPT-WORTHY — prefer adding 2-3 small tests (e.g. internal/credentials IsNotFound + IsUnsupported + Store-interface compile-time pin) over expanding the exemption set. The exemption set is reserved for thin main-glue and `doc.go`-only placeholders. Adding the keyring_test.go tests took ~70 lines and was strictly preferable to a `internal/credentials` exemption entry, because the package has real classification logic (errors.Is over upstream keyring sentinels) that a future upstream rename could silently break. (8) PRD AS A VERIFICATION SURFACE — the prd.json's `verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands` array is itself part of the contract. An AI agent reads the PRD before picking up a story; pinning the suite command there guarantees the agent sees it without needing to discover CI workflows or shell scripts. The matcher uses encoding/json with a partial struct (`{VerificationLoop struct{RequiredLocalCommands []string `json:"requiredLocalCommands"`}}`) so a future PRD schema extension doesn't break it. (9) CI MATRIX AS PART OF THE CONTRACT — pinning ubuntu-latest + macos-latest + windows-latest is what prevents a silent "we only test on Linux now" regression. The matrix dimension is a separate substring assertion per host so the failure diagnostic names the missing host explicitly. Forward-applicable to BE-0380 (repository integration tests — replace findPackagesMissingTests with findStoreTestsMissingTenantIsolation), BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests — replace with findHandlersMissingContractTest), BE-0382 (OpenAPI conformance — replace with findEndpointsMissingOpenAPI), BE-0383 (policy matrix), BE-0384 (quota concurrency), BE-0385 (job worker leases), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade), BE-0391 (race detector), and every future P=1 verification-suite story. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical suite command (e.g. `go test -run TestMigrations ./...`), (b) scope predicate (which packages/files the matcher walks), (c) doc heading constant, (d) substring-set capturing the ACs of that story, (e) per-story exemption set if any. The pattern is the next-strongest form of the BE-0354 (dependency_scan) single-file multi-surface pattern, applied to a meta-contract whose subject is the test suite itself rather than a specific security control. The two pattern families share: (i) single file, (ii) closed substring/constant set per surface, (iii) self-check with synthetic fixtures driving every matcher. They differ in: (i) BE-0379-family stories have a `findPackagesMissingTests`-style repo walker as their primary matcher (the security-control family has no such walker; their primary matcher is an AST scan over package source); (ii) BE-0379-family stories pin the PRD itself (security-control stories generally don't); (iii) BE-0379-family stories use a closed exempt set with per-entry rationale (security-control stories use closed constant lists like `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments` but no per-entry rationale).
- Security-verification cross-package secret-confinement pattern, Dokploy-token instance (BE-0361 pattern, internal/release/dokploy_token_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/config/dokploy_token_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Dokploy Token Isolation" section + internal/controlplane/dokploy/AGENTS.md "Token isolation (BE-0361)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST cross-package instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346) and "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF) to "this scarce in-process secret MUST flow from source-of-truth package A through redaction package B into wire-emission package C with NO leak surface in customer-facing package D". Six load-bearing rules: (1) TRAILING-SELECTOR-NAME matching — when the field name is unambiguously the secret in the codebase ("DokployToken" is unique to the Config secret field, no other struct has a field of the same name), match by `SelectorExpr.Sel.Name == "DokployToken"` regardless of the receiver. This catches `config.DokployToken`, `cfg.DokployToken`, `c.DokployToken`, and any future-receiver shape alike, in ONE matcher rule, where the package-qualified-only variant would miss the `c.DokployToken` form a method using a `*config.Config` receiver would naturally write. The receiver-name freedom is the load-bearing rule: any closed list of qualifiers (`config.`, `cfg.`, …) is brittle to local-naming choices in a future refactor. (2) RETURN-FALSE-FROM-INSPECT to suppress double-counting when a matcher fires on a parent node whose children would ALSO match — `config.EnvDokployToken` is both a SelectorExpr (X=`config`, Sel=`EnvDokployToken`) and contains an inner `EnvDokployToken` Ident that the bare-Ident matcher would also flag. Returning `false` from the `ast.Inspect` callback after emitting the SelectorExpr diagnostic stops descent and keeps the count at 1. The self-check fixture `handler that reads config.EnvDokployToken is rejected` pins this — without the `return false` it would fail with `got 2, want 1`. (3) COUNT-AND-PIN-LOCATION instead of count-only — for invariants where the secret MUST appear at exactly one structural location (here, `c.token` in `client.go`'s Authorization header), the matcher returns the full set of `file:line:col` triples across every production file in the package, the test asserts `len == 1` AND the single hit's path-prefix equals the canonical file. A count-only assertion would let a future refactor move the line to a different file silently; a path-only assertion would let a duplicate slip in. The two assertions in series are strictly stronger than either alone. The constructor's `token: token` binding (a `KeyValueExpr.Key`, not a `SelectorExpr`) is NOT counted by construction — `ast.Inspect` walks the Key as an `Ident`, not a `SelectorExpr`, so the matcher's `SelectorExpr` pivot skips it for free. The self-check fixture `constructor binding token: token is NOT counted` pins this distinction. (4) COMPOSITE-LITERAL-FIELD-VALUE inspection for redaction-seam invariants — `(*Config).Redacted()` returns a `RedactedConfig{...}` composite literal whose `DokployToken` field value MUST be a CallExpr to the local `redact(...)` closure. The matcher uses `findMethodOnReceiver(file, "Config", "Redacted")` (a small helper that walks `file.Decls` filtering `FuncDecl.Recv.List[0].Type` against both `*ast.Ident` and `*ast.StarExpr` receivers), then walks the method body looking for ANY composite literal containing a `DokployToken` KeyValueExpr, then asserts the value is a CallExpr with `Fun.Name == "redact"`. Three orthogonal regressions fire one diagnostic each: missing method, missing field in the literal, bare-selector value instead of a call. The same pattern applies to ANY future "method M MUST stamp field F through helper H before returning" structural rule. (5) USES-FIRST gate for defence-in-depth log surfaces — `(*Config).LogValue()` MUST call `c.Redacted()` first AND MUST NOT touch `c.DokployToken` directly. The matcher walks the method body, tracking `seenRedactedCall` (set when a `c.Redacted()` CallExpr is found) and emitting a diagnostic for EVERY `c.DokployToken` SelectorExpr seen. The two diagnostics are independent — a method that called `c.Redacted()` but ALSO read `c.DokployToken` directly would fire one; a method that omitted the call but had no direct read would fire the other; a method that did both would fire both. Forward-applicable to any "MUST consult helper H before reading secret S" pattern (variables LogValue, future credentials redactor, etc.). (6) MARKER-PATTERN runtime gate at the projection seams — three table-driven tests in `dokploy_token_isolation_test.go` install a fixed `BE0361DOKPLOYTOKENMARKERXYZ` prefix on the Config.DokployToken field and assert the marker never appears in (a) the `json.Marshal(c.Redacted())` byte stream, (b) the `slog.JSONHandler` output under `slog.Any("config", c)`, and (c) the `c.String()` debug projection — for EACH of three fixtures: short-token (canonical), long-token (longer than the redactor's sentinel, longer than any reasonable line-buffered slog chunk so a partial-flush leak would still surface), and collision-prefix (token prefix collides with the projection's field name `dokploy_token`, so a regression that truncated at the field-name boundary would still trip the marker check). The marker is the leak detector — asserting on token bytes alone would false-positive whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the stable projection text. (7) GOLANGCI-LINT S1025 gotcha — staticcheck (under golangci-lint v2) flags `fmt.Sprintf("%s", x)` as redundant when `x` has a `String()` method, suggesting `x.String()` instead. For BE-0361 the intent of the runtime test was to PROVE that `fmt.Sprintf("%s", cfg)` doesn't leak, but since `fmt.Sprintf` with `%s` against a Stringer DELEGATES to `.String()`, calling `.String()` directly exercises the same code path and satisfies the linter. The test comment must explicitly document the equivalence so a future reader understands why the seemingly indirect "%s" check is actually identical to the direct Stringer invocation. The same gotcha applies to any future Stringer-surface security test.
- Security-verification package-confinement pattern, backup-encryption instance (BE-0362 pattern, internal/release/backup_encryption_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/backup/encryption_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Backup Encryption" section + SECURITY.md Required Verification Gates table row + internal/controlplane/backup/AGENTS.md "Backup encryption boundary (BE-0362)" subsection; EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST whole-package-confinement instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346), "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF), and "this cross-package secret MUST flow through one seam" (BE-0361 Dokploy token) to "this leaf package MUST NOT carry an entire data plane". Eight load-bearing rules: (1) IMPORT-WHITELIST-AS-FORBIDDEN-LIST — the backup package's only legitimate I/O is os.ReadFile on the operator-supplied status path, so the gate enumerates the symmetric/asymmetric cipher set (crypto/aes, crypto/cipher, crypto/des, crypto/rc4, crypto/rsa, crypto/ecdsa, crypto/ed25519, crypto/tls), the archive/compression set (archive/zip, archive/tar, compress/gzip, compress/zlib, compress/flate, compress/bzip2), the database driver set (database/sql, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool), and the process-boundary set (os/exec, net/http) as a closed forbidden map. The set is intentionally aggressive because the boundary contract is "no data plane", not "this specific library is bad"; a future contributor who needs one of these libraries must surface the design change in review, not slip it in. (2) INTERFACE-METHOD-COUNT-AND-NAMES — the read-only Reporter interface is pinned by name AND by its single method's name (Status). The matcher locates the InterfaceType named Reporter, rejects every field in iface.Methods.List whose len(Names)==0 (embedded interfaces would let any method through, e.g. embed io.Writer would silently expand the surface), and rejects every named method whose Name != "Status". A missing interface fires a separate "missing required interface" diagnostic — a future refactor that renamed Reporter to BackupSurface would silently disable the gate otherwise. (3) CALL-EXPR-SELECTOR write-seam scan — the package MUST NOT call package-qualified write/exec functions. The matcher walks every CallExpr, asserts call.Fun is a SelectorExpr whose X is an ast.Ident (package alias) and joins pkg.Name + "." + sel.Sel.Name to compare against forbiddenBackupWriteSeams (os.Create, os.CreateTemp, os.WriteFile, os.OpenFile, os.Mkdir, os.MkdirAll, os.Rename, os.Remove, os.RemoveAll, os.Symlink, os.Link, os.Truncate, os.Chmod, os.Chown, exec.Command, exec.CommandContext). os.ReadFile / os.Stat / os.Open are deliberately excluded — they are the legitimate read-only seams. (4) MARKER-PATTERN runtime evidence with FOUR encryption-shaped fixtures — a parameterised subtest installs the BE0362KMSMARKERXYZ prefix inside four payload shapes (kms-key-id with arn:aws:kms:, aes-wrap-blob with base64-shaped repeated bytes, restore-failed-message with multi-line bucket URL, pem-private-key with BEGIN/END markers) and asserts the marker never appears in (a) err.Error(), (b) every errors.Unwrap layer (TestFileReporterEncryptionMarkerSurvivesErrorWrapping walks the wrap chain — a regression that wrapped raw content inside a deeper %w would be caught), or (c) json.Marshal(Status) (defensive: the struct has no content field today, but a future Detail field would surface the operator's accidentally-seeded payload through every downstream projection). (5) FIXTURE-SHAPE diversity for redaction robustness — every fixture exercises a different class of regression a future "improvement" to the parse-error message might introduce; the marker is what makes the leak detector load-bearing across all four shapes, because asserting on the fixture's full content would false-positive whenever the content happens to share a substring with a stable error phrase (e.g. a fixture containing "is malformed" would trip the existing "backup: status file is malformed" message). (6) WRAP-ROBUST DOC SUBSTRINGS — when pinning Markdown content from a wrapped document (SECURITY.md and the runbook), choose substrings short enough to survive line wrapping. The first iteration used "Plaintext WAL must never" and "no read access" — both spanned line wraps in the source files ("Plaintext WAL must\n   never" and "**no** read access") and the test fired false positives. Fix: use "Plaintext WAL" and "read access" — same semantic, robust to Markdown wrap and to **bold** runs. The closed substring list still names every load-bearing fact (KMS-managed key for at-rest, read access for the no-access invariant, Plaintext WAL for the WAL ban, Auditability for restore audit events) without being brittle to formatting. (7) DUAL-DOC PIN — the gate pins TWO operator-facing documents: SECURITY.md (the public posture) AND docs/operations/backup-restore.md (the operator runbook). Each gets its own test function with its own heading constant and closed substring set. Pinning both prevents a future "tidy" pass from collapsing one without the other — the operator-facing runbook is part of the public contract because operators rely on it to discover the KMS rotation cadence, the no-read-access invariant, and the audit-event posture. (8) SELF-CHECK with KNOWN-GOOD AND KNOWN-BAD pairs for EVERY matcher — TestBackupEncryptionStaticAnalyzerDetectsRegressions feeds three pairs (imports, reporter-interface, write-seams) through every matcher. Each known-bad fixture must produce ≥N hits (N matches the synthetic regression count); each known-good fixture must produce zero false positives. The reporter-interface self-check has a THIRD synthetic — a renamed interface (Surface) — that proves the matcher fires a "missing required interface" diagnostic when Reporter is removed, not just when its method set widens. A self-check that only feeds known-bad through the matcher cannot catch over-tightening (a matcher that fires on legitimate code would still satisfy the bad-fixture assertions); a self-check that only feeds known-good cannot catch under-tightening (a matcher that fires on nothing would still satisfy the good-fixture assertions). Both pairs are load-bearing. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant is "this leaf package MUST NOT carry a data plane for cross-cutting concern X" — credential vaulting (the secrets package must not encrypt under a process-resident key), audit shipping (the audit package must not network out to a SIEM), rate-limit storage (the ratelimit package must not persist a token-bucket distributed store), backup restore orchestration (a future restore package's port shape), and KMS proxying (any future package that mediates KMS calls).

- Read-endpoint contract-test triple (BE-0095/BE-0101 pattern,
  internal/controlplane/httpapi/{limits_get_contract_test.go,
  usage_get_contract_test.go}; structural template for every
  P=2 "Add contract tests for GET .../{org_id}/X" story whose
  endpoint shape is "{org_id} path param + no body + no query +
  list response with the StoreUnavailable dependency-failure
  path"). Three named tests close the contract gaps the base
  *_test.go + *_policy_test.go pair leaves open: (1) "server
  writes response data only through ResponseWriter" — wraps the
  served request in captureProcessOutput, asserts os.Stdout
  AND os.Stderr are empty, AND that the structured logger sink
  is non-empty (a regression to no-logging would surface here),
  AND that the response body still carries the seeded payload
  (the data path must work even with stdout/stderr capture in
  effect). Not parallel — captureProcessOutput swaps the global
  os streams. (2) "request log redacts bearer token" — two
  parallel subtests (success + authorization-failure where the
  authorization-failure leg uses a Disabled-flagged principal
  AND a reader whose List path errors if invoked, so any logged
  reader-error cannot be the leak source); each asserts the
  bearer credential constant does not appear in the log buffer
  nor in the response body. Logger level = LevelDebug so a
  future debug-level log addition is also captured. (3) "error
  envelope does not leak dependency cause" — wraps a fixed
  cause string carrying a bare datastore address
  ("connection refused dialing 10.0.0.5:5432") in
  apierr.StoreUnavailable, asserts 503 / E_UNAVAILABLE / non-
  empty stable Message / cause string absent / host "10.0.0.5"
  absent from the body. Probe bytes are intentionally identical
  across sibling contract tests so the invariant is a single
  closed regression set, not a per-endpoint variant. The new
  helper this pattern requires is a "WithLogger" clone of the
  existing handler-builder that swaps the second-to-last
  NewHandler nil slot for the caller-supplied *slog.Logger —
  identical edit across every read endpoint, because the logger
  seam lives at the same position in NewHandler's parameter
  list regardless of which reader port the route depends on.
  Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0104 audit-events, BE-0107
  variables-list, and every future GET .../{org_id}/* read
  endpoint contract-test story; the only per-story knobs are
  the reader port type, the fake reader's field name, and the
  envelope decoder.
- Log-redaction fuzz harness, single-test runtime-only variant (BE-0359 pattern, internal/output/redact_fuzz_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Log Redaction Fuzzing" section + internal/output/AGENTS.md "Log redaction fuzzing (BE-0359)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the single-test runtime-only shape called out in the BE-0344 pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract with NO static analog, e.g. log redaction fuzzing, idempotency replay, chaos tests for timeouts). Six load-bearing rules: (1) FUZZ-MARKER design — when proving a structural redaction rule scrubs the value-half of a transport pattern, bracket the fuzz-supplied value with a unique fixed marker (`"FUZZAUTHMARKERXYZ"`, `"FUZZQRYMARKERZYX"`) and assert the marker never appears in the redacted output. The marker is the leak detector, independent of the value's bytes. Asserting that the value-bytes do not appear false-positives whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the test's static suffix (e.g. `"example"` if the test constructs `Host: example.com`). The marker pattern is reusable for ANY future fuzz target that proves "structural rule X scrubs the value half of transport Y" — cookies, refresh tokens, future header/query patterns. (2) REGEX-VALUE-CLASS gotcha — Go's `regexp` `\s` is exactly `[\t\n\f\r ]`; vertical-tab (`\v`) is NOT in `\s` but form-feed (`\f`) IS. When constructing a fuzz value that must traverse a regex value class without being cut short, strip every character in the regex's exclusion set including every member of Go's `\s`. The first revision missed `\f` and a seed containing it ended the regex mid-value, surviving the marker bracket and producing a false-positive leak. (3) DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH interactions — when two structural rules can both fire on a fuzz value (e.g. a query value containing `AUTHORIZATION:` triggers the bearer regex which eats the rest of the URL), assert ONLY the safety property (marker absent), NOT cosmetic properties (non-secret params survive). Document the interaction in the test file so a future reader understands why the survival assertion is absent. (4) SENTINEL-OVERLAP scope-out — `Redactor.Redact` uses `strings.ReplaceAll(s, secret, Sentinel)`; if `secret` is a substring of `Sentinel` (e.g. `"REDA"` inside `"[REDACTED]"`), the second pass rewrites the freshly-inserted sentinel substring and the function is NOT idempotent. Skip these inputs in fuzz AND pin the exclusion in a regression test (`TestRedactor_FuzzScopeExclusionsAreReal`) so a future redactor fix (single-pass regex substitution that does not re-scan substituted text) deliberately breaks that test and forces a coordinated update to the fuzz scope, the SECURITY.md section, and the AGENTS.md note. (5) SEED-CORPUS vs `-fuzz` — `go test ./...` runs every seed in `f.Add` once, so the seed corpus is the regression net every commit exercises; `-fuzz` is operator-opt-in. When adding a new redaction rule, the worked pattern is: append a seed input to `redactFuzzSeeds` that triggers the new rule, add a fuzz target if the rule has a distinct structural shape, and update both the AGENTS.md and SECURITY.md sections at the same time. (6) `-fuzztime` SHUTDOWN quirk — `go test -fuzz=… -fuzztime=5s` with the default 16 workers sometimes reports `context deadline exceeded` at shutdown; this is a harness-internal timing quirk, not a real fuzz failure (no `testdata/fuzz/…` corpus file is written). Re-running with `-parallel=2` makes the shutdown cleaner. Forward-applicable to any future security-verification story whose invariant has no AST shape to gate (BE-0360 environment-variable redaction if the rule is purely "the variables service MUST pass every rendered value through the redactor before logging" with no AST shape; BE-0363 rate-limit bypass resistance; chaos-tests for timeouts; idempotency replay).
- Admin endpoint isolation static-pin pattern (BE-0357, internal/release/admin_endpoint_isolation_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Admin Endpoint Isolation" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "Admin endpoint isolation (BE-0357)" subsection; SECOND instance of the BE-0356 Go-source-file variant of the BE-0355 single-test-file template applied to ENTIRE-DIRECTORY-OF-SOURCE-FILES rather than a single file — structural template for ANY future security-verification story whose invariant is 'no production source under directory D may import package P, name selector S, or carry literal L'). Eight load-bearing rules: (1) The test file uses `package release_test` (NOT `package release`) so the canonical `projectRoot(t)` helper from release_test.go links — same gotcha as BE-0356, every static-pin test in internal/release MUST follow this convention. (2) The directory scan is bounded by a SINGLE small helper (`adminProductionFiles(t, root) -> []string`) that returns ONLY non-`_test.go` `.go` files plus the entry binary, sorted for deterministic diagnostics; test files are deliberately skipped because a future runtime regression test needs to mention the threat without tripping the gate. (3) The import scan parses with `parser.SkipObjectResolution|parser.ImportsOnly` (fast; ignores function bodies), the literal/selector scan parses with `parser.SkipObjectResolution|parser.ParseComments`; mixing modes by matcher is the right call when the matcher's needs differ. (4) Closed sets of forbidden imports (`net/http/pprof`, `expvar`, `golang.org/x/net/trace`), forbidden selector roots (`pprof`, `expvar`), and forbidden literals (`/debug/pprof`, `/debug/vars`, `/debug/requests`, `/debug/events`, `http.DefaultServeMux`) at the top of the test file — every set is closed, every member is named in SECURITY.md, every set extension is a deliberate two-file edit. (5) The http.Server `Handler` field is pinned by PRESENCE (omission falls back to DefaultServeMux per the stdlib docs) AND an explicit `Handler: nil` is rejected with the same diagnostic — both shapes are functionally equivalent and must both fail the gate. (6) The private-mux contract on `internal/controlplane/httpapi/server.go` is TWO orthogonal passes: a presence assertion (`http.NewServeMux` call must appear) AND a forbidden-reference scan (`DefaultServeMux` selector must not appear). Both are required — a regression that builds a private mux AND ALSO hands routes to the default mux would slip past either pass in isolation. (7) SECURITY.md gets a dedicated section heading (`## Admin Endpoint Isolation`), a verification-gates table row keyed on `-run TestAdminEndpointIsolation`, and a closed list of canonical substrings that name every forbidden import path BY NAME so a reader does not need to open the test file to learn the contract. (8) The runtime evidence half is implicit (the existing `notFoundRecorder` returns the stable `yalla.error.v1` envelope for any `/debug/*` request) — same single-test-file shape as BE-0355 and BE-0356. Forward-applicable to: any future security-verification story whose invariant is 'no production source file under directory D may carry shape X', including future stories for forbidden environment-variable echoes, forbidden log-format strings, forbidden cipher-suite literals, or forbidden file-scheme URL literals.
- TLS and proxy header trust static-pin pattern (BE-0356, internal/release/http_server_hardening_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## TLS Termination and Proxy Header Trust" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "TLS and proxy header trust" subsection; FIRST instance of the BE-0355 single-test-file template applied to GO SOURCE FILES instead of declarative manifests; structural template for ANY future security-verification story whose invariant lives in (a) the construction shape of a typed struct literal in production Go code AND (b) the closed allow-list of string literals consulted in a designated function body). Six load-bearing rules: (1) The test file MUST use `package release_test` (NOT `package release`) so the canonical `projectRoot(t)` helper from release_test.go is in scope — the other static-pin tests in internal/release follow the same convention and a mismatched package gives an undefined-symbol build error at link time. (2) The struct-literal scan uses a package-qualified type selector helper (`isHTTPServerCompositeLit` matches a SelectorExpr with X=`http`, Sel=`Server`) so unrelated structs in the same source file are not scanned. Two closed sets ride on this matcher: `requiredHTTPServerFields = {ReadHeaderTimeout}` (every literal MUST include them, missing field = regression) and `forbiddenAPITLSStructFields = {TLSConfig, TLSNextProto}` (presence = regression). (3) The closed-set string-literal scan needs TWO ORTHOGONAL PASSES: a scoped pass on the named function body (`ClientIP` here — every `r.Header.Get(...)` literal must be in `allowedProxyHeaderLiterals` case-sensitively) AND a file-wide pass on every BasicLit in the file (no `forbiddenProxyHeaderLiterals` literal anywhere, case-insensitively). One pass is insufficient because a regression could land in a sibling helper added later; the file-wide pass catches that. Both passes share the same diagnostic prefix so operators see a consistent error format. (4) The scoped pass MUST also assert a required-identifier presence (`RemoteAddr` here — the fallback chain) AND emit a single 'missing function' hit if the named function is absent entirely, so the gate cannot pass silently when its target is removed by refactor. (5) SECURITY.md is the human-facing co-contract: the test pins (a) a verification-gate row in the Required Verification Gates table (with the `-run <Prefix>` selector — use a single shared test-function name prefix like `TestHTTPServerHardening` so one selector covers all four tests) AND (b) a dedicated section heading AND (c) a closed list of canonical substrings (operator expectations, the closed allow-list of headers BY NAME so a reader cannot mistake the policy, the load-bearing field name like `ReadHeaderTimeout`, and the test file path itself as the link to the enforcement). Watch line breaks in SECURITY.md substrings — a typed prose paragraph reflows and the apostrophe in `operator's` can land on its own line, breaking `strings.Contains`; either rewrap to keep the canonical phrase on a single line (preferred) or split into shorter pinned substrings. (6) The self-check sub-test feeds known-good AND known-bad synthetic fixtures through every matcher — for the struct-literal scan, every required field AND every forbidden field AND every forbidden call selector gets its own isolated regression fixture, plus a multiple-regressions fixture that confirms hits compose additively (e.g. missing-field + forbidden-field + forbidden-call = 3 hits). Forward-applicable to: any future security-verification story whose invariant is 'this typed struct literal in production code MUST/MUST NOT carry field X' or 'this named function MUST consult only allow-listed string literals' (e.g. middleware composition order pins, context-key allow-lists, telemetry attribute redaction key allow-lists, future proxy-trust expansions for X-Forwarded-Proto or RFC 7239 `Forwarded` if Yalla ever adopts them).
- Container image hardening static-pin pattern (BE-0355, internal/release/container_hardening_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "Container Image Hardening" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "Container image hardening" subsection; structural template for ANY future security-verification story whose invariant lives in a non-Go declarative file — Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml, .github/workflows/*.yml, .goreleaser.yaml, Kubernetes manifests, OCI runtime spec). Single-test variant of the BE-0344 two-test pattern: the "runtime" half is implicit because the build/CI pipeline IS the runtime — there is no application-code call site to drive a parallel runtime test against, so the static analyser plus the self-check IS the load-bearing defence. Six load-bearing rules: (1) The parser lives in the test file itself, not in a separate package — keeps the production package API surface clean (only the SupportedTargets matrix in internal/release) and lets future hardening stories add their own parsers without coupling. The Dockerfile parser scope is intentionally narrow: join backslash continuations into logical instructions, resolve global ARG defaults so `FROM ${RUNTIME_IMAGE}` expands to the canonical value, group instructions under their owning FROM stage. Heredocs, `--mount=type=cache` flags, and ONBUILD are deliberately opaque — the matchers work above the parser's awareness of them. (2) Every invariant gets its own matcher function plus its own in-repo test plus its own self-check sub-test. The matcher takes the parsed model and returns a self-describing error; the in-repo test runs the matcher against the real Dockerfile/compose file; the self-check sub-test drives the matcher with a synthetic known-good baseline (a `minimalCanonicalDockerfile()` helper) AND a series of mutations each isolating one regression. (3) Required-value constants live at the top of the test file: `requiredRuntimeBaseImage = "gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot"`, `requiredNonrootUser = "nonroot:nonroot"`, `requiredCopyChown = "--chown=nonroot:nonroot"`, `requiredBuilderBuildFlags = {CGO_ENABLED=0, -trimpath, -s -w}`. Closed sets for forbidden patterns: `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments = {TOKEN, PASSWORD, SECRET, API_KEY, PRIVATE_KEY, SIGNING_KEY, DSN}` (case-insensitive substring on ENV key), `forbiddenRuntimePackageInstalls = {apt-get, apt install, yum install, dnf install, apk add, microdnf install}` (substring on runtime stage's joined RUN payload). The closed-set design is deliberate — it catches the secret-shaped fragments the project's config layer actually uses, not every possible English word that contains "key", so a future ENV that holds a non-secret hint does not false-positive. (4) SECURITY.md is the human-facing co-contract: the same test file pins (a) a verification-gate row in the Required Verification Gates table AND (b) a dedicated `## Container Image Hardening` section with literal substrings (`distroless`, `nonroot`, `Multi-stage build`, `CGO_ENABLED=0`, `-trimpath`, `Container image hardening`). A regression that drops either is caught as deterministically as a Dockerfile regression. (5) The postgres image pin regex `^postgres:\d+$` deliberately rejects variant tags like `postgres:16-bookworm` because the local integration-test stack must remain reproducible across local environments; if a future story needs a variant the regex must widen in lockstep with the fixture catalogue. (6) Builder stage vs runtime stage have ASYMMETRIC rules: builder stages may install packages (`apt-get install git` for the goreleaser-style git tag lookup is legitimate), the runtime stage may not. The matcher pins the asymmetry by walking only the runtime stage's instructions for the package-install scan and only the builder stage's instructions for the build-flag scan. The runtime stage is identified as the LAST stage (the unnamed final FROM); the builder stage as the FIRST stage with an `AS` name. Five test-fixture gotchas: (a) when SECURITY.md and the matcher disagree on case (`multi-stage` vs `Multi-stage`), the test pins the EXACT casing the documentation file uses — Go's `strings.Contains` is case-sensitive, and pinning the documentation casing makes the matcher serve as a stability anchor for the published policy. (b) Self-check cases that mutate ONE invariant on top of a shared canonical baseline are independent by construction — a future widening of matcher M cannot accidentally pass a fixture that was constructed to test matcher N. (c) The runtime stage's `COPY --from=builder` matcher MUST scope the `--chown=` requirement to COPY instructions that ALSO carry `--from=` — a plain `COPY ./foo /etc/foo` from the build context legitimately does not need the flag because the build context's owner is the current user. (d) The `ADD <url>` matcher uses a regex anchored at start-of-args that tolerates leading `--chown=` style flags; a future addition that allows local-path `ADD` would fail this assertion only on URL shapes. (e) The Dockerfile parser silently ignores instructions that appear before the FIRST FROM (other than ARG, which becomes a global ARG default). This is the Dockerfile spec behaviour; the test relies on it so `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` parser directives and pre-FROM comments don't accidentally count as instructions in any stage. NOT applicable to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract WITH an application-code call site (request body size, JSON parser hardening, log redaction fuzzing) — those follow the BE-0344 two-test (static + runtime) shape because the runtime test has a real handler/repository call site to drive.
- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails); also pin the struct declaration has no mutation-timestamp field `{UpdatedAt,ModifiedAt,RevisedAt,LastModifiedAt}` — the SQL column absence is the migration's job, the Go field absence is the surface's job, both must hold. (3) DML-SCAN pin (walk every non-test `.go` in the owning package, find every `*ast.BasicLit` of `token.STRING` kind, unwrap raw-string and double-quoted shapes, normalise whitespace and case, and reject any literal that contains a forbidden DML substring against the target table; only the carved-out INSERT shape inside the repository's own file is allowed — every other shape is a regression flagged with `file:line: forbidden DML against <table>: "UPDATE <table>" in literal`). The case-insensitive uppercase normalisation handles lowercase shapes that snuck in via copy-paste; the whitespace normalisation handles multi-line literals like `UPDATE\n  audit_events\nSET ...`. Comments are NOT scanned — the rule targets executable code paths and a SQL example in a doc comment is fine. (4) RUNTIME-wire pin (insert a fixture row, attempt every UPDATE shape an operator might try — by id, by tenant id, table-wide without WHERE, partial-column-rewrite, verdict-reclassification — and assert each rejection surfaces a `*pgconn.PgError` with `pgErr.Code == "<sqlstate>"` AND `strings.Contains(pgErr.Message, "<canonical phrase>")` AND the row's columns are byte-identical to the original after the attempted UPDATE; PG's `restrict_violation` is SQLSTATE 23001 NOT 38000 — 38000 is external_routine_exception, an easy typo because both classes start with a 3, verify the actual wire code by running once and reading the failure). Also assert the error message does NOT echo any caller input — the trigger function is value-free by design and a future log path that interpolates the rejected payload would surface here as a redaction regression. (5) SELF-CHECK pin (drive each matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
- Cryptographic-primitive rotation contract pin (BE-0351 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{key_rotation_static_test.go, key_rotation_test.go} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/secrets/AGENTS.md "Key rotation" section; structural template for any future security-verification story over a long-lived cryptographic primitive that ships a rotation surface — JWT signing keys, webhook HMAC secrets, backup-target keys, mTLS issuance keys). Three load-bearing rules: (1) The cryptographic surface is pinned by a static AST analyser, not just by behavioural tests: parse the implementation file, locate the surface methods by name (`NewAESGCM`, `ActiveKeyID`, `KeyIDs`, `Open` for AESGCM), and assert each one's structural shape — `NewAESGCM` must take `[][]byte` (not a single key), `Open` must contain a range loop over the receiver's `keys` slice (not a single-key decrypt), `Open` must return the typed `ErrUnknownKey` identifier (not a string error). Behavioural tests catch the runtime regression but a static guard catches the silent refactor before the runtime test has a chance to fail. The self-check pins both directions of every analyser with synthetic regression sources. (2) The runtime test mirrors the operator runbook line-for-line as a three-phase rotation lifecycle: pre-rotation provider seals under k_old; mid-rotation provider boots with `[k_new, k_old]` and proves (a) old ciphertext still Opens via the retired key id, (b) new Seals reference the new active key id, (c) a fresh Seal under k_new does NOT decrypt under k_old's id (returns ErrInvalidCiphertext); re-seal step (the rotation worker's loop body) Opens under retired key then Seals under active and proves round-trip plaintext intact; post-retirement provider boots with `[k_new]` only and proves a row missed by the re-seal worker surfaces ErrUnknownKey (NOT ErrInvalidCiphertext, NOT a silent plaintext recovery). The runtime test reads as the runbook does so a future operator can compare the runbook against the executable contract. (3) The redacted-projection invariant is captured at runtime with a real `slog.NewJSONHandler` writing into a `bytes.Buffer`: `logger.LogAttrs(..., slog.Any("provider", provider))`, `json.Unmarshal` the result, assert `provider.master_keys == output.Sentinel`. The leak check substring-searches the JSON record for the FULL hex of every probe (k_old, k_new, plaintext, pre/mid ciphertext) — per-byte substring checks false-positive against the SHA-256-prefix key id (the 16-char id will collide with random 2-hex pairs in any 32-byte key at ~p=1/256 per byte). The same probes are checked against `fmt.Sprintf("%s", provider)` to pin the fmt.Stringer channel (panic messages). Both channels MUST also contain at least one accepted key id — the operator's only confirmation that a rotation took effect. (4) The store-layer routing index that the rotation worker depends on is pinned by a SEPARATE sealing trap in the migration package (the `migrations/` directory contains only .sql files — no Go package — so the trap lives in `internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go` and reads each migration via `fs.ReadFile(embeddedMigrations, ...)`). The regex matcher tolerates harmless variations (`IF NOT EXISTS`, optional whitespace, multi-line) but pins the load-bearing parts: index name, table, column order `(secret_provider, secret_key_id)`, and `WHERE is_secret = true` partial-index predicate. A migration that drops the WHERE clause widens the index from "secret rows only" to "every row", which changes the index's purpose and breaks the worker's bounded lookup; the self-check exercises eight known-bad shapes to pin this. The `requiredVariableMigrations` slice is the canonical set of `*_variables` tables — adding a new variable table without adding it here silently reduces coverage.
- Inbound-hostname domain-takeover guard, symmetric to the SSRF outbound-URL guard (BE-0350 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{domain_takeover.go, domain_takeover_static_test.go, domain_takeover_test.go, AGENTS.md} + network.go Domain() call site + fuzz_test.go FuzzDomain seeds; second worked port of the BE-0349 two-test SSRF template into the validate package, but for a STRUCTURALLY DIFFERENT threat: INBOUND hostnames the platform agrees to ROUTE traffic for, NOT outbound URLs the worker dereferences). Six load-bearing rules the new file added on top of the inherited BE-0349 template: (a) The takeover guard MUST live in a separate helper (`disallowedTakeoverHost`) and a separate static analyzer (`callsDisallowedTakeoverHost`) from the SSRF guard — they share the two-test template but check DISJOINT sets: a host that is legitimate to ATTACH (`myapp.example.com`) may still be illegitimate to FETCH if it resolves to a private IP, and vice versa. Reusing the SSRF helper would collapse both threat models into one, hiding which rejection class applies to which surface. (b) The guard's call site goes AT THE END of `Domain` (after the FQDN/label checks), not at the start. Single-label reserved names (`localhost`, `local`, `internal`, `lan`, etc.) and IPv6 literals (a single label after the `.` split) are rejected by the existing FQDN check BEFORE reaching the guard — so the test table for `TestDomainRejectsTakeoverTargets` restricts itself to inputs that actually pass through the helper today (IPv4 dotted literals, multi-label reserved exact-matches like `home.arpa`, suffix-match cases, shared-hosting eTLD bare forms). The single-label reserved names stay in `reservedTakeoverHostnames` as defence-in-depth (a future FQDN-check loosening must still trip the takeover guard) but the runtime regression table only asserts on what's actually reachable, so a missing call site is caught by a FAILING subtest rather than a silently-skipped one. Document the layering explicitly in the file's package-doc block AND in the test file's table-header comment, so the next reader doesn't add an unreachable case and then debug for an hour. (c) The reserved-name lists must DELIBERATELY EXCLUDE the RFC 2606 documentation-only suffixes (`.test`, `.example`, `.invalid`) from the SUFFIX walk while KEEPING them in the EXACT-MATCH map. Reason: those suffixes are not publicly routable so they cannot intercept real customer traffic — they are a squatting threat (bounded by the `service_domains` UNIQUE (hostname, path) constraint) rather than a takeover threat. The httpapi service-domains test fixtures across ~7 files (`api.canonical.example`, `domain-matrix-recognisable-needle.example`, etc.) use `.example` per RFC convention; adding `.example` to the suffix walk would break every one of those tests, and the cost is asymmetric: a real takeover defence would require migrating the fixtures whereas a softer squatting defence is bounded by the UNIQUE constraint and adds no value to either threat model. Document the omission AND the asymmetry in the helper's package-doc, the AGENTS.md section, and the test file's blocked-hosts table comment — so a future reader doesn't "fix" the omission and re-break the fixtures. (d) The wildcard interaction: `Domain` strips the leading `*.` BEFORE my helper sees the host. With wildcards enabled, `*.appspot.com` reduces to `appspot.com` which the exact-match map rejects — a wildcard makes the takeover STRICTLY WORSE (the bare suffix locks one routing entry, the wildcard locks every sub-tenant of that suffix) so the wildcard rejection is load-bearing and gets its own test (`TestDomainRejectsTakeoverTargetsForWildcard`). The helper ALSO defensively strips a leading `*.` itself so it remains correct for any future caller that passes the raw value. (e) The redaction backstop (`TestDomainTakeoverReasonsAreValueFree`) must plant the canary as a SUB-LABEL of a reserved-SUFFIX host (`canary + ".lan"`), NOT a reserved-EXACT-MATCH host (`canary + "appspot.com"` would not match the exact-match map). The suffix walk's `strings.HasSuffix(h, suffix)` reduces the entire host to the suffix-class reason without inspecting the canary — so a reason containing the canary proves the validator interpolated the value. The canary string itself MUST be picked to NOT collide with any word in the canonical reason set (no "domain", "namespace", "address", "literal", "hostname") — the BE-0343 redaction lesson is that substring redaction checks false-positive on stable classification phrases. (f) The FuzzDomain seed set added one entry per documented reject category PLUS an accept-branch re-derivation: `net.ParseIP(v) != nil` and `strings.HasSuffix(v, reservedSuffix)` MUST be false for every accepted host. The accept-branch re-derivation is what makes the fuzz target actually catch a regression of the takeover guard — without it, dropping the helper would let IPv4-literal seeds pass through to the accept branch without firing any assertion (the existing FQDN+label re-derivation accepts `8.8.8.8`). The reservedSuffixes slice in the fuzz target is a DUPLICATE of the package-private one — that is the cost of the validate_test (`_test` package) split: a future helper change must update BOTH lists or `TestDomainTakeoverListsAreCanonical` fires for the package list and the fuzz target silently accepts the dropped category. Document the duplication in the seed-loop comment.

- Security-verification SSRF blocklist pattern, validate-port instance (BE-0349 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{ssrf.go, ssrf_static_test.go, ssrf_test.go, fuzz_test.go, AGENTS.md}; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST in the validate package — confirms the template scales to "this pure validator MUST funnel its parsed host through a named blocklist helper" invariants in addition to single-function-hardening (BE-0346), per-call-site predicate scans (BE-0344/0345/0347), and absence-of-API scans (BE-0348). Six SSRF-flavoured rules this instance surfaces that the earlier five do NOT capture: (a) The analyzer is a per-function call-presence scan rather than a per-call-site scan or absence-of-API scan: each of {URL, GitRepoURL} is parsed via findFuncDecl, its body walked with ast.Inspect, and the predicate "calls disallowedSSRFHost by exact identifier" must hold. Exact-identifier matching is the load-bearing rule — a synthetic "calls disallowedHost (look-alike)" fixture is in the self-check so a future refactor that renames the helper inside one validator (but not the other) fails the static guard; substring or prefix matching here would mask the regression. (b) The reason text is a closed set, NOT a templated string with the host interpolated. Nine canonical reasons (8 IP-range classifications + 1 hostname classification) live in package-private constants emitted by ipReason / disallowedSSRFHost. The runtime test pins the set via canonicalSSRFReasons + isCanonicalSSRFReason and asserts every rejection is set-membership equal — strictly stronger than substring redaction checks, because words like "localhost" / "metadata" / "loopback" / "private" appear in the classification text BY DESIGN and would false-positive a substring "host did not appear in reason" guard. The BE-0343 redaction-fixture-collision pattern (choose canary values that don't collide with stable reason phrases) is the predecessor; the closed-set assertion is the next-strongest form. (c) Reject IP literals via net.ParseIP first, then hostnames; the order matters because an IP literal can never be made safe by downstream DNS, so the unconditional-reject decision happens before any hostname rule applies. The IP classifier uses Go stdlib's IsUnspecified / IsLoopback / IsLinkLocalUnicast / IsLinkLocalMulticast / IsMulticast / IsPrivate as the first cut, then adds the four IPv4 ranges stdlib does NOT classify (CGNAT 100.64/10, benchmark 198.18/15, limited broadcast 255.255.255.255 — 169.254/16 is already covered by IsLinkLocalUnicast). Whitelisting IsPrivate alone would miss the cloud-metadata range (169.254.169.254 is link-local, not private). (d) Hostname forbidden set is asymmetric: an exact-match map (forbiddenSSRFHostnames) for {localhost, ip6-localhost, ip6-loopback, metadata, metadata.google.internal, metadata.aws.internal, 169.254.169.254} and a leading-dot suffix list (forbiddenSSRFHostSuffixes) for {.localhost, .internal, .local, .localdomain}. The leading dot on suffixes is required — a bare ".local" suffix without the dot would false-positive on "flocal.example.com". TestSSRFHostnameListsAreCanonical is a sealing-trap pinning required entries AND the leading-dot invariant; dropping any documented entry fails the build. (e) GitRepoURL has THREE entry shapes (https:// / ssh:// / scp-like git@host:path) and the SSRF check must fire on ALL THREE: the analyzer pins the presence of ONE disallowedSSRFHost call in the function body, but the runtime test exercises each shape independently (https loopback, ssh link-local v6, scp .internal, etc.) so a future split that drops the call from the scp branch alone — analyzer still happy because the https branch still has it — surfaces in the runtime table. The lesson: presence-of-call analyzers are necessary but not sufficient; per-shape runtime coverage is the second wall. (f) IPv6 literals in URL form MUST use the bracketed `[fd00::1]` shape because url.Parse with a colon-bearing v6 address outside brackets parses the colons as port separators; the runtime test fixtures call this out explicitly. The disallowedSSRFHost helper strips one set of surrounding brackets so it accepts BOTH the URL-extracted (already unbracketed) form AND a literal bracketed form a caller might supply directly. Layered defence is explicit in AGENTS.md: this is the PURE validator layer (no DNS) and a second wall in the HTTP/git client at fetch time is REQUIRED to catch DNS-rebinding (a public name resolving to an internal IP at fetch time). The validator is the first wall; the runtime resolved-IP re-check is a separate story.
- Security-verification two-test pattern, httpapi-port cookie/CSRF instance (BE-0348 pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/{csrf_static_test.go, csrf_test.go, AGENTS.md}; FIFTH worked instance of the BE-0344 template and THIRD in httpapi after BE-0345 body size and BE-0347 CORS — confirms the template scales to "this surface must NOT use a named stdlib API family AND must NOT emit a named wire header" invariants in addition to single-function-hardening (BE-0346) and per-call-site predicate scans (BE-0344/BE-0345/BE-0347)). Five httpapi-flavoured rules this instance surfaces that the earlier four do NOT capture: (a) The CSRF stance is a TWO-prong analyzer (string literal scan + SelectorExpr scan) rather than the single-prong scans BE-0344..BE-0347 used, because cookie usage can enter production code through two structurally unrelated paths — the wire-level header literal (`w.Header().Set("Set-Cookie", ...)`) or the typed net/http API (`http.SetCookie(w, c)`) — and either alone is a cookie surface. The two prongs are kept in ONE analyzer function (`findCookieAPIUsages`) with a single AST walk that switches on `*ast.BasicLit` vs `*ast.SelectorExpr` per node, NOT two separate walks, so a regression that combines both shapes (the canonical `http.SetCookie(w, &http.Cookie{Name:"session"})` shape) produces three deterministic diagnostics in one pass. The self-check covers each prong AND the combined shape so a future change that disables one prong fails fast. (b) The selector set is asymmetric: `{SetCookie, AddCookie, CookieJar, Cookies}` are flagged on ANY X (because user-defined identifiers with these names would themselves be cookie regressions camouflaged behind friendly names), but `Cookie` alone is flagged ONLY when X is the bare `http` ident — because `.Cookie` matches too many user struct fields to flag unconditionally. The asymmetry is the load-bearing false-positive guard: a synthetic `type fixture struct{ Cookie string }; return f.Cookie` must pass cleanly while `&http.Cookie{Name:"session"}` must fail. The self-check has both fixtures wired explicitly so the asymmetry can't drift. (c) The forbidden cookie header set is exact-match (case-insensitive) over `{cookie, set-cookie, cookie2, set-cookie2}` rather than the prefix-match shape BE-0347 used for `access-control-`. Exact match is correct here because the bare word "cookie" appears in unrelated header names (no IANA cookie header outside this set exists today) — a prefix match would false-positive on imaginary `cookie-policy`/`cookie-jar` extensions. The four-element set is the complete RFC 6265 + obsolete RFC 2965 surface; a future extension would be a deliberate retrofit reviewed against the threat model. (d) The runtime test pattern reuses `corsHeaderHolder` and `recorderShim` from BE-0347's cors_test.go directly — both helpers were already package-private, so the CSRF runtime test gets the determinism-comparison shape "two requests with/without an attacker header return the same response header key set" for free. Future security-verification stories in this package that need the same shape (e.g. "the X-Forwarded-For header does not influence the response" or "the Host header does not influence the response") should reuse `corsHeaderHolder` rather than declaring a third interface — the CORS interface is the canonical "{ Header() http.Header }" surface for header-emission invariants. (e) Cookie-sentinel redaction backstop: every runtime test plants a globally-unique sentinel (`session=yalla-csrf-sentinel-9f3c2a7e-do-not-echo`) in the request Cookie header and asserts both the sentinel string AND its bare value never appear in the response body. The bare-value check is the load-bearing one — a regression that logged the Cookie header into an error message would likely strip the `session=` prefix when JSON-encoding, leaving only the bare value, so checking ONLY the prefixed form would silently miss the redaction failure. The sentinel-not-echoed assertion ports verbatim to any future security-verification story whose threat model includes "this request header value must not be reflected in the response" (Cookie, Authorization, X-Api-Key, Range, If-Match, etc.).

- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfiguration that silently reduces the rotation pool is caught fast. (4) cmd/yalla-api wires the provider in ONE buildSecretsProvider helper near runStartupChecks, NOT one per scope. Strict profiles (staging/production) refuse to start without YALLA_SECRET_KEYS (config.requireStrictFields enforces) via an explicit sentinel error; permissive profiles (local/test) fall back to Plaintext with a Warn log line. (5) The interface is INTENTIONALLY same-width every scope needs (org/project/env/service variables), so a single provider configured at startup covers every variables table — sibling at-rest stories become mechanical clones of schema + service + test changes. (6) For internal-only read paths that legitimately need the plaintext (the future Dokploy renderer worker, BE-0343 variable-inheritance resolver), Open the sealed tuple through the same Provider passed into the service — NEVER a wire-facing endpoint. Six load-bearing rules for the storage shape per table: (1) Add three nullable columns (secret_provider text NULL, secret_key_id text NULL, secret_ciphertext bytea NULL) ALONGSIDE the existing plain `value` column, never instead of it; force value='' for secret rows and cipher columns NULL for non-secret rows via ONE CHECK constraint named <table>_secret_columns_consistent that pins the invariant in both directions. The CHECK is the defence-in-depth backstop the service relies on — a bug that tries to write a plaintext secret rolls back as deterministic apierr.Conflict rather than silent corruption. (2) Mirror DO $$ guards in BOTH the up and the down migration that REFUSE to run while is_secret=true rows exist with the wrong shape — a re-application against a database that accumulated plaintext secrets fails loudly rather than silently leaving them behind, and a rollback against a database with sealed rows fails loudly rather than silently losing every secret value. (3) Add a partial index on (secret_provider, secret_key_id) WHERE is_secret=true so the future key-rotation worker (BE-0351) can locate rows referencing a retired key without a full-table scan. (4) The repository's Upsert + UpdateMutable signatures take a closed-set Input tuple (<Table>VariableUpsert with Value/IsSecret/SecretProvider/SecretKeyID/SecretCiphertext) — keeps the signature narrow as new secret fields land and centralises the "nullable columns when is_secret=false" projection in one helper (nullableSecretColumns). (5) The OrganizationVariable struct (and siblings) gain the three encryption-at-rest fields as INTERNAL-only — never on the wire — and the LogValue redaction MUST be updated in the SAME edit to redact BOTH the plaintext AND the new ciphertext to output.Sentinel; an unstale slog record that captures the struct cannot leak either. (6) scanRow projection routes through sql.NullString for the nullable text columns (pgx-scan into *string rejects NULL). Five load-bearing rules for the service-layer seam: (1) Seal at the SERVICE layer, never the repository — Seal is a side-effecting call that depends on a runtime Provider not a *Tx, calling Seal BEFORE opening the transaction shortens the SQL critical section, and the repository stays pure SQL. The cleanest helper is a one-line projector (sealVariableValue) that takes the customer's input shape (<Table>VariableReplace) and returns the repository's tuple shape (<Table>VariableUpsert) — non-secret items pass through untouched, secret items go through Provider.Seal. (2) The Service's constructor (NewOrganizationVariableService) takes Provider as a REQUIRED dependency rejected at construction if nil — a production process cannot accidentally start with the at-rest seam disabled. (3) Patch path for sealed rows has THREE post-state cases the service must enumerate explicitly: (a) caller supplied a new value → use it verbatim; (b) caller supplied only is_secret AND the current row is_secret → Open the current ciphertext via Provider.Open to recover the plaintext, then re-Seal under the active key (the only correct way to re-key a secret without surfacing the plaintext to the caller); (c) otherwise → use the current row's `value` column. Skipping case (b) would either lose the secret bytes on a re-seal or write a "previously-sealed plaintext" string into the value column. (4) The demotion path (is_secret=true→false) reuses case (b) to recover the plaintext, then writes it back to the value column with NULL encryption columns — the ONLY customer path that surfaces a previously-sealed plaintext, and only as a deliberate operator action. (5) Seal failures surface as apierr.Internal with a value-free wrapped cause — a sealing error is a server-side problem, never customer input, and the wrap never echoes the plaintext. Six load-bearing rules for the integration-test surface: (1) The integration test file lives next to the existing repository tests as <table>_secrets_at_rest_test.go, uses testutil.RequireMigratedDB (skips cleanly when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres), and wires a REAL AES-256-GCM provider (mustAESGCM helper that mints a fresh 32-byte key per test so parallel tests cannot bleed key ids across each other). (2) Five pinned tests per scope: SealsSecretValuesAtRest (the load-bearing contract — secret value reaches Postgres ONLY as AES-GCM ciphertext under the active key id, value column is "", on-disk bytea does not contain the plaintext substring, ciphertext round-trips through Provider.Open, audit row never carries any of {plaintext, key name, hex ciphertext, key id, provider id}); PatchRotatesCiphertextOnEveryWrite (a patch that flips is_secret only on a sealed row Opens-then-Seals under a fresh nonce so on-disk bytes change between writes); PatchDemotesSecretToPlaintext (is_secret=true→false with no new value recovers the plaintext via Open and writes to the value column with NULL encryption columns); SchemaRejectsPlaintextSecretWrite (three direct INSERT attempts that would land a plaintext-secret row each surface a constraint violation whose error message names the constraint); LogValueRedactsCiphertextAndPlaintext (a structured log record that captures the variable struct redacts BOTH plaintext AND ciphertext to output.Sentinel while exposing provider id + key id). (3) For the existing tests, fixture-shape changes (seedOrganizationVariable wrote plaintext into the value column for is_secret=true rows) need updating IN PLACE rather than introducing a second helper — the new helper writes the encryption columns under the Plaintext provider's wire identifiers ("plaintext-v1" / "plaintext") for is_secret=true fixtures, so every legacy seedOrganizationVariable(..., true) call site keeps working. (4) Pair the fixture change with a package-level test helper (revealVariableValue) that projects OrganizationVariable.Value back to the plaintext for both shapes — for the Plaintext provider, ciphertext IS the plaintext bytes, so the helper is one branch (if IsSecret → string(SecretCiphertext) else v.Value). Tests that previously asserted v.Value == "expected" become revealVariableValue(v) == "expected" with no further restructuring. (5) ActorKind in audit-fed test inputs MUST be one of {"", "usr", "sa", "system"} — the audit_events.actor_kind CHECK constraint will reject "user" / "human" / any other value as a 23xxx that surfaces as a misleading "an audit event with this id already exists" via mapWriteError's canonical conflict message. The actual diagnostic is the actor_kind CHECK, not duplicate id. (6) When extracting Value/Plaintext assertions out of failing tests against a contract change, FIRST stash the working tree and re-run the failing tests on HEAD to separate "I introduced this failure" from "this was already failing"; the diff of failure name sets is the cleanest signal that the new code is at parity with the baseline. BE-0339 used this to confirm 23 of the 25 store-wide failures and 3 of the 12 org-variable failures were pre-existing. NOT applicable to BE-0343 (variable inheritance resolver) — that story consumes the at-rest seam through Provider.Open against multiple scopes simultaneously and needs its own pattern around the merge order + per-scope Open call site. NOT applicable to BE-0351 (secret encryption key rotation) — that story consumes the partial index organization_variables_secret_routing_idx + the KeyIDs() / ActiveKeyID() surface to enumerate rows referencing a retired key and re-Seal them, but the rotation worker is a separate operational concern from the at-rest write path this pattern owns.
- Per-service-type renderer-subtype contract pin (BE-0301 pattern, internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_application_git_test.go; structural template for BE-0303 application-image, BE-0305 application-drop-artifact, BE-0307 compose, BE-0309..0317 database engines, BE-0319 worker, BE-0321 cron — every `provisioning-service-type` PRD story whose durable provisioning worker / dokploy_refs / quota-conversion seam is deferred to a later worker story but whose declarative-content contract is owed today): single t.Parallel test file in package dokploy_test, anchored on two per-subtype input-helpers (applicationGitDockerfileInput / applicationGitNixpacksInput) that reuse the shared validInput()/fixedID()/mustRender() helpers from renderer_test.go so canonical-id + per-level secret fixtures stay in lockstep with every other renderer pin. Seven load-bearing pins per subtype: (1) subtype-identity happy path per builder variant (asserts Service.Type, Build.Builder, builder-specific fields preserved verbatim, and the subtype boundary — the foreign-builder-only field stays empty); (2) DropsXField subtype-boundary test that submits the foreign-builder field and proves both renderer-drop AND that the submitted value never appears in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String() — the structural barrier against the SIBLING subtype story (BE-0301 vs BE-0303 boundary: Image dropped for dockerfile/nixpacks; BE-0303 will mirror with a DropsGitField test pinning git_branch/commit/dockerfile_path drop for builder=image); (3) RoleMatrix across (web / worker / cron) × (dockerfile / nixpacks builder) — closure-captured builder name in `string(builderInput.Build.Builder)` keeps subtest names readable; (4) ValidationFailures table per subtype using stable wantField + per-row leakyValues — every leaked-value candidate the mutate() introduces is added to a shared leakyValues slice so the no-echo assertion runs against ALL submitted values per case, not just the row's own input; (5) TierResourceDefaults pin across (tier × role × builder) — pins the load-bearing 2-replica production web default + 1-replica everywhere-else default and proves the builder choice does NOT affect resource allocation; (6) RedactsAllVariableValuesAcrossRoles that asserts BOTH (a) the verbatim RenderedSpec carries the secret value the worker needs AND (b) every debug surface (Summary JSON, slog JSON record, Summary.String()) shows output.Sentinel for EVERY variable value — Secret-flagged AND plain — so a future logging path cannot turn the non-secret variable channel into an exfiltration vector; (7) Determinism per builder. The pattern is the renderer-layer counterpart to the engine-pin pattern used for admin-action policy matrices (BE-0285 / BE-0288 / BE-0291 / BE-0294 / BE-0297 / BE-0300) — a state-independent file that pins the contract the future worker-side story must continue to satisfy. The acceptance-criteria coverage map per story: AC1 (renderer supports + validates required fields) is pinned by the success + validation table; AC2 (provisioning worker + dokploy_refs atomic) defers to the worker story but the renderer-side determinism + drop-foreign-field invariants are the structural barrier the worker depends on; AC3 (quota reserved before enqueue) defers — already pinned generically by BE-0323's projectservice + quota.Checker integration; AC4 (fake Dokploy create/update/deploy/poll/failure/retry/replay) defers to the worker story; AC5 (yalla.output.v1 / yalla.error.v1 envelopes) is already pinned globally by apienvelope/apienvelope_test.go — the renderer file just confirms apierr.InvalidInput surfaces; AC6 (unit tests for success/validation/auth/not-found) is pinned at the renderer layer for success+validation, auth+not-found defer to the wire-endpoint story; AC7 (integration tests against migrated Postgres) is the worker story's surface; AC8 (secrets redacted in logs/errors/audit/test output) is pinned per-role per-builder by the RedactsAllVariableValuesAcrossRoles permutation. NOT applicable to subtypes whose subtype-boundary lives outside the dokploy renderer's BuildSettings switch (e.g. BE-0305 application-drop-artifact will need a new builder constant first and a sibling pin that mirrors but also pins the new builder's renderer contract; BE-0309..0317 database engines pin Engine instead of Builder and the role field is dropped — those will follow the database variant of this template).
- BE-0303 application-image confirms the inverted-boundary clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_application_image_test.go): a single-builder subtype (Builder == BuilderImage) collapses the BE-0301 builderCase outer loop to a single role-only loop, swaps DropsImageField for DropsGitFields (submits leaky dockerfile_path + git_branch + git_commit and asserts every one is dropped AND never appears in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String()), and adds the image-builder-missing-image case to the ValidationFailures table (the case BE-0301 cannot pin because git-source builders never require an Image). The leakyValues slice grows to include the rendered Image reference so an echoed image-builder error also fails. The renderer's normaliseBuild() switch already encodes both invariants — Image is required for BuilderImage and every git-source field is dropped — so BE-0303 ships test-only (no production code change) as a structural barrier against accidental weakening. The same single-builder collapse applies to future BE-0319 worker (Role fixed to RoleWorker, drops domains) and BE-0321 cron (Role fixed to RoleCron, drops domains, requires cron_schedule) sibling pins.
- BE-0319 worker is the FIRST role-axis clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_worker_test.go): when the per-PRD-story subtype identity is the Role (Role == RoleWorker) rather than Build.Builder, Service.Type, or Service.Engine, the renderer-subtype pin generalises with four structural swaps from the canonical Builder-axis template. (1) The two input helpers (workerApplicationInput / workerComposeInput) span the two ServiceType variants where the worker role applies (application + compose), mirroring how the BE-0301 helpers span two builder variants — the orthogonal axis becomes ServiceType because the role is now the fixed axis. Both helpers explicitly clear Domains and CronSchedule in their bases, so the "submit a foreign-axis value and prove the drop" assertion stays load-bearing. (2) The DropsXField pin (TestRenderWorkerDropsWebAndCronFields) submits BOTH a leaky domain host and a leaky cron schedule in one test, proving the worker drops TWO foreign-axis fields at once (web's Domains + cron's CronSchedule); the same Summary-JSON / slog-record / Summary.String() redaction sweep used by BE-0303 catches leaks of either value. This is the role-axis analogue of "git fields dropped for image builder" — the structural barrier against any future refactor that would weaken the silent drops at lines `cronSchedule = ""` and the "rendered only for web" Domains contract. (3) The BuilderMatrix subtest takes the place of the BE-0301 RoleMatrix: since Role is now the fixed axis, the orthogonal axes become (ServiceType × Builder) yielding 2×4=8 subtests that prove every supported builder works on every supported service type while preserving the worker shape (no domains, no cron, no replica bump). The check closure per builder stays mechanical — same pattern as BE-0303's single-builder verification but generalised to a four-builder set. (4) The ValidationFailures table deliberately OMITS the cron_schedule legs (missing schedule, bad schedule) because a worker is never a cron service — those legs are owned by the role's own pin (BE-0301/0303/0305/0307 already cover them via web-role inputs flipped to cron, and BE-0321 will own them on the cron-role side). The TierResourceDefaults pin shrinks from a 6-row (tier × {web, worker}) matrix to a 3-row (tier-only) matrix because the worker role is fixed; every row pins replicas=1, the INVERSE of BE-0301/BE-0307's production-web replica bump. Production code change: zero — the renderer already drops Domains and CronSchedule for non-web/non-cron roles. The role-axis pin is the FOURTH structural template in the renderer-subtype family after the Builder axis (BE-0301/0303/0305), the ServiceType axis (BE-0307), and the Engine axis (BE-0309..0317). BE-0321 (cron) will be the FIFTH role-axis variant, cloning this file with a 3-token swap (Worker→Cron in identifiers, RoleWorker→RoleCron, label value) plus one structural addition: a CronScheduleValidation leg in ValidationFailures, since the cron role IS subject to cron_schedule validation while the worker role is not — the inverse omission to the one this file documents.
- BE-0307 compose is the FIRST type-boundary clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_compose_test.go): when the per-PRD-story subtype identity is the ServiceType ITSELF (Service.Type == ServiceCompose) rather than the Build.Builder, the seven-pin template is preserved with three structural swaps. (1) Input helpers (composeDockerfileInput / composeNixpacksInput) carry the SAME two git builders as application-git — the boundary is NOT what the builder is — and the only difference vs applicationGit*Input is `in.Service.Type = dokploy.ServiceCompose`. (2) The Drops* test becomes a SubtypeBoundaryFromApplication test that renders BOTH composeDockerfileInput() AND applicationGitDockerfileInput() side-by-side and asserts each rendered Service.Type matches its input verbatim — neither is silently coerced — AND that the `yalla.service-type` label in spec.Service.Labels reflects the same value, because that label is the audit/observability surface a downstream consumer queries to distinguish a compose stack from a single-container application without re-reading the spec body; an alternate seam-coverage shape preserves the structural barrier when there is no foreign-builder-only field to drop. (3) The ValidationFailures table expands to cover EVERY builder's failure path that the subtype can reach — compose may use any of dockerfile/nixpacks/image/drop-artifact, so BE-0307's table includes BOTH the image-builder-missing-image case (from BE-0303) AND the drop-artifact-builder-missing-artifact-url case (from BE-0305) PLUS the bad-builder case — the leakyValues slice grows to carry the rendered Image AND ArtifactURL references so any echo from those branches fails too. The RoleMatrix / TierResourceDefaults / Redaction / Determinism pins keep the seven-pin shape intact, but RoleMatrix and the per-test invariants assert `Service.Type == ServiceCompose && Image == "" && ArtifactURL == "" && Engine == ""` (engine is database-only — that is the OTHER per-type seam BE-0309..0317 will pin). The load-bearing production-web 2-replica default participates here too: defaultResources() in renderer.go gates the bump on `(typ == ServiceApplication || typ == ServiceCompose)`, so TestRenderComposeTierResourceDefaults pins the compose half of that disjunction directly — a future regression that narrowed the disjunction to ServiceApplication-only would flip this pin. Forward-applicable to ANY future per-service-type subtype whose differentiator is the ServiceType itself (currently only compose vs application; future "function" / "edge" / similar would clone this template); NOT applicable to the BE-0309..0317 database engines, which pin Engine instead of either ServiceType or Builder, drop the role field entirely (effectiveRole := "" for ServiceDatabase in renderer.go:351), and need a database variant whose RoleMatrix collapses to a single (no-role) case and whose Engine-required + Image-dropped invariants supersede the Build.Builder switch's normalisation. The test-only-no-prod-code-change shape from BE-0303 is preserved — BE-0307 ships purely as a structural barrier against accidental weakening of the renderer's existing service-type discrimination.
- Real-quota concurrent oversubscription integration test (BE-0323 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_quota_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0324..0338 — every other quota-dimension PRD story that needs to prove "parallel requests cannot oversubscribe the quota" against the dimension's owning Service.Create call): one Go test, t.Parallel, against testutil.RequireMigratedDB; wires a REAL *quota.Checker over a REAL *store.QuotaRepository + quota.StaticPlanResolver, and a REAL *store.<Resource>Service + REAL *store.AuditRepository, with the only fakes being the Authorizer and JobEnqueuer (recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs from projectservice_test.go) which always succeed because their failure-path stories are covered elsewhere. Six load-bearing rules: (1) pre-seed quota_usage with used_value=0 for (org, dimension) BEFORE the race so the SELECT FOR UPDATE inside Checker.LockUsage acquires an existing row rather than racing inserts — the lock contract under test is row-lock serialisation, not row-creation; (2) seed quota_policies as scope_kind='plan_default' (not 'organization' override) so the plan-default fallback path inside QuotaRepository.EffectiveLimit is the one exercised — the org-override path has its own dedicated repository test (TestQuotaRepositoryEffectiveLimitPrefersOrgOverride); (3) launch attempts > limit goroutines (BE-0323 uses 20 attempts / limit 5 — 4:1 ratio gives a comfortable margin against any future pool-size or scheduling change without inflating runtime past ~0.5s); each goroutine generates its OWN canonical resource id via domain.MustNewID + a unique canonical slug fmt.Sprintf("p-%02d", i) so a slug conflict cannot disguise itself as a quota rejection; (4) assert successes == limit AND rejections == attempts - limit exactly, NOT >= / <= — the lock contract guarantees an exact split, and a "successes >= limit / <= attempts" range would silently let a regression through; (5) for EVERY rejection, assert all five ExceededDetail fields (Resource, Limit, Requested=1, Reserved==limit, Current==0) because at the moment the lock yields to a losing transaction every winning reservation is already committed — anything less than Reserved==limit means the lock did not serialise — and assert the diagnostic string never echoes any caller input (the i-indexed slug, display name, request id, or correlation id) so a future logging path cannot turn the error into a content channel; (6) post-race assertions cover three independent surfaces of the same invariant: raw count of projects rows for the org (must equal limit), raw count of active quota_reservations rows for the dimension (must equal limit), AND quotaRepo.SumActiveReservations through Store.Read (must equal limit) — the third surface catches a future regression where the table content diverges from the repository's view. Fake-call assertions pin step order at the unit-of-work level: authz.calls == attempts (every transaction reaches authorization) but jobs.calls == limit (rejected transactions never reach the JobEnqueuer because quota fails first and rolls back the whole tx). The dimension constant comes from the public store.QuotaResource* surface (BE-0323 uses store.QuotaResourceProjects) so a future rename surfaces as a compile error rather than a flaky test. NOT applicable to quota dimensions whose enforcement seam lives outside the *Service.Create unit of work (e.g. cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb where the dimension is summed across child resources — those need a different shape that exercises the dimension's actual enforcement gate). The CapDeploy concurrent_deployments dimension is the load-bearing precedent for "summed across rows" enforcement (DeploymentService.Create at deployment_service.go:209+691) and would clone this template by swapping the Service + dimension while keeping the six rules intact.
- testutil.Factory id-prefix gotcha (BE-0324 surfaced while cloning the BE-0323 pattern): the in-memory factory builds IDs with prefixes "prj"/"env"/"svc" but domain.KindProject is "proj" — the "prj_" form does NOT pass domain.ParseID. Tests that pass a factory Project.ID into an input which re-validates project_id (CreateEnvironmentInput, CreateServiceInput, etc.) get rejected with E_INVALID_INPUT. Workaround: seed parents with raw SQL using domain.MustNewID(domain.KindProject).String() (mirror seedDomainOrg). Owed: align the factory's id prefixes with the canonical domain.Kind values in a focused triage story.
- Race-safe local fakes for concurrent integration tests (BE-0324 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_quota_concurrent_test.go atomicAuthorizer / atomicJobs): the shared recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs / recordingQuota fakes in projectservice_test.go use plain int counters with no synchronisation, so the BE-0323-pattern 20-goroutine concurrent integration test trips the race detector against a live Postgres (BE-0323's TestProjectServiceCreateRejectsParallelOversubscriptionForProjectsQuota fails under -race for the same reason — a pre-existing issue documented under "Known pre-existing -race-with-DB failures" and owed to a focused triage story). For BE-0325..0338 (and any new concurrent integration test) define LOCAL race-safe fakes in the new test file: type atomicAuthorizer struct{ calls atomic.Int64 } satisfying the Authorizer port with calls.Add(1) on Authorize, and the equivalent atomicJobs for the JobEnqueuer port. Read counts with .Load() after wg.Wait(). Keeps the new test green under -race without touching the shared recording fakes or 80+ unrelated test files; the migration back to shared fakes belongs to the triage story when those are made atomic.
- Endpoint-pinned twin of an action-level engine-only policy matrix (BE-0288 pattern, internal/controlplane/policy/admin_dokploy_reconcile_endpoint_policy_test.go, sibling of BE-0285's admin_reconcile_policy_test.go): when two PRD stories cover two distinct WIRE ENDPOINTS that share the SAME action constant (admin.reconcile here — GET /v1/admin/dokploy/drift + POST /v1/admin/dokploy/reconcile), each story gets its own engine-only matrix file, structurally parallel but state-independent. Three load-bearing rules: (1) every canonical id (project / sibling / service-account principal) is renamed with the endpoint-distinguishing suffix so a future shared helper cannot accidentally couple the two matrices — BE-0288 uses canonicalAdminReconcileEndpointProjectID / canonicalAdminReconcileEndpointSiblingID and ServiceAccount IDs suffixed `_reconcile_endpoint`, deliberately distinct from BE-0285's `_reconcile` suffix; (2) docstrings frame the engine pin around the SPECIFIC endpoint's failure mode — BE-0285's file frames the action as "the contract admin.reconcile must satisfy regardless of which endpoint surfaces it" (action-level), BE-0288's file frames it as "the engine contract specifically for the mutating reconcile verb where an asymmetric regression that left listing intact but downgraded mutation capability cannot ship without flipping one of these expectations" (endpoint-level); (3) the test surface is structurally identical seven-function shape (Roles / Revoked&Expired / WrongOrg / Project / Env / Service / OrgGrant / ForeignHomeOrg) and verdict matrix is byte-identical to the action-level twin — the point of the duplication is endpoint-specific regression coverage, not behavioural difference at the engine seam. Applicable to any future PRD story pair where the wire endpoint differs but the policy action is shared (currently unique to admin.reconcile in BE-0285+0288; future similar pairs would clone this template). NOT applicable when two endpoints have distinct action constants — those follow the BE-0279/0282 sibling-action template instead, where the differentiator is the action's capability not the endpoint's role.
- Engine-only policy-matrix file pattern for CapSupport actions (BE-0285 admin.reconcile, applicable to admin.import / admin.read / admin.break_glass when their wire endpoints have not landed): same structural shape as the BE-0282 (job.cancel) CapDeploy template, but with three load-bearing flips: (1) role matrix collapses to one allow (Support) + five denies — the photo-negative of CapDeploy actions, (2) cross-tenant test flips Support from DENY (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) to ALLOW (ReasonAllowedBySupport) because CapSupport sits INSIDE the engine's `required == CapRead || CapSupport` exception clause — the single most load-bearing distinction from BE-0279/0282 and the structural barrier against any future catalog drift that demoted admin.reconcile out of CapSupport, (3) grant-containment cases use RoleSupport for the "happy path" grant role and RoleAdmin for the "wrong capability" deny because only RoleSupport holds CapSupport at any role-grant combination. Resource Kind is KindOrganization with only Scope.OrganizationID pinned (admin actions operate on the source-of-truth view of an entire organization, no project/env/svc leg), so every project/env/service-scoped grant denies via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (one-way covers()) — admin actions are unreachable through any scoped grant other than org-level Support. The foreign-home-org "stolen scoped key" test uses a ServiceAccount principal with empty Role and a foreign-org Support grant: the principal's empty `Role.roleCaps()` does NOT contain CapSupport, so the cross-tenant exception clause does NOT fire even though the grant nominally pins RoleSupport — empty Role is treated as "no role caps", NOT as ReasonDeniedUnknownRole, because the engine checks `if principal.Role != "" && !roleOK` before denying unknown roles. Canonical project id pair (`canonicalAdminReconcileProjectID` / `canonicalAdminReconcileSiblingID`) is deliberately distinct from sibling-file ids (`canonicalJobCancelProjectID`, etc.) so the read/retry/cancel/reconcile matrices cannot accidentally share state through a future shared helper.
- Operations artefact verification leaf (BE-0409 pattern, internal/controlplane/build/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, api_dockerfile_test.go}; structural template for BE-0410 worker Dockerfile, BE-0411 compose, BE-0412 k8s/Helm, BE-0413 systemd, BE-0414+ migration / seed-admin / backup / restore commands, BE-0418 config example files): canonical shape for ANY operations artefact (Dockerfile, docker-compose, k8s manifest, systemd unit, deploy script) the backend ships at the repo root. One Go test file per artefact in this package (api_dockerfile_test.go, worker_dockerfile_test.go, compose_test.go, ...), package `build_test` (external _test package) so the verification surface has no production-code dependency. Tests locate the artefact via repoRoot(t) which walks up from os.Getwd() until it finds go.mod — hard-coded relative paths break when the test moves. The verification surface is structural (regex over Dockerfile text, set-membership over .dockerignore lines, YAML parse + key lookup for compose/k8s) rather than a real `docker build` because the audit properties operations review cares about (non-root user, pinned tags, no baked secrets, EXPOSE matches the production listen address from config.ProfileProduction's apiAddr default) are textual, not behavioural. Forbidden-secret list MUST track config.Config's secret fields (DatabaseURL, SigningKeys, DokployToken) plus the most common database-side secret (POSTGRES_PASSWORD); extend it in lockstep when future config stories add new secret fields. ENV NAME=VAL, legacy `ENV NAME VAL`, and `ARG NAME=VAL with non-empty default` are ALL three banned forms — a developer who pastes `ARG YALLA_DOKPLOY_TOKEN=local-dev-token` into a Dockerfile fails this test in CI. Distroless static-debian12:nonroot is the canonical runtime image for any CGO_ENABLED=0 Go binary in this repo (UID/GID 65532, no shell, no package manager); HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted because the image has no curl/wget/sh — orchestrators probe /healthz and /readyz over HTTP directly. The BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache,target=/go/pkg/mod` and `/root/.cache/go-build`) require the `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` directive at the top of the file; without that line the syntax silently falls back to legacy and the cache mounts become no-ops. GO_VERSION and RUNTIME_IMAGE are ARGs with pinned defaults so a downstream operator can override them without editing the file — and the verification test asserts the ARG defaults themselves are not :latest. The .dockerignore is documented as defence-in-depth, not an authorization boundary (secrets must never be checked in regardless); the required-exclusion set tested includes .git, .env, secrets/, *.pem, *.key. EXPOSE port is pinned to 8080 to match ProfileStaging/ProfileProduction's apiAddr default in internal/controlplane/config/load.go — drift here would force operators to read the source to know which port to map.

- Hard DELETE endpoint on a versioned bare-{id}-resolver child resource (BE-0244 pattern, DELETE /v1/services/{service_id}/domains/{domain_id}, store/service_domain.go ServiceDomainRepository.DeleteByID with optional If-Match + classifyServiceDomainConcurrencyMiss reuse, store/service_domain.go ServiceDomainService.Delete orchestrator (no in-tx Authorize — reserved for Create; structural-identifier-only audit metadata; no caller-body fields because a DELETE has none), httpapi/service_domains.go ServiceDomainDeleter port + deleteServiceDomainHandler (parseIfMatchVersion + 200 OK + snapshot payload — never 204, keeps wire shape uniform with the other service-domain endpoints), httpapi/routes.go DELETE entry with serviceIDResolver, contract suite at service_domains_delete_test.go, policy matrix at service_domains_delete_policy_test.go): canonical shape for any future bare-{id} DELETE on a versioned child where the row is removed outright (no soft-delete column, no scheduled-deletion stamp — the resource is a routing artefact, not a continuing audit-trail tie). The "deleter MUST never run on any deny path" assertion is the destructive-op counterpart of the updater-not-reached invariant — more load-bearing for DELETE because a row destroyed in the background while the wire reports 403 is the worst possible failure mode. Mechanically clonable from the PATCH twin (BE-0241+0242+0243) by s/Update/Delete/ + s/update/delete/ + s/Patcher/Deleter/ + action swap.
- Port-arity sweep tooling (BE-0244 mechanical refinement of the BE-0175 ALL-FOUR-FORMS rule): when bumping NewHandler's arity by one positional port, the cleanest mechanical sweep across every test's NewHandler call site is `perl -i -pe 's/fakePREVIOUS\{\},/fakePREVIOUS{}, fakeNEW{},/g' $(grep -rl 'fakePREVIOUS{}' internal/controlplane/httpapi/)` keyed on the IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING fake type literal. BE-0244 sweep updated 69 test files in one pass. The single non-literal call site (a `var, fakeService...` pattern where the previous port is bound to a local variable rather than the bare struct literal) is fixed by hand with one extra perl. Then `go build ./...` confirms the arity matches end-to-end; the new fake type itself is declared once in the new test file (here: fakeServiceDomainDeleter in service_domains_delete_test.go) and is visible to every test file in the same package.
- Repository tenant-isolation for a tenant-root table (BE-0426 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/organization_tenant_isolation_test.go; structural template for BE-0428/0430/0432/0434/0436 and every future tenant-root-table isolation story): the strongest isolation guarantee in the codebase lives on the row-id-keyed tenant root (organizations) and the proof shape is "byte-identical snapshot of the OTHER tenant across every mutation path the repository exposes". Skeleton: one Go test file per table, one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) — Get returns-only-named-tenant + Get unknown-id-is-NotFound (cross-tenant + missing render the same yerr.CodeNotFound — no oracle); Update only-touches-named-tenant (snapshot orgB, Update orgA, re-read orgB, assert byte-identical across slug, display_name, version, updated_at, deletion_scheduled_at; assert orgA's update DID land so a no-op repository can't pass trivially); Update-with-mismatched-If-Match also-does-not-touch-orgB (catches "fall back to unconditional UPDATE on stale" regressions); ScheduleDeletion only-touches-named-tenant (stamp on orgA, orgB byte-identical incl. DeletionScheduledAt == nil); Get-of-soft-deleted-tenant-does-not-mask-other (soft-delete is per-row, not SQL-level hide; the scheduled row is still readable and orgB unchanged); Insert enforces-global-slug-uniqueness (typed yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty user-facing Message; first row's slug unchanged; second row's id is yerr.CodeNotFound on follow-up Get — the failed Insert lands no row). Three load-bearing rules: (1) anchor on BOTH version (trigger-bumped on every UPDATE so a missing WHERE bumps every row) AND updated_at (trigger-refreshed; same property) — either one catches a WHERE-less UPDATE independently of the other, keep both; (2) the "stale-If-Match" leg is structurally distinct from the "successful update" leg and exercises the conflict-detection path which historically falls back to extra reads/writes that could leak across tenants; (3) Insert conflict-message assertions anchor on yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty Message ONLY, never on a substring (the static "an organization with this slug already exists" message is a stable contract but story-specific prose checks are brittle). Tenant-root tables have NO ListByParent method at this layer (no parent to scope by) — the "list queries don't leak total counts" acceptance criterion is vacuously satisfied for tenant-root tables; the corresponding proof for /v1/me/organizations lives in membership tests. Document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test. CHILD-TABLE variant (BE-0428+ for users/memberships/projects/environments/services/api_keys/etc.) extends this skeleton with one additional leg: a cross-tenant parent_id probe — passing org A's parent_id (project_id, etc.) alongside org B's organization_id must render NotFound, NEVER A's row, NEVER a 500 leaking the cause. Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestXxxRepository' to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset, so CI without Postgres remains green. Known pre-existing -race-with-DB failures in environment-service / api-key-service Create paths and organization_variable_patch audit metadata are NOT introduced by this pattern (confirmed by stashing the new test file and re-running each failing case); a focused triage story should pick them up before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
- Operator-driven health probe (BE-0039 pattern, internal/controlplane/backup/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, health.go, health_test.go} + httpapi/routes.go GET /healthz/backup + docs/operations/backup-restore.md): canonical shape for ANY unauthenticated probe that surfaces operator-written state the Yalla process did not produce itself — database backup timestamp, WAL position, replication lag, external queue depth, cloud-bucket presence, certificate expiry, etc. Layout: a LEAF package under internal/controlplane that owns the parsing + redaction contract (Status struct + Reporter interface + a concrete FileReporter and an Unconfigured() helper); a single new entry in newRouteTable with tagOperations + a stable operationId (getHealthzBackup mirrors getHealthz/getReadyz/getVersion); a wire-DTO struct with omitempty pointers for every optional field so the four wire-shape variants (configured-and-healthy / configured-and-stale / configured-but-empty-sentinel / unconfigured) share one shape. Five load-bearing rules: (1) the leaf reads exactly one external resource — a path or URL the operator supplied via a YALLA_* env var; never reads the database, never invokes Dokploy, never logs the source content; (2) error messages from the leaf name the configured path or url but never echo the source's content (a status file accidentally seeded with a secret cannot leak through the leaf's parse-error string, pinned by a TestFileReporterParseErrorRedactsContent-style test); (3) the HTTP handler never echoes the reporter's error message in the wire response either — it forwards a stable handler-side message via apienvelope.WriteErrorStatus(yerr.CodeUnavailable, ...) and the redaction proof lives in a TestXxxErrorMessageDoesNotLeakReporterError test on the http side; (4) a sentinel "configured but no value yet" path (ErrNoBackupRecorded shape) renders 200 with configured=true + fresh=false + a human-readable detail string — a freshly provisioned environment is healthy, it just hasn't fired its first event yet; (5) an unconfigured Reporter renders 200 with configured=false (NOT 404) so operators can wire the integration later without restarting clients that already probe the route. The four BE-acceptance-criteria slots map onto the new endpoint as: success = source returns a value; validation failure = wrong HTTP method (router-driven 404/405; the BE-0039 "no input to validate" version of a validation test); authorization failure = explicit unauthenticated-with-and-without-bearer-header test that fails loudly if a future regression wraps the route in RequireAuth; not-found behavior = the sentinel path (configured but no value yet recorded). Config layer gains TWO env vars per probe — a YALLA_XXX_PATH/URL (string, optional, must be absolute when set) and a YALLA_XXX_MAX_AGE (Go duration, optional, ≥ 0) — with Validate rejecting a relative path and the RedactedConfig+LogValue+String chain surfacing the non-secret fields. NewHandler grows ONE positional port (Reporter backup.Reporter or similar) at the head of the operational-args block (after meta, before authenticator); a nil Reporter is rewritten to Unconfigured() inside NewHandler so every test path still serves the route. The positional-arg sweep across ~60 test files is dominated by three Python regexes anchored on the common test helpers (`nil, nil, a, policy.NewEngine()` literal-a, `nil, nil, AuthIdent{}, policy.NewEngine()` struct-literal-auth, multi-line `nil, nil, a,\n\t\tpolicy.NewEngine()`); the all-nils newRouteTable shape tests bump by one nil; the bootstrap-endpoints list test in server_test.go adds the new path. The runbook in docs/operations/ is part of the deliverable — it pins the SLO (frequency, retention, RTO/RPO, encryption), the operator-pipeline handshake (the exact file format + atomic-rename write contract), the four wire-shape variants the probe emits, and the rehearsal procedure for staging. Forward-applicable to every future operator-driven health probe (queue depth, replication lag, certificate expiry, cloud-bucket presence, archive freshness, license validity, KMS key health).
- Cross-cutting policy package = retention-shaped leaf (BE-0038 pattern, internal/controlplane/retention/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, policy.go, policy_test.go}, third instance of the pure-package layout after reconcile and migrateimport): canonical shape for ANY cross-cutting authority over a closed-set domain enum + a validated config + boundary-style predicates — retention windows, rate-limit tiers, idempotency horizons, audit-redaction policies, plan-limit ladders, etc. Layout: doc.go (boundary + the invariants the predicates encode), policy.go (closed-set ResourceKind + a Window struct of just-durations + an immutable Policy value with a private map; DefaultPolicy() returning the production values + NewPolicy(map) that validates every kind is present and every Window passes validate() + copies the input map so caller mutation cannot tamper with a live Policy + Window/Restorable/RestorableAt/RestoreDeadline/RemainingRestore/ReapableAt/ReapDeadline/AllowsSlugReuse predicates over (kind, stampedAt, now)), policy_test.go (every kind × every transition + boundary case + zero-value-Policy safety + NewPolicy validation table + caller-mutation defense + cross-predicate coherence (restorable → held → reapable monotone) + closed-set guard via AllKinds()), AGENTS.md (boundary + invariants). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the boundary comparisons MUST be strict-less-than at the upper bound of RestorableAt and `>=` at the lower bound of ReapableAt so the two predicates are mutually exclusive at the exact instant of transition (a row never reports as both restorable and reapable at the same nanosecond); (2) the package is a LEAF — it imports only std-lib (time, fmt, sort, errors-only-from-tests) and is imported by store/httpapi/worker, so its predicates are cheap to call from anywhere without dragging a database or network dependency along; never let retention import apierr/store/audit; (3) "soft delete where safe" in the BE-0038 description is a real qualifier — memberships do not get a soft-delete column because role_version invalidates sessions at the auth layer and audit_events outlives the resource via the organization-level cascade, so KindMembership.SoftDeleteColumn() returns "" and Policy.SupportsSoftDelete(KindMembership) is false by design (a future schema migration that adds a removed_at column to memberships must update both in lock-step, and the closed-set test will fail until they do). Two follow-up wirings the package was designed for but does not yet require: a future teardown worker (BE-0203+) iterates retention.AllKinds(), looks up each kind's SoftDeleteColumn(), scans rows where the column is non-NULL and the stamp is older than ReapAfter, and calls the kind-specific hard-delete repository method; a future CapAdmin admin-purge endpoint calls retention.Policy.AllowsSlugReuse before bypassing the wait — the slug-reuse predicate is the contract those two consumers will share. Forward-applicable to every future cross-cutting policy module (plan tiers, audit redaction horizons, idempotency-key TTL, rate-limit ramp).
- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
- POST sub-action endpoint on a deep resource by bare id (BE-0163 pattern, POST /v1/environments/{environment_id}/clone, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.Clone + CloneEnvironmentInput + validateCloneEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go cloneEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentCloner): canonical shape for ALL sub-action POSTs on a bare-id route (clone, restart, rollback, cancel, restore, etc.). The path carries the SOURCE id and a verb suffix, and the resolver pins only (OrganizationID, ID) — scoped grants pinning ProjectID are denied by covers() (one-way containment, the same boundary GET/PATCH/DELETE inherit on the bare-id route family). The structural parent_id of the new row (project_id for env.clone, etc.) is DERIVED FROM THE SOURCE ROW inside the same tx, NEVER from the body or path — the request body strictly omits the parent_id field, and the strict decoder rejects any caller who tries to supply one (proven by an explicit RejectsUnknownField contract test). The service-layer orchestrator composes Create-style steps PLUS a source-row read at the front PLUS a lifecycle-state guard before any write: read source under (orgID, sourceID) → reject DeletionScheduledAt != nil as Conflict (a lifecycle-dead row is not a valid source) → in-tx Authorize → Quota.Reserve → Repository.Insert (the new row inherits source.ProjectID) → JobEnqueue → Audit.Append with Metadata{"slug","project_id","cloned_from": source.ID} (the cloned_from / restored_from / rolled_back_to entry records non-secret structural provenance — the audit trail's only handle on "where did this row come from"). Reuse the EXISTING action constant rather than minting a sub-action one (environment.create on a clone route, not environment.clone): the PRD-mandated action is the same CapWrite, same role matrix, same grant containment, and a new action constant would force a sweep of the action catalog, builtinRoleCaps, every policy matrix test, and the OpenAPI surface for zero contract benefit. The HTTP handler reuses environmentIDResolver (the bare-id family's shared resolver), returns 201 Created (a Create-style sub-action), and mirrors row.Version into the ETag header via writeOrganizationETag. Service-level tests MUST seed with seedDomainOrg / seedDomainProject / seedDomainEnvironment helpers (canonical 26-char-suffix IDs via domain.MustNewID — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical and the service's strict ParseID validation correctly rejects them); repository-level tests can use either helper. NewHandler+newRouteTable each grow ONE positional port (environmentCloner EnvironmentCloner at the tail of the env-ports group, after environmentDeleter); the three-pattern sweep across ~38 test files anchors on `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{}, nil)`, plus the `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})` newRouteTable form → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{})`, plus the all-nils OpenAPI-shape tests bumped from N to N+1 nils. cmd/yalla-api/main.go threads environmentService as the cloner — the single concrete *EnvironmentService satisfies Creator/Reader/Updater/Deleter/Cloner together.
- PATCH endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0157 pattern, PATCH /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.Update + classifyEnvironmentConcurrencyMiss, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.Update + buildEnvironmentUpdate, httpapi/environments.go updateEnvironmentHandler): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource PATCH endpoints. Repository.Update takes a fully-formed row + *int64 ifMatchVersion, runs a tenant-scoped (organization_id, id) UPDATE that touches only the mutable columns (slug, display_name — NEVER the composite tenant key), and classifies a zero-row return via a classify*ConcurrencyMiss helper that disambiguates gone-vs-stale by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator runs the validation BEFORE the transaction is opened (buildXxxUpdate is split out for unit-testability, rejects empty patches as 400, never echoes submitted values in violations), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale with row's authoritative version BEFORE the version-checked UPDATE — even a trivial no-op patch with stale If-Match surfaces the conflict), apply patch in-memory, call Repository.Update, append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the update path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses the shared parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag helpers (yes, the helper is org-named but it is the SHARED chokepoint for every endpoint mirroring versions to ETag — do not duplicate it per resource family). Body is strictly decoded with `slug *string` and `display_name *string` (pointer nil-ness IS the partial-update contract); the body intentionally exposes no organization_id/parent_id/self_id field — tenant isolation is structural. Response is yalla.output.v1 carrying the same wire shape GET returns, plus ETag header. Path with NO parent_id (bare-id route): the resource scope pins only OrganizationID + the deep id, so the engine's covers() rule DENIES every scoped grant at the policy boundary (a grant with a pinned ProjectID cannot cover a resource with no ProjectID); principals whose only access is a scoped grant must use a parent-scoped route. Every NewHandler positional-arg addition triggers a ~50-site sweep across ~35 existing test files — anchor on `fakeXxxReader{}, nil)` and `fakeXxxReader{}, logger)` plus the `reader, nil)` / `reader, logger)` outliers in the resource's own get/policy test helpers. The newRouteTable-only OpenAPI shape tests (all-nils form, partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form) each need one more `nil` appended. AVOID double-counting: run go vet AFTER each automated sweep and undo overshoots before continuing.
- DELETE endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0160 pattern, DELETE /v1/environments/{environment_id}, migration 0016_environment_deletion_schedule, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.ScheduleDeletion, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.ScheduleDeletion + DeleteEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go deleteEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentDeleter): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource SOFT-delete endpoints. The migration is structurally identical to 0013_project_deletion_schedule — `ALTER TABLE <resource> ADD COLUMN deletion_scheduled_at timestamptz` + a partial index `WHERE deletion_scheduled_at IS NOT NULL` for the future teardown-worker scan. The repository ScheduleDeletion is an SQL-IDEMPOTENT unconditional UPDATE stamping `deletion_scheduled_at = now()` returning the row in column order (so callers see the trigger-refreshed updated_at + bumped version); the already-scheduled distinction is the service's job (the repository must remain SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers — workers, admin jobs — that retry). ifMatchVersion uses the SAME pattern as Update — nil disables, non-nil adds `AND version = $N` to the WHERE — and classify*ConcurrencyMiss disambiguates the no-rows case (gone vs stale) by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator validates blank-input BEFORE opening the tx (typed InvalidInput, missing actor org is Internal — wiring error, never client input), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale BEFORE the already-scheduled check, so a stale caller learns version-stale instead of an already-scheduled message that races with concurrent edits they did not see), reject already-scheduled as apierr.Conflict (rolls the whole tx back so the audit record can never name a deletion that did not change resource state), call Repository.ScheduleDeletion, record the resolved deletion_scheduled_at stamp as audit metadata (`event.Metadata["deletion_scheduled_at"] = row.DeletionScheduledAt.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)` — a non-secret timestamp, safe to record verbatim), append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the delete path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag + telemetry.FromContext for correlation; returns 202 Accepted (the scheduling is durable but the destructive teardown is a later worker job). The wire DTO (projectEnvironment / projectResource) gains a `DeletionScheduledAt *string \`json:"deletion_scheduled_at,omitempty"\`` field so a live row's JSON shape is byte-identical to before (omitempty + nil pointer = field absent), while a scheduled row exposes the stamp as RFC 3339 — mirror this on every resource DTO that gains a soft-delete column. Routes.go registration uses `Method: http.MethodDelete`, `SuccessStatus: http.StatusAccepted`, the same `environmentIDResolver` as GET/PATCH (the bare-id three routes share one resolver), and the same description shape as `deleteProject`'s scheduled-not-immediate / version-pre-check / one-tx-audit clauses. NewHandler+newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (`environmentDeleter EnvironmentDeleter` at the very tail of the env ports group, after `environmentUpdater`); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, nil)` -> `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` and `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, logger)` (37 NewHandler call sites across 37 files) handled the bulk; the named-updater outlier `fakeEnvironmentReader{}, updater, nil)` in the resource's own update test got `..., updater, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)`; the all-nil newRouteTable form (7 files) received one trailing nil; the partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form (projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go ending `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil)`) needed one MORE trailing nil; the multi-line newRouteTable-with-explicit-fakes form (3 files ending `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{})`) became `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})`. Run `go vet ./...` AFTER each sweep — Go's positional-arg compile error tells you exactly which call sites the regex missed. The store-side integration tests cover repo happy-path + NotFound + tenant-scoped + nil-tx-rejection plus service happy-path-with-audit + already-scheduled-rolls-back-AND-stamp-unchanged-AND-only-one-audit-row + NotFound-no-audit + tenant-scoped-no-audit + stale-If-Match + blank-input + missing-actor-org. The httpapi contract tests cover happy-path-forwards-(principal,path,if-match)+202+ETag+wire-DTO + named-If-Match forwarded + malformed-If-Match 400 + NotFound 404 + already-scheduled 409 + stale-If-Match 409 + missing-deleter 500 + unauthenticated 401 + OpenAPI route registered. Forward-applicable to every future bare-id soft-delete endpoint (service.delete on /v1/services/{service_id}, etc.).
- New top-level resource family that already has its persistence table from an earlier migration (BE-0148 pattern, environments table from 0002 + version column from 0011, store/environment.go, httpapi/project_environments.go): a reader-only scoped-child vertical lands JUST the GET endpoint and pairs with the existing table — NO new migration. The vertical is structurally identical to BE-0142 (project_variables reader) with one narrowing worth pinning: when the table already exists (environments was created by 0002_tenant_hierarchy.up.sql at the same time as projects/services, and gained its version + bump_version trigger in 0011_optimistic_versions.up.sql), the story collapses into store layer + http layer + wiring + tests — no migration to declare composite FKs, scope-uniqueness indexes, or triggers. The store layer (Environment struct, EnvironmentRepository.ListByProject, EnvironmentReader.ListProjectEnvironments) mirrors ProjectVariableRepository/ProjectVariableReader exactly — every read is tenant-scoped at the SQL predicate, the reader Gets the project first inside the same Store.Read tx so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound (never an empty list), and the repository has its own list cap (environmentListMaxRows = 500). Crucially, environments carry NO credential material (the table stores only id, organization_id, project_id, slug, display_name, version, created_at, updated_at) so the HTTP projection (projectEnvironmentOf) needs no value-redaction chokepoint — every field is structural — but the projection STILL exists as a single chokepoint so a future column added to store.Environment is reviewed for its wire exposure here rather than leaking by default. The integration tests (store/environment_test.go) prove ordering, tenant scoping (a cross-tenant (orgA, projB_id) pair yields no rows), the empty-project shape (non-nil empty slice), the reader's project existence check, and that a cross-tenant id surfaces as apierr.NotFound WITHOUT leaking any of the foreign tenant's environment id / project id / slug into the error message. The HTTP layer adds a new operation tag (tagEnvironments = "environments") because environments will get their own /v1/environments/{environment_id} surface later; the list endpoint is tagged [tagProjects, tagEnvironments] mirroring how project_variables uses [tagProjects, tagVariables]. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectEnvironments ProjectEnvironmentReader at the TAIL of the arg list, after projectVariableReplacer); cmd/yalla-api/main.go wires *store.EnvironmentReader at startup. A Python regex sweep over 33 test files anchored on `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{}, nil)` (44 sites) plus a second sweep on `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, logger)` (server_test.go + me_contract_test.go) plus the named-replacer outlier `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, replacer, nil)` -> `..., replacer, fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{}, nil)` (project_variables_test.go's replaceProjectVariablesHandlerFor) plus the openapi-only newRouteTable form `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, fakeProjectVariableReplacer{})` -> `..., fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{})` (project_variables_test.go's BE-0142 OpenAPI test) handled the NewHandler/newRouteTable churn (54 sites total). The all-nil newRouteTable form with 32 nils received one trailing nil to become 33 (6 sites). projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go newRouteTable calls ended `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, nil)` and got one MORE trailing nil to become `nil, nil, nil, nil)`. The KindEnvironment domain Kind ("env") already existed and is in the valid-kinds map from earlier work; no domain/id.go changes were needed. Forward-applicable to every future reader-only vertical on a hierarchy resource whose table predates the story.
- env.read-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0144, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_variables_get_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the project.grants.read-on-{project_id}-resolver matrix (BE-0138, projects_grants_policy_test.go) — same CapRead capability class, same projectIDResolver scope (`{principal home org, path project_id}`), same six-roles-all-allow matrix via ReasonAllowedByRole, same support cross-tenant exception at the engine (ReasonAllowedBySupport — pinned even though projectIDResolver structurally blocks it on the customer-facing wire), same engine cross-tenant deny matrix for non-support roles, same scoped-grant containment shape (sibling project denied, env-/service-scoped grants denied against the project resource, org-level Viewer grant allowed via ReasonAllowedByGrant). The load-bearing distinction is the wire-redaction chokepoint: project_variables rows carry credential material (a non-null Value that flips to output.Sentinel when IsSecret), so the deny-body leak guard (variablesDenyBodyLeak) anchors on BOTH structural identifiers (canonical project/var ids, keys, non-secret values) AND the seeded secret plaintext (canonicalVariablesSecretPlaintext). project_grants rows store no credential material, so grantsDenyBodyLeak omits a plaintext anchor. The allow-path test also asserts secret rows project as output.Sentinel and that the seeded plaintext never appears in the response body — pinning projectVariableOf as the chokepoint independent of how authority was earned (role / org-level grant / project-level grant). Forward-applicable to every future env.read matrix that resolves into a project-, env-, or service-level scope on a variables route: keep the role / disabled / wrong-org / grant-containment skeleton, swap the action+resolver for the new endpoint's, and ALWAYS add a secret-plaintext needle to the leak guard plus a Sentinel-projection assertion to every allow-path tie-in.
- environment.read-on-bare-{environment_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0156, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environments_get_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the {project_id}-resolver siblings (BE-0144 env.read on the variables route, BE-0150 environment.read on the parent-scoped route) for endpoints that use a BARE-ID resolver (environmentIDResolver, future serviceIDResolver / deploymentIDResolver). TWO load-bearing distinctions from the projectIDResolver siblings. (1) The resolver pins NO ProjectID leg on the resource scope (`policy.Scope{OrganizationID: principal.home, EnvironmentID: r.PathValue("environment_id")}`), so the engine's one-way Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) denies EVERY scoped grant at the boundary via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope — project-scoped, env-scoped, AND service-scoped grants all DENY on this route, even when the grant names the target environment's own id or the target environment's own parent project, because the grant scope pins ProjectID and the resource scope does not. The acceptance criteria's three containment properties (sibling project not reached / staging-grant-doesn't-imply-production / service-grant-shields-parent-env-secrets) are still pinned at the engine against their natural resources (project / env / service Resource), but the wire tie-in INVERTS the projectIDResolver shape: instead of "project-scoped grant on target ALLOWS, sibling DENIES," every scoped-key type DENIES with ReasonDeniedOutOfScope on this route and only org-wide roles + the org-level grant allow. The org-level Viewer grant remains the load-bearing ALLOW because its grant scope pins nothing past OrganizationID — covers() matches a resource without ProjectID. Pin a "grant on the SAME environment id as the path names" engine-level deny too, so a future relaxation of covers() (e.g. admitting `Scope{EnvironmentID: env_X}` to cover `Resource{Scope{EnvironmentID: env_X}}` regardless of ProjectID) cannot land without failing this test. (2) The support cross-tenant exception is suppressed on the wire by construction — environmentIDResolver pins OrganizationID to principal.home — so the support cross-tenant ALLOW only fires at the engine layer against a hand-built foreign-org Resource. The wire-level "wrong organization" probe surfaces as 404 E_NOT_FOUND from the tenant-scoped GetByID query (never 403, which would confirm existence), and the body must not echo the foreign org id. The contract-test helper bakes in a single canonical principal (getEnvironmentHandlerFor takes `t, reader` only, with authForEnvGet's hardcoded principal), so this matrix introduces a per-test identity-injected helper LOCAL to the policy file (getEnvironmentByIDHandlerForIdentity, signature `(id auth.Identity, authErr error, reader EnvironmentReader) http.Handler`) modelled on listProjectsHandlerFor / listProjectEnvironmentsHandlerFor — adding the helper locally avoids modifying the 9 contract tests that already use the simpler form. ACTION-CONSTANT gotcha to pin: routes.go:574 sets `RequiredAction: string(policy.ActionEnvironmentRead)` for this route, NOT `policy.ActionEnvRead` — both are CapRead per catalog.go but they're different catalog entries (env-vars vs env entity); mixing them up silently swaps which catalog row a future tier-upgrade watches. Forward-applicable to every future bare-id matrix on a deep resource (service.read on /v1/services/{service_id}, deployment.read on /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}, etc).
- environment.read-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0150, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_environments_get_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the env.read matrix at the same resolver (BE-0144) with TWO narrowings worth pinning because the persistence row carries NO credential material: (1) drop the secret-plaintext deny-body leak anchor — environmentsDenyBodyLeak anchors ONLY on canonical structural identifiers (project id/slug/display_name + per-env id/slug/display_name) because the environments table has nothing that needs redaction; (2) drop the "secret row still projects as output.Sentinel even when authority came via a project-level or org-level grant" allow-path assertion — projectEnvironmentOf has no value-redaction chokepoint to anchor. Everything else stays one-for-one identical: same six-roles-all-allow ReasonAllowedByRole role matrix, same Disabled-principal 403 with ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled, same wire-level cross-tenant test (reader always called with principal home org id, the persistence layer rejects the cross-tenant project_id pair as 404 NotFound), same engine-level cross-tenant deny matrix for every non-support role + ReasonAllowedBySupport for support (CapRead inside the support cross-tenant exception clause), same four-wire-tie-in grant-containment shape (project-scoped Viewer on target ALLOWS, sibling project DENIES, env-scoped grant DENIES against parent project, service-scoped grant DENIES against parent project, org-level Viewer grant ALLOWS, scoped key planted in foreign home org DENIES at engine). CRITICAL action-constant gotcha: env.read variables and environment.read entities live in the same Org→Project→Env→Service hierarchy but use DIFFERENT action constants (policy.ActionEnvRead = "env.read" for env-vars vs policy.ActionEnvironmentRead = "environment.read" for the env entity); both are CapRead and both go through projectIDResolver on the project-scoped reader routes, but mixing them up silently swaps which catalog row a future tier-upgrade regression watches. Always grep the route registration in routes.go AND the existing contract test's OpenAPI assertion to confirm which action a route is gated on before writing the policy matrix. Forward-applicable to every future CapRead-on-{X}-resolver matrix where the underlying row carries no credential material (org_grants, members, audit_events, etc.): keep the four-test skeleton, swap action+resource Kind, and drop the secret-plaintext anchor + Sentinel-projection assertion.
- CapAdmin-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0141, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_grants_put_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the CapWrite-on-{project_id}-resolver matrix (projects_delete_policy_test.go) but ONE TIER UP — Developer JOINS the deny set at the role boundary (it holds CapWrite, not CapAdmin) and the org-level Developer grant flips from ALLOW for project.delete to DENY for project.grants.write. The load-bearing pair is org-level Admin grant ALLOW + org-level Developer grant DENY: that pair locks the CapAdmin requirement against the grant path so a future catalog change that downgraded project.grants.write to CapWrite would still pass the allow check but fail the deny check (silently letting every developer key rewrite a project's authority), and the regression direction is documented as a structural test, not only catalog assertion. The cross-tenant engine matrix DENIES every role including Support (CapAdmin is OUTSIDE the engine clause `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)`); the CapRead-on-{project_id}-resolver siblings (project.grants.read at BE-0138 etc) ALLOW Support cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport — pinning the same-shape distinction in both directions documents that read-vs-write is the load-bearing axis for the support exception. Mutating-policy-matrix tests carry TWO directions of leak guard: a request-body leak guard (replaceGrantsRequestBodyLeak, anchored on distinctive caller-supplied identifiers like usr_request_matrix that NEVER appear in the canonical fixture data) and a would-have-returned-data leak guard (grantsDenyBodyLeak, anchored on canonical fixture data the fake replacer would return on an allow path) — used together on every deny path so a future renderer that gained either a "render request body on error" or "render would-have-returned data on 403" mode fails here. A read endpoint needs only the second; a write endpoint needs both. CI must be listed EXPLICITLY in both the per-role deny rows and the cross-tenant engine deny loop, because CapDeploy (which CI holds) is its own axis — neither CapWrite nor CapAdmin. The "scoped key planted in a foreign home org" engine assertion (a principal whose home org is org_sibling but whose grant scope still names a project in org_acme, authorized against org_sibling's resource) is independent of CapAdmin/CapWrite/CapRead — the engine refuses to consider the grant at all because the principal's OrganizationID no longer matches the grant scope's OrganizationID. Pin this on every grant-containment test so a stolen scoped key documentedly cannot be smuggled across tenants by anyone who finds it.
- env.write-on-{project_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0147, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_variables_put_policy_test.go) is the writer-side structural sibling of the env.read matrix at the same resolver (BE-0144, projects_variables_get_policy_test.go) and the project-scope sibling of the org-level env.write matrix (BE-0111, variables_put_policy_test.go). Same CapWrite capability class as project.delete/project.update (CapAdmin lives one tier up at project.grants.write/BE-0141), so the role table is identical to the org-level env.write: owner/admin/developer ALLOW via ReasonAllowedByRole, viewer/ci/support DENY via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — CI denies despite holding CapDeploy because env.write is CapWrite, not CapDeploy (CapDeploy is its own axis). Cross-tenant engine matrix DENIES every role INCLUDING Support (CapWrite is OUTSIDE the `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)` exception, same posture as CapAdmin and the load-bearing distinction from env.read where support IS allowed cross-tenant). Wire-level cross-tenant story is the projectIDResolver shape: the resource org is the principal's home org, never the path's tenant, so the engine admits the same-tenant resource and the persistence layer rejects the (attacker_home_org, foreign_project_id) pair as 404 NotFound — the replacer DOES run (callCount=1) but with the attacker's home org id, so a production store could not have replaced the victim's variables regardless. Grant containment proves the org-level Developer ALLOW + org-level Viewer DENY pair (locks CapWrite against the grant path against both directions of catalog drift); project-level Viewer grant on target project ALSO DENIES (Viewer is CapRead-only); project-level Developer grant on target project ALLOWS, sibling project DENIES, env-/service-scoped Admin grants DENY against the parent project (covers() one-way). Variables-flavoured leak guards carry the same TWO directions that BE-0141 introduced — a request-body leak guard (anchored on caller-supplied MATRIX_KEY/matrix_value that never appear in the canonical fixture) AND a would-have-returned-data leak guard (postWriteProjectVariablesBodyLeak, anchored on the seeded post-write rows: per-row ids, keys, the non-secret value AND every recognisable fragment of the seeded secret plaintext post-write-plaintext-correcthorsebatterystaple). The allow-path Sentinel-projection assertion is the chokepoint anchor: even right after a customer submitted a secret, the post-write re-read STILL redacts via projectVariableOf -> output.Sentinel, so PUT cannot leak a secret value the customer just submitted. Forward-applicable to every future CapWrite-on-{X}-resolver matrix at any scope — keep the role / disabled / wrong-org / grant-containment skeleton + dual-direction leak guard, swap the action+resolver target+resource Kind, and ALWAYS add a secret-plaintext needle to the post-write leak guard plus a Sentinel-projection assertion on every allow-path tie-in.
- Replace-grants writer vertical (BE-0139 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant.go + httpapi/project_grants.go): a PUT-replace endpoint on a (parent, scoped-children) resource family pairs the existing reader-port adapter with a unit-of-work writer (ProjectGrantService) modeled exactly on OrganizationVariableService. The repository grows two new methods: Upsert(tx, id, organizationID, projectID, principalID, principalKind, role, *envID, *svcID) (ProjectGrant, error) using `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, project_id, principal_id, COALESCE(environment_id, ''), COALESCE(service_id, ''))` so the customer-mutable `role` is the only column the UPDATE branch touches (the immutable scope tuple + the bump_version trigger handle the rest); DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(tx, organizationID, projectID, keepIDs) renders the empty-keepIDs case as an unconstrained DELETE (an explicit clear is a meaningful customer operation), the non-empty case as `NOT (id = ANY($3::text[]))` so the bulk delete-by-exclusion is one round trip — same shape as DeleteByOrganizationExceptKeys but anchored on the row id (which Upsert returns from RETURNING) rather than a customer-supplied key, so an upsert that mutates an existing row deterministically keeps the survivor's id. The service Replace runs inside one Store.Write tx: project existence Get (a missing parent is typed NotFound, never a generic FK conflict), per-grant Upsert collecting the persisted ids into keepIDs, DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(keepIDs), audit.Append, post-write ListByProject re-read — every step shares the *Tx so a partial replace and an orphaned audit row are both impossible. Validation lives in buildProjectGrantReplace (split out, unit-testable without a DB): closed-set checks on principal_kind / role mirror the table's CHECK constraints, structural checks reject blank/NUL ids and a service-scope-without-environment, in-memory dedup uses `principalID + "\x00" + COALESCE(envID,'') + "\x00" + COALESCE(svcID,'')` to match the SQL unique index byte-for-byte. Audit metadata records counts only — `grant_count`, `project_scope_count`, `environment_scope_count`, `service_scope_count` — never principal ids, role names, or environment/service identifiers; per-row mutation history is the GET /v1/projects/{project_id}/grants surface's job. The HTTP layer adds a ProjectGrantReplacer port mirroring OrganizationVariableReplacer, a strict-decoded replaceProjectGrantsRequest with Grants *[]projectGrantRequest (pointer-to-slice so missing field is structurally distinguishable from an explicit empty array — a missing field is a stable 400, an empty array reaches the store), and the handler clones caller-supplied *string EnvironmentID/ServiceID pointers before forwarding them so the store cannot observe the caller's backing memory. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectGrantReplacer ProjectGrantReplacer just after projectGrants ProjectGrantReader); a Python regex sweep over 27 test files anchored on `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, nil)` handled the bulk of the NewHandler churn (43 call sites), with three follow-up patterns hand-fixed: `, logger)` in server_test.go and me_contract_test.go (NewHandler with non-nil logger), `, nil)` newRouteTable call (different return type), and the 29-nil all-nil newRouteTable pattern in six test files (added one trailing nil for the new port). The story implicitly delivers BE-0439's CRUD repository acceptance criteria (Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptIDs are the missing half) and the writer side of BE-0440's tenant-isolation criteria.
- Replace-variables writer vertical for a scoped-child resource (BE-0145 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_variable.go + project_variable_service.go + httpapi/project_variables.go): a PUT-replace endpoint on a (parent, scoped-children) variables family pairs the existing reader-port adapter with a unit-of-work writer (ProjectVariableService) modeled exactly on OrganizationVariableService — same Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptKeys + Replace shape, same audit metadata convention (counts-only: `variable_count`, `secret_count`), same value-redaction chokepoints (LogValue + wire output.Sentinel + audit Auditor regex), but with two distinguishing axes worth pinning. (1) The repository's Upsert key is the (organization_id, project_id, key) triple from migration 0015's UNIQUE (not the (organization_id, key) pair from migration 0012), so the ON CONFLICT target carries an extra column — every other column position in the INSERT statement shifts by one. The DELETE-except-keys SQL similarly has two tenant predicate legs (organization_id AND project_id) before the `key <> ALL($3)` clause; the empty/nil keepKeys path is the deliberate full-clear (a customer asking to remove every project-scoped variable is meaningful, not a no-op). (2) The ProjectVariableService re-uses the org-level helpers verbatim — `itoaVariableIndex`, `posixEnvVarName`, validate.MaxEnvVarNameLen/MaxEnvVarValueLen/MaxSecretValueLen — and uses `projectVariablesWriteAction = "env.write"` (same string constant as the org-level path) so the audit Action is identical at both scopes; the resource_kind axis (domain.KindProject vs domain.KindOrganization) is what disambiguates the audit row. The Service runs inside one Store.Write tx: ProjectRepository.Get (a missing OR cross-tenant project surfaces as the typed NotFound the GET reader already proves, NEVER as a generic FK conflict from the upsert), per-variable Upsert minting a fresh `pvar_` id (the existing-row's id is preserved on conflict, NOT the caller-supplied id), DeleteByProjectExceptKeys(keepKeys), audit.Append, post-write ListByProject re-read so the response body byte-matches the committed state. The HTTP layer adds a ProjectVariableReplacer port mirroring OrganizationVariableReplacer, strict-decoded replaceProjectVariablesRequest with `Variables *[]projectVariableRequest` (pointer-to-slice so a missing field is structurally distinguishable from an explicit empty array — a missing field is a stable 400 with reason `errProjectVariablesBodyMissing` "must be supplied (use an empty array to clear every variable)", an empty array reaches the store as the deliberate clear). The handler takes OrganizationID from the principal's home org (NEVER the caller — the body carries no org id), so a cross-tenant {project_id} still hits the tenant-scoped Get and surfaces as 404 at the persistence boundary. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectVariableReplacer ProjectVariableReplacer just after projectVariables ProjectVariableReader); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, nil)` and `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, logger)` (multi-line variant included) handled the bulk of the NewHandler churn (31 files); the all-nil newRouteTable form with 31 nils received one trailing nil to become 32; two outlier patterns needed targeted edits — project_variables_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, reader, nil)` (where `reader` is a named parameter, not a literal) and projects_delete_test.go / projects_restore_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil)` (where the new-port slot was already nil-filled by an earlier story, so one MORE trailing nil was added rather than inserting in the middle). The story implicitly delivers the writer half of project_variables CRUD repository acceptance criteria and the writer side of tenant-isolation criteria.
- PUT-replace-variables contract suite on a bare-{id}-resolver route (BE-0175 contract pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_variables_put_test.go): canonical 12-test contract suite for ANY customer-facing PUT-replace-{secret-bearing-children} endpoint sitting on a bare-{id}-resolver route. Structural HYBRID of (a) environment_grants_put_test.go (BE-0169 contract pattern — route-shape twin: same env-id resolver, same NewHandler positional port placement, same missing-field-400 + malformed-400 + unknown-field-400 + unauth-401 + viewer-403 + store NotFound 404 + store InvalidInput 400 + store unavailable 503 + unwired 500 + OpenAPI registration skeleton) and (b) project_variables_test.go (BE-0142 GET pattern — resource-shape twin: output.Sentinel projection of is_secret rows, secret-plaintext leak guards on response bodies including the seeded substring). The pattern adds TWO load-bearing assertions BE-0169 (a grants-only resource) cannot supply: (1) the happy-path body POSITIVELY asserts got0.Value == output.Sentinel for the secret row AND negatively asserts the body does not contain the seeded plaintext — the wire chokepoint must fire on the post-write re-read even though the caller just submitted the plaintext; both directions catch different regressions. (2) A dedicated TestDeniesSupportInTenant — CapWrite has no support exception (the CapRead cross-tenant exception is one-way), so a CapWrite contract suite MUST positively assert support in their own tenant is denied 403, locking the deny against a future engine regression that widens CapWrite to support. apierr.InvalidInput wire shape: the renderer emits FieldViolation as "<field>: <reason>" inside the hint, NOT as a JSON key with quotes — a contract test that asserts the field path reaches the wire must use the substring "variables: " (with space), NOT "variables" wrapped in double-quotes; the success envelope DOES use "variables" as a JSON key but the error envelope serialises the field path into a human-readable hint instead. The handlerFor uses the trailing replacer port (everything else is fakeXxx{} zero-value); the all-nils logger is nil to silence test output. The future PUT-replace-{secrets} story on api_keys, service_variables, or any other credential-bearing child resource is a verbatim clone of this 12-test skeleton with the resource type swapped and the action const swapped.
- NewHandler/newRouteTable positional-port arity sweep — ALL FORMS rule (BE-0175 sweep pattern): each port-arity bump must cover FOUR distinct forms; missing one leaves a compile-blocker that only surfaces under go vet. The four forms in this repo: (a) the literal fully-typed helper anchor like `fakeEnvironmentGrantReplacer{}, reader, nil)` where the trailing nil is the logger and a new port is inserted between reader and logger — the most common form and easiest to bulk-edit; (b) the all-nils form `newRouteTable(runtime.BuildInfo{}, nil x N)` which has to grow from N to N+1 nils on every wave — multi-line, single-line, parallel sub-tests, etc.; (c) the build-variant form `newRouteTable(build, nil x N)` — uses `build` instead of `runtime.BuildInfo{}` and trips a different anchor; (d) the partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form like `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, ..., nil)` where the trailing nil run has to grow by one. Pre-flight invariant: after every port-arity bump, run `go vet ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...` BEFORE committing — vet surfaces every short-arity call site in one pass and is the only authoritative way to find all four forms. Do NOT commit on the assumption that the dominant-pattern Node walker caught everything; vet is the authority. Particular failure mode pinned by BE-0175: a test helper authored in an earlier wave (BE-0172, when EnvironmentVariableReader was the LAST port) ended with `reader, nil)` where the nil was the logger; the BE-0175 wave inserted the new Replacer port between the reader and the logger, but a helper that pre-dates the new port can't be caught by a sweep anchored on the new port's fake — only `go vet` catches it.
- P=1-policy-matrix-on-missing-P=2-endpoint picker rule (BE-0175 vs BE-0177): when a P=1 policy-matrix story names an endpoint whose contract surface (handler / replacer / OpenAPI registration / port wiring / test fakes) is not fully in place, the policy matrix CANNOT be implemented end-to-end — the test file would reference a non-existent handler builder / request builder. Historical precedent (BE-0172 P=2 endpoint → BE-0174 P=1 matrix, BE-0175 P=2 endpoint → BE-0177 P=1 matrix) shows the picker resolved this by selecting the P=2 endpoint story first even though a P=1 story was technically "highest priority". The rule generalises: the highest-priority pending story that CAN be implemented end-to-end (i.e. whose endpoint contract surface is either already on disk or part of THIS story) is the next story; a P=1 matrix story whose endpoint is missing is BLOCKED on its P=2 endpoint sibling and must wait one iteration.
- Reader-only scoped-child vertical (BE-0142 pattern, project_variables migration 0015, store/project_variable.go, httpapi/project_variables.go): a new (parent, scoped-children) resource family that ships JUST the GET endpoint mirrors the BE-0136 full-vertical shape with two narrowings worth pinning. (1) The migration is structurally identical to project_grants 0014 — composite (organization_id, project_id) FK -> projects with ON DELETE CASCADE, composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target a future override table (environment_variables / service_variables) can use, bigint version + bump_version trigger from 0011, set_updated_at trigger — but a different scope-uniqueness shape: project_variables uses UNIQUE (organization_id, project_id, key) directly (no COALESCE because key is non-nullable) where project_grants needs the COALESCE-NULLS index for env/svc-scoped uniqueness. (2) The reader vertical defers the writer's repository surface — only ListByProject(ctx, Querier, organizationID, projectID) lands now — so the repo file is short and the audit-grade write path (Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptKeys + bulk validation) is BE-0145's job. The store-backed adapter (ProjectVariableReader) mirrors ProjectGrantReader exactly: composes ProjectRepository.Get + ListByProject inside one Store.Read transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound, never an empty list. ProjectVariable.LogValue redacts every value at the slog boundary (mirrors OrganizationVariable.LogValue), so a stray panic stack that captures the struct cannot leak a non-secret value either — the slog redaction is unconditional even though the wire layer only redacts secret values. NewHandler signature grows ONE positional port (projectVariables ProjectVariableReader after projectGrantReplacer); a JS regex sweep over 30 test files anchored on `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, fakeProjectVariableReader{}, nil)` plus a second sweep on `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, logger)` handled the NewHandler churn (45 call sites); a separate state-machine sweep over newRouteTable calls appended one trailing arg to every direct invocation (8 files), and project_grants_put_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, replacer, nil)` (where replacer is a real var, not a literal) was the one anchor the bulk regex couldn't reach. The KindProjectVariable Kind prefix is `pvar` (mirrors the ovar/pgrnt prefix shape); the existing id_test.go's hardcoded valid-kinds lists do NOT need updating — they sanity-check a subset (KindProjectGrant is also absent from those lists yet works in tests). KindProjectVariable still has to land in the kinds map for Kind.Valid() / ParseID to accept it.
- Inverse-lifecycle endpoints (restore vs schedule-delete) reuse the same Repository+Service pattern: the repository is an SQL-idempotent UPDATE returning the row (with optional version WHERE — NotFound on no-rows + nil ifMatch, classifyProjectConcurrencyMiss on no-rows + non-nil), and the Service does the lifecycle-state precondition check inside the same Store.Write transaction. For restore: pre-check current.DeletionScheduledAt != nil (else apierr.Conflict "project is not scheduled for deletion"); for schedule-delete: pre-check ==nil (else "deletion is already scheduled"). The pre-check is the customer's truth-finding step — the repository MUST stay SQL-idempotent so workers/admin jobs can retry without surfacing a misleading Conflict. Audit metadata captures the prior state (for restore, current.DeletionScheduledAt read BEFORE the UPDATE; for schedule-delete, the new stamp from the persisted row) so the trail documents what the resource looked like before/after the mutation.
- Backend code lives under internal/controlplane/<pkg>; CLI stays under internal/cli. Keep HTTP handlers thin.
- Shared primitives are reused, not reinvented: typed errors come from internal/errors (yerr.New/Newf, CodeConfig, etc.); the redaction sentinel and Redactor come from internal/output.
- API success envelopes use yalla.output.v1; API errors use yalla.error.v1. ALL HTTP responses render through internal/controlplane/apienvelope (WriteData/WriteError/WriteErrorStatus); envelope structs are unexported so handlers cannot hand-roll JSON. HTTP status derives from the error Code via apienvelope.StatusForCode (WriteErrorStatus is the explicit-override escape hatch, e.g. /readyz 503). Error envelopes carry documentation_url; apienvelope is also a regex-based redaction backstop for message/hint/warnings.
- Config is env-only for backend processes (no flags/files): internal/controlplane/config.Load(LookupFunc) layers env vars over per-profile defaults, then validates. cmd/yalla-api and cmd/yalla-worker call config.LoadFromEnv() once at startup and os.Exit(1) on error.
- Every config/validation failure is a typed yerr CodeConfig error and must NEVER echo a secret value. Tests assert secrets do not appear in error strings, Redacted(), LogValue(), or String().
- Secret-bearing structs implement slog.LogValuer + fmt.Stringer with redacted views as defence-in-depth.
- Tests inject dependencies (e.g. config.MapLookup) instead of touching real process env/filesystem. Use t.Parallel().
- Quality gate before commit: gofmt -w ., go mod tidy, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test ./..., go test -race ./....
- Mutating POST endpoints reject organization_id in the request body via the strict JSON decoder (validate.DecodeJSON disallows unknown fields). The handler builds the store *Input.OrganizationID from p.OrganizationID (principal), never from the body, so the tenant boundary is structural — a caller cannot point a create at another tenant even if defense-in-depth were skipped. ActorOrgID on the audit record is also the principal's home org; ResourceID names the created resource.
- store.ProjectService is the canonical write-port unit-of-work with the full 5-step composition: authorize, reserve quota, INSERT, enqueue provisioning job, append audit — all in one Store.Write transaction. cmd/yalla-api wires placeholder alwaysAllowAuthorizer/noopQuotaReserver/noopJobEnqueuer adapters today (HTTP RequireAuth is the authoritative gate; real adapters land with the plan-resolver and provisioning-worker stories). store.NewProjectService rejects nil dependencies at construction, so placeholders must remain explicit-named values, never nil.
- Partial-update (PATCH) endpoints: the request DTO and store *Input use *string fields — nil means "field omitted, leave unchanged". The store builds+validates the patch BEFORE opening the transaction (rejecting a patch that names no field as a 400), then inside Store.Write reads the current row, applies the non-nil fields, and Updates. A repository Update using UPDATE ... RETURNING must check errors.Is(err, pgx.ErrNoRows) for the missing-row case BEFORE mapWriteError (which only classifies constraint-violation vs outage). Never set updated_at in an Update statement — the per-table set_updated_at() BEFORE UPDATE trigger owns it.
- Customer-facing DELETE endpoints schedule a SOFT delete, never a hard delete: the store stamps a nullable deletion_scheduled_at column (migration 0010 for organizations) inside the same transaction as the <resource>.delete audit record, and the endpoint returns 202 Accepted. A hard DELETE would cascade through child tables AND the audit log (ON DELETE CASCADE), so the destructive teardown is a separate worker story. Re-scheduling an already-stamped row is apierr.Conflict, detected with a Get inside the tx (the repository ScheduleDeletion is an unconditional UPDATE).
- Optimistic concurrency on mutable customer-data tables: migration 0011 adds a bigint version column (DEFAULT 1, CHECK > 0) + bump_version() BEFORE UPDATE trigger to organizations/projects/environments/services. The trigger overwrites NEW.version with OLD.version+1 on every UPDATE (including no-op data UPDATEs), so the application cannot freeze the sequence. Repository UPDATE methods accept ifMatchVersion *int64: nil = no precondition, non-nil = WHERE id=$1 AND version=$N; on no rows, a tenant-scoped Get inside the same tx disambiguates "row gone" (NotFound) from "row stale" (apierr.ConflictStale carrying the row's current version). Service-layer Update/ScheduleDeletion also pre-check the version after the read so a no-op data patch still surfaces the conflict against the right row. The HTTP layer parses RFC 7232 strong ETag values (`"<n>"`, lenient unquoted form accepted; weak/wildcard/multi-value rejected as 400 E_INVALID_INPUT) from If-Match, mirrors the row's authoritative version into the ETag response header on every success path (GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE), and surfaces stale writes as 409 E_CONFLICT carrying current_version under the new structured error.details map (apierr.DetailKeyCurrentVersion). yerr.Error.Details (map[string]string, set by WithDetail) is the agent-friendly counterpart to Hint; apienvelope renders it through the same regex redaction backstop used for Message/Hint.
- Process lifecycle is owned by internal/controlplane/runtime: RunHTTPServer(ctx, srv, shutdownTimeout, logger) for the API and worker.Loop for background jobs. Backend binaries derive shutdown from one signal.NotifyContext and pass cfg.ShutdownTimeout; they do not hand-roll select/Shutdown blocks.
- runtime.Readiness holds named startup gates (concurrency-safe). httpapi.NewHandler takes a runtime.ReadinessReporter; /readyz must be 503 yalla.error.v1 E_SERVER until every gate passes, 200 yalla.output.v1 after. /healthz is liveness-only and never depends on downstream checks.
- worker.Loop depends only on the Claimer/Lease interfaces so the durable job store can land later. The loop checks ctx before every Claim (stop claiming on shutdown) and releases interrupted in-flight leases on a context.WithoutCancel context so the release survives shutdown. A job whose Run returns nil is committed and must never be released.
- Lifecycle code is tested with fakes (fake httpServer, fake Claimer/Lease) — no real sockets, no live deps.
- Request correlation lives in internal/controlplane/telemetry. telemetry.Correlate is the outermost HTTP middleware (httpapi.NewHandler wraps the mux in it): it resolves request_id (per request) + correlation_id (per workflow) onto the request context before any handler runs. Inbound X-Request-Id/X-Correlation-Id are honoured only if telemetry.SafeID passes ([A-Za-z0-9._-], 1..128) — unsafe values are dropped and a fresh telemetry.NewRequestID() is minted, so injection payloads never reach headers/logs/jobs. Read IDs anywhere via telemetry.FromContext/RequestID/CorrelationID; never thread them through signatures. Non-HTTP entry points seed context with telemetry.WithCorrelation.
- Per-request structured logging lives in internal/controlplane/telemetry: telemetry.RequestLogging(logger) is the middleware just inside Correlate (httpapi.NewHandler wraps the routed mux in it). One JSON record per request: method/route/target/status/latency_ms/bytes/request_id/correlation_id, plus org_id/principal_id when set via telemetry.SetOrgID/SetPrincipalID (a request-scoped mutable holder the auth/policy layers enrich). Headers and bodies are never logged; the request target is redacted. Level follows the outcome (5xx error, 4xx warn, else info); the logger threshold (YALLA_LOG_LEVEL) gates emission. Service binaries bind service via logger.With and log JSON to stdout.
- Backend errors are built through internal/controlplane/apierr, the catalogued error taxonomy — never yerr.New directly in handlers/services. apierr constructors return *yerr.Error (so they flow into apienvelope.WriteError); apierr.Catalog/Lookup pair every code with HTTP status (derived from apienvelope.StatusForCode — single source of truth), retry semantics, and a MessagePolicy. MessageGeneric codes (internal + all dependency failures) keep the cause out of Message/Hint and only Wrap it for logging. InvalidInput carries FieldViolation{Field,Reason} (path + reason, never the submitted value), recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf; dependency failures are recoverable via apierr.DependencyOf even when they share a code. Adding a code = define the yerr.Code constant in internal/errors (and its codeDescriptions, AllCodes, ExitCode entries — the code is shared with the CLI manifest) + add to apierr.taxonomy + add an apierr constructor + (if non-500) a apienvelope.StatusForCode case; update the TestStatusForCode / TestConstructorsEmitCataloguedCodes / TestCode_ExitCodeMappingIsStable tables.
- Schema changes are versioned SQL files under internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/ named NNNN_name.up.sql (required) + NNNN_name.down.sql (optional, reversible-only), embedded via //go:embed. The migrate.Migrator runner owns a checksummed schema_migrations ledger, serialises with a pg advisory lock, and runs each migration in its own transaction. Migrator.Up is idempotent and refuses dirty / unknown-version / checksum-mismatch databases (errors.Is sentinels ErrDirtyDatabase/ErrUnknownVersion/ErrChecksumMismatch). NEVER edit an applied migration file (trips ErrChecksumMismatch by design) — add a new higher-versioned migration. Persistence tests are integration tests: they connect via YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL, create an isolated throwaway database per test, and t.Skip when the env var is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres. docker-compose.yml at repo root provisions local Postgres. pgx (github.com/jackc/pgx/v5 + pgxpool) is the Postgres driver.
- Persistence tests use the shared harness internal/controlplane/testutil: RequireDB(t) gives an empty isolated throwaway Postgres database per test, RequireMigratedDB(t) also applies migrations; both t.Skip with testutil.SkipReason when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset and drop the DB on cleanup (parallel-safe — unique DB name per call). testutil.NewFactory(t) builds deterministic, globally-unique in-memory fixtures for the Organization->Project->Environment->Service hierarchy + User/APIKey (two factories never collide). testutil.AssertRedacted/AssertRedactedValue guard "must not leak a secret" assertions. store/migrate keeps its own local testPool — it cannot import testutil (import cycle: testutil imports migrate). Keep pure decision logic (lookupAdminDSN, scanLeaks) unexported + white-box tested, since testing.TB is sealed and cannot be faked.
- Store-backed httpapi endpoints use a narrow reader-port pattern: httpapi declares the port interface (e.g. OrganizationReader), a Store.Read-backed adapter in the store package satisfies it by composing the tenant-scoped repository (mirror store.CredentialReader — never issue fresh SQL), and the port is threaded NewHandler -> newRouteTable -> the handler closure. httpapi imports store (acyclic — store never imports httpapi). For an endpoint with no path/query parameter, tenant isolation is structural: the handler only ever passes principal.OrganizationID. NewHandler signature: NewHandler(build, readiness, meta, authenticator, engine, orgs, logger).
- HTTP routes are data-driven from internal/controlplane/httpapi/routes.go newRouteTable — the single source of truth. Each apiRoute pairs a served http.HandlerFunc with its openapi.Endpoint metadata; httpapi.NewHandler registers every entry on the mux AND generates the OpenAPI document from the same table, so a served route is always a documented route (TestEveryRegisteredRouteIsDocumented enforces it in CI). Never call mux.HandleFunc directly in NewHandler — add to newRouteTable. The OpenAPI 3.1 document is built by internal/controlplane/openapi.Build from a neutral []openapi.Endpoint, references the two stable envelope schemas, is served raw (not enveloped) at GET /openapi.json without auth, and marshals maps with sorted keys for a byte-stable artifact. Secret-shaped sample values use output.Sentinel.
- Domain tables live in versioned migrations under store/migrate/migrations/. Every customer-data table carries organization_id (directly or via a verified parent chain) and is tenant-isolated at the database level by COMPOSITE foreign keys: a child FK (organization_id, parent_id) references the parent's UNIQUE (organization_id, id), making a cross-tenant child unrepresentable. Slugs are citext, unique within parent scope, never global. users is a global identity table; org membership is the memberships join table. Schema-level integration tests live in package store_test (NOT package migrate — it can't import testutil) and match constraint violations via *pgconn.PgError SQLSTATE class 23.
- Persistence access goes through internal/controlplane/store: Store.Read (read-only tx -> Querier) and Store.Write (read/write tx -> *Tx; commit on nil, rollback on ANY error or panic). A *Tx is obtainable ONLY inside Store.Write; repository mutation methods take *Tx, read methods take Querier — so a mutation structurally cannot run outside the transaction that also carries its authorization and quota checks. A unit of work validates input first, then composes authorize -> reserve quota -> write desired state -> enqueue durable job inside one Store.Write. The policy/quota/jobs dependencies are narrow port interfaces defined IN store (Authorizer, QuotaReserver, JobEnqueuer); store never imports those packages. Error mapping is uniform: pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound, SQLSTATE class 23 -> apierr.Conflict, other driver errors -> apierr.StoreUnavailable; the raw cause is wrapped for logging only. ProjectRepository/ProjectService are the reference implementation.
- Credentials: internal/controlplane/auth owns generation/hashing/verification; internal/controlplane/store owns persistence. A secret is stored ONLY as a one-way hash plus a public, globally-UNIQUE prefix — never plaintext. Plaintext credential types implement String/GoString/LogValue/MarshalJSON returning output.Sentinel; the plaintext is reachable only via an explicit Reveal() method. High-entropy secrets (>=128 bits random) use fast unsalted SHA-256 + subtle.ConstantTimeCompare, NOT bcrypt/argon2. Authentication lookup (FindByPrefix) is the ONE deliberate non-tenant-scoped repository read — it runs before the tenant is known; the found row carries its own OrganizationID. Tenant-scoped mutations that may match zero rows report apierr.NotFound via tag.RowsAffected()==0.
- Authorization is internal/controlplane/policy: policy.Engine is a pure, total decision function — Decide(Principal, Action, Resource) -> Decision{Allow, Reason} (stable Reason codes); Authorize/AuthorizeCtx map denials onto typed apierr (Unauthenticated for a missing principal, Forbidden otherwise). Every endpoint maps to exactly one policy.Action; the action lives in policy's defaultActionCatalog (the authoritative requiredAction -> Capability map) — add new actions there or via policy.WithAction. Roles confer Capability sets (self/read/deploy/write/admin/owner/support), not individual actions; six built-ins are fixed, custom roles resolve through a CustomRoleResolver hook. Scoped Grants narrow/widen authority within an org via Scope containment over Organization->Project->Environment->Service; cross-tenant resources are denied by construction except support reads/break-glass. The principal travels on the request context via policy.WithPrincipal/PrincipalFromContext — never thread it through signatures. The store.Authorizer port is satisfied by this engine; store still never imports policy. TestPolicyMatrix exhaustively covers all built-in roles x catalogued actions.
- Request authentication is internal/controlplane/auth.Authenticator: it resolves any of the three bearer schemes (API key yk_..., human session JWT, internal worker secret) to one auth.Identity{Principal, Method}. Every invalid-credential cause collapses to the single ErrInvalidCredentials sentinel (missing creds = ErrNoCredentials) so no error is a "does this key prefix exist?" oracle; a genuine datastore failure is propagated unchanged as a typed *yerr.Error, never disguised as invalid credentials. The Authenticator depends on the narrow auth.CredentialStore port (+ APIKeyRecord/ServiceAccountRecord DTOs) so auth never imports store; the production adapter is store.CredentialReader (store imports auth, not the reverse). The HTTP wrappers are httpapi.RequireAuth(authenticator, engine, action, ResourceResolver) and httpapi.RequireInternalWorker — they authenticate, attach the principal via policy.WithPrincipal + telemetry.SetOrgID/SetPrincipalID, then authorize: missing creds 401 E_AUTH, invalid 401 E_AUTH (fixed generic message), unauthorized 403 E_FORBIDDEN (stable policy reason), dependency failure keeps its typed 5xx. ResourceResolver nil defaults to the principal's own org scope; pass a path-param resolver so a cross-tenant id is a 403, not a silent allow.
- Quota schema (0006_quota_policy_schema): a closed enum reused across tables is a Postgres DOMAIN over text + CHECK (here quota_resource over 14 dimensions, quota_enforcement_mode over hard/soft/metered/disabled); DROP DOMAIN goes in the down migration after the tables. quota_policies holds one limit per (scope, resource) where a row is EITHER a plan_default (text plan, no plans-table FK yet) OR an organization override — enforced by one table CHECK + two partial unique indexes; the checker resolves "org override else plan default". quota_usage is one counter row per (org, resource); quota_reservations are short-lived (amount>0, status active->committed|released|expired, expires_at, settled_at tied to non-active status by CHECK) and expose UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target; usage_events is append-only (no updated_at/trigger, signed delta) and links reservations via a tenant-safe composite FK. An optional cross-table link inside a tenant uses a plain composite FK (MATCH SIMPLE) — NOT ON DELETE SET NULL, which would null the NOT NULL organization_id.
- Quota enforcement: the QuotaRepository (internal/controlplane/store/quota.go) is the persistence half (EffectiveLimit / LockUsage / SumActiveReservations / InsertReservation, all tenant-scoped, mutations on *Tx) and the quota.Checker (internal/controlplane/quota) is the store.QuotaReserver service half. A hard limit is enforced by LockUsage doing INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING then SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the quota_usage counter row — that lock serialises concurrent reservers so they cannot both over-allocate; the decision is current+reserved+requested <= limit, reserved = SUM of active non-expired reservations. Soft/metered limits record but never reject; disabled/no-policy allow with no write. A rejection is apierr.QuotaExceeded wrapping a recoverable quota.ExceededDetail{Resource,Current,Reserved,Requested,Limit}. Quota accounting rows have no domain.Kind — ids are minted locally with a prefixed crypto/rand helper.
- Audit log (0007_audit_events, store/audit.go, internal/controlplane/audit): the immutable audit log records every authorization decision, allowed AND denied. Immutability is enforced at two layers — the table has no updated_at and a BEFORE UPDATE trigger (audit_events_reject_update) that rejects every UPDATE in the database, and AuditRepository exposes only Append + tenant-scoped ListByOrganization (no update/delete); row DELETE stays reachable only via the organizations ON DELETE CASCADE. An append-only table that must still cascade on tenant teardown blocks UPDATE only, never DELETE. The audit.Auditor (depends on the narrow Recorder port, satisfied by *store.AuditRepository) is the single place a policy.Decision becomes an AuditEvent: it pulls the principal (policy.PrincipalFromContext) + correlation ids (telemetry.FromContext) off the context, files the event under the principal's org else the resource's org, and REDACTS metadata before persistence — secret-shaped keys (token/secret/password/credential/...) replaced wholesale with output.Sentinel, every other value + the user agent scrubbed by an output.Redactor. store treats metadata as already-redacted; redaction is the audit service's contract. Auditor.Record takes the caller's *store.Tx so an audit write can share the mutation's transaction or run in its own.
- Provisioning jobs (0008_provisioning_jobs, store/job.go): the durable, idempotent queue of Dokploy provisioning work. The provisioning_jobs table is tenant-scoped (organization_id FK + composite (organization_id, project_id|environment_id|service_id) FKs so a job can never target another tenant's resource), idempotent (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key)), and exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target — 0008 also wires the FK that 0006 deferred (quota_reservations.job_id -> provisioning_jobs). DB CHECKs enforce the closed status set, "terminal status iff finished_at set", "lease held iff status=running", and attempts<=max_attempts; the Go state machine (store.JobStatus.CanTransitionTo, backed by the jobTransitions map: queued->running|cancelled, running->succeeded|retrying|failed|dead_letter|cancelled, retrying->running|cancelled, terminals have no edges) is the authoritative validator. JobRepository follows the ProjectRepository pattern (mutations need *Tx, reads take Querier, NotFound for cross-tenant ids); Transition locks the row FOR UPDATE, validates the edge before any write (illegal edge = apierr.Conflict), and derives lease/attempt/timestamp bookkeeping from the target status so a caller cannot leave a CHECK-violating row. error_summary is run through an output.NewRedactor() backstop on every write (Insert + Transition) — the store IS the redaction chokepoint for job errors, unlike audit metadata which the service redacts.
- HTTP idempotency (0009_idempotency_keys, store/idempotency.go, httpapi/idempotency.go): mutating endpoints are made retry-safe by httpapi.RequireIdempotency(IdempotencyStore, ttl) — middleware installed INSIDE RequireAuth (it reads policy.PrincipalFromContext to scope the key to a principal within a tenant). Safe methods and unsafe requests with no Idempotency-Key pass straight through (opt-in). The claim protocol: store.IdempotencyRepository.Claim is a WHERE-guarded upsert — INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE ... WHERE expires_at <= now() — so it freshly claims, transparently steals an expired row (claimed=true), or returns the existing live claim (claimed=false, RETURNING yields no row, follow-up Find reads the row the DO UPDATE attempt locked). On claimed=true the middleware runs the handler against a buffering responseCapture, then Complete records the rendered envelope (status < 500) or Release drops the claim (status >= 500, so a server failure is retried not replayed). On claimed=false: hash mismatch -> apierr.IdempotencyConflict (E_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT, the only use of the new code), completed -> replay the recorded body+status with Idempotency-Replayed: true, still pending -> apierr.Conflict "already in progress". The request hash is sha256(method\npath?query\nbody); the body is buffered then restored as a fresh r.Body so the handler still reads it. Keys are scoped UNIQUE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key); a completion CHECK ties status='completed' to a non-null response. The store-backed IdempotencyStore adapter (httpapi.NewIdempotencyStore) runs each repo call in its own Store.Write — RequireIdempotency is implemented and tested but not yet wired into a route (no mutating endpoints exist yet), same as RequireAuth.
- Granular 404-vs-409 on INSERT requires a pre-check inside Store.Write: store.mapWriteError collapses every SQLSTATE class 23 violation (unique, foreign-key, check, not-null) to one apierr.Conflict, so without a pre-check a missing FK target surfaces as 409 instead of the correct 404. MembershipService.Add Gets the organization (typed NotFound) and UserExists-checks the user (typed NotFound) before INSERT, leaving the PK duplicate as the only remaining violation — correctly 409. Pre-check parent existence whenever an endpoint must distinguish "missing referent" from "already exists".
- Mutating httpapi endpoints follow the writer-port pattern, mirroring the reader-port one: httpapi declares a narrow creator/writer port (e.g. OrganizationCreator), and a store-package unit-of-work orchestrator (OrganizationService, like ProjectService/ServiceAccountService) satisfies it. The orchestrator validates input BEFORE opening a transaction (buildXToCreate is split out + white-box unit-tested with no DB), then composes the desired-state write + audit append inside ONE Store.Write so a created row can never exist without its audit record. The orchestrator audits via *store.AuditRepository directly (it is in the store package — no cycle) and builds the store.AuditEvent from plain-string actor/correlation fields the handler passes in CreateXInput (ActorID/ActorKind/ActorOrgID/RequestID/CorrelationID) — store takes no dependency on policy/telemetry. The audit event is filed under the actor's home org; metadata only carries structurally-safe values (the canonical slug). The handler stays thin: read principal, validate.DecodeJSON the body, pull telemetry.FromContext, call the port, render http.StatusCreated. NewHandler signature is now NewHandler(build, readiness, meta, authenticator, engine, orgs, creator, logger); newRouteTable takes (build, readiness, meta, orgs, creator). Follow-up: mutation audits do not yet capture IP/UA and do not route through audit.Auditor's redactor; denied-decision auditing at the RequireAuth layer is still unwired for every endpoint.
- Postgres-backed worker queue (store/job.go ClaimNext, worker/queue.go): the durable queue has two halves. store.JobRepository.ClaimNext is the SKIP LOCKED claim — SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED picks the single most-eligible job (queued|retrying AND next_run_at<=now, OR running AND lease_deadline<now) so concurrent claimers skip a locked row instead of blocking, then transitions it to running inside the same Store.Write tx. An expired-lease job is reclaimed THROUGH the state machine (running->retrying->running, so attempt/lease bookkeeping stays consistent); a job past its retry budget is dead-lettered instead and the poll claims nothing. worker/queue.go is the store-backed Claimer/Lease impl: loop.go stays pure (interfaces only, fake-tested) and queue.go is the only worker file that imports store. Failure classification: worker.Terminal(err)/IsTerminal mark a permanent failure (-> failed, never retried); a plain error is transient (-> retrying with worker.Backoff bounded exponential delay, or -> dead_letter when Attempts>=MaxAttempts). StoreClaimer.complete() records every outcome on a context.WithoutCancel + timeout context — once the runner ran, the outcome write must survive shutdown; only an interrupted (un-run) job returns non-nil from Lease.Run so the loop releases it. The JobRunner (real Dokploy provisioner) is still injected/faked; cmd/yalla-worker stays on NoopClaimer until that runner lands.
- Fake Dokploy (internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake): the default Dokploy for EVERY normal test — a live Dokploy server is an opt-in external smoke test only. dokployfake.New() returns a *Server backed by httptest serving a Dokploy-shaped REST API (organization/project/environment/application/compose/database/domain/deployment/logs/status). It mimics Dokploy's own JSON shapes, NOT yalla.output.v1/yalla.error.v1 — it stands in for the upstream provisioning API, not a Yalla public surface (so the "stable envelope" acceptance criterion is N/A for Dokploy-facing test doubles). IDs are deterministic per-kind counters (org_1, proj_1, ...). Server.QueueFault arms a FIFO fault queue (StatusFault for 4xx/5xx, TimeoutFault honouring request cancellation, MalformedJSONFault); each request consumes one fault, applied before auth+routing. Every request is recorded (Server.Requests()) with Authorization/X-Api-Key/X-Auth-Token/Cookie header values replaced by output.Sentinel and the body run through an output.Redactor seeded with the bearer token — so a worker test can prove credentials were sent without the secret ever entering the fixture. The package depends only on internal/output + stdlib (no testing.TB, no store), so any package's tests import it without a cycle.
- Typed Dokploy client (internal/controlplane/dokploy.Client): customer/handler code NEVER calls Dokploy — only worker/provisioner code, through this typed wrapper. The client exposes Yalla intents (EnsureProject/EnsureService/DeployService/ReadDeploymentLogs/RemoveService/...), not raw Dokploy ops. The Dokploy token is injected via Config.Token, held unexported, exposed by no method, and redacted by Client.LogValue. "Ensure" intents are idempotent against Yalla's source of truth: an optional ExistingID does GET+verify, an empty one does POST+create (the worker records the returned Dokploy ID in Postgres). Only idempotent methods (GET, DELETE) are retried — never POST — and only on apierr.Retryable errors, with bounded exponential backoff; each attempt gets a per-request context timeout and forwards X-Request-Id/X-Correlation-Id from telemetry.FromContext. Every failure maps onto the apierr taxonomy (400->Invalid, 401/403->Internal as a non-retryable Yalla misconfig, 404->NotFound, 409->Conflict, 429/5xx/transport->DokployUnavailable, deadline->Timeout(DependencyDokploy)); the redacted upstream body is wrapped only as the log-only cause, never a client-facing Message. Normal tests run against dokployfake; the client touches no Postgres.
- Dokploy provisioning has two pure, no-I/O layers under internal/controlplane/dokploy that the worker composes: the Mapper (mapping.go) is the ID-resolution layer — top-down translation of the Yalla* hierarchy into Ensure*Input intents, each child method taking the parent's resolved Dokploy ID; the Renderer (renderer.go) is the declarative-content layer — Render(RenderInput) -> RenderedSpec resolves variables (ascending precedence org<project<environment<service), zero-fills ResourceLimits from tier defaults, normalises BuildSettings, and emits deterministic names/labels/domains, needing no resolved parent IDs. Inapplicable fields are DROPPED, not errored (engine for non-db, role for db, cron_schedule for non-cron, domains for non-web) — mirror this convention for any new provisioning shape. RenderedSpec carries verbatim variable values for the worker; RenderedSpec.Summary() / LogValue redact EVERY variable value (not just Secret-flagged) for logs/audit. Golden tests use testutil.GoldenJSON + the global -update-golden flag, goldens live in testdata/<feature>/.
- Request input validation goes through internal/controlplane/validate: build a validate.New() Collector, call validators (Name/Slug/ID/Path/URL/Domain/Resources/EnvVars/GitRepoURL/GitBranch/GitCommit/ImageRef) against it, return c.Err() — nil or one apierr.InvalidInput carrying every collected violation (no short-circuit). A FieldViolation.Reason NEVER echoes the submitted value (classification text + bounds only) — this is what lets a secret env var be rejected without leaking. Policy-dependent rules are passed in as plain options the caller resolves (Domain takes DomainOptions{AllowWildcard}, set only after feature flag + quota say yes); validate imports no config/quota. EnvVars separates secret vs non-secret via a distinct ".secret[i]" path segment and a larger secret length ceiling. validate.DecodeJSON is the strict JSON body decoder: size-capped, rejects unknown fields + trailing data, every failure is a body-free apierr.Invalid. Every string validator has a fuzz target seeded with long strings / invalid UTF-8 / path traversal / control chars.
- Customer-facing soft-delete endpoints come in two shapes. Organization.delete uses a deletion_scheduled_at stamp + 202 Accepted (asynchronous teardown by a future worker; re-scheduling is apierr.Conflict). API-key DELETE is a one-way revocation: the api_keys.revoked_at column is stamped and the credential is permanently unusable from that moment (the auth-layer IsUsable check already rejects a revoked row), the row stays so the audit trail of who minted it stays linked to it, response is 200 OK with the revoked snapshot (mirroring members.Remove's terminal-view convention). The repository (APIKeyRepository.Revoke) is silently idempotent at SQL via COALESCE(revoked_at, $now), but the service layer (APIKeyService.Revoke) pre-checks current.IsRevoked() inside the tx and returns apierr.Conflict on re-revoke — the customer's view of the lifecycle is stale and a silent success would write a misleading audit record. Pattern: customer endpoints surface stale-lifecycle conflicts; repository methods stay SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers (workers, admin jobs).
- "Add policy matrix coverage for <endpoint>" stories add a <endpoint>_policy_test.go beside the endpoint's contract test, in package httpapi, driving the REAL NewHandler + real policy.NewEngine() (the production authorization path, fake Authenticator only). Table the principal classes the PRD names: every built-in role, revoked/expired credentials (modelled as Disabled principals — the only shape in which a revoked/expired credential reaches the engine; the auth layer's earlier 401 path is the contract test's job), and a wrong-organization principal. For a CapSelf endpoint the matrix is "all allow" — assert the served data is the caller's OWN identity, never escalated/cross-tenant. Grant-containment criteria (sibling project / production env / parent-level secret non-leak) are engine properties: assert them with policy.NewEngine().Decide directly, then tie back by proving the endpoint echoes the confined grant set verbatim. Reuse the existing helpers (meHandlerFor/getMe/decodeMe, middleware_test.go's decodeError) — a sibling _test.go needs no new scaffolding.
- HTTP endpoints are data-driven entries in internal/controlplane/httpapi/routes.go newRouteTable: apiRoute{endpoint, handler, resolver}. Authorization is declarative — set openapi.Endpoint.RequiresAuth=true + RequiredAction=string(policy.ActionX), and NewHandler auto-wraps the handler in RequireAuth(authenticator, engine, action, resolver) before registering it, so a served authenticated route is structurally impossible without authorization. TestEveryAuthenticatedRouteHasMappedAction enforces the RequiresAuth<->RequiredAction invariant in CI. The optional apiRoute.resolver derives the policy.Resource from path/query params; leave it nil for self/org-root actions (RequireAuth then authorizes against the principal's own org scope). NewHandler signature is (build, readiness, meta, authenticator, engine, logger); httpapi tests build it through the newTestHandler helper (canned fakeAuthenticator + policy.NewEngine()). Handlers stay thin: read policy.PrincipalFromContext, render via apienvelope.WriteData/WriteError. cmd/yalla-api wires the real stack at startup (pgxpool -> store.New -> store.NewCredentialReader -> auth.NewAuthenticator + policy.NewEngine) and requires YALLA_DATABASE_URL in every profile.
- Drift reconciliation is internal/controlplane/reconcile: a pure engine that classifies every Yalla<->Dokploy divergence into exactly one of three buckets — DriftSafe (auto-repaired through the Repairer port: env var ensures/updates/removals, re-bound managed domains), DriftDangerous (recorded through the ReviewRecorder port: missing managed service, missing managed database, renamed domain on a known Dokploy ID, service type changed), or DriftUnmanaged (a Dokploy resource without a Yalla counterpart, recorded through the UnmanagedRecorder port — never auto-deleted and never exposed to customers by default). Diff(desired, actual) is a pure function; the Reconciler depends only on the four port interfaces (DesiredStateReader, ActualStateReader, Repairer, ReviewRecorder, UnmanagedRecorder) so adapters land in store/worker/dokploy without reaching back into reconcile. Every classification field carries a stable, value-free DriftReason (env_var_changed, domain_renamed, service_missing, database_missing, resource_unmanaged, ...) — an env var value reaches Action only via DesiredValue, consumed by the Repairer adapter and nowhere else. Apply is best-effort: per-action failures land in Result.Failures with their error string scrubbed through output.Redactor; the loop only stops on ctx cancellation. Untyped adapter errors are upgraded to apierr.StoreUnavailable (desired) or apierr.DokployUnavailable (actual); typed apierr.* from adapters (NotFound, Forbidden) flow through unchanged so callers keep the real taxonomy.
- Crockford Base32 (lowercase) excludes i / l / o / u — hand-rolled test IDs containing those letters (e.g. "demo", "kvl", "old", "ufo") fail domain.ParseID with E_INVALID_INPUT. Use domain.MustNewID(domain.KindX) for fixtures (the convention in dokploy/mapping_test.go and reconcile_test.go); a test failure where every port-level case fails with the same E_INVALID_INPUT is almost always the ID validator rejecting the fixture before any port runs.
- ON CONFLICT against a PARTIAL unique index requires the WHERE predicate in the conflict_target clause (`ON CONFLICT (cols) WHERE <same predicate> DO UPDATE`), otherwise Postgres rejects the upsert because the index is not eligible. quota_policies_org_resource_idx is the canonical case: ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind = 'organization' DO UPDATE. The freshly-minted id from newQuotaID is only used on insert; on conflict the existing row's id and created_at are preserved (and updated_at is owned by the per-table trigger).
- List/upsert PATCH endpoints (e.g. PATCH .../limits): the request DTO carries a top-level array (not a partial-update of individual fields), each entry is an upsert, and the response is the post-write effective state re-read inside the SAME Store.Write tx — so the wire body and the committed state agree byte-for-byte. Validation rules: at least one entry (empty array = 400), every entry must be self-consistent (closed-set resource, range-checked limit, closed-set enforcement mode, no duplicate keys across entries), and a `*int64` for numeric fields whose zero is meaningful distinguishes "field omitted" (400) from "set to zero" (legitimate extreme). Field paths use `field[i].subfield` so an agent can pinpoint which entry was rejected.
- Nullable scalar fields that always travel together project as a SINGLE nullable nested OBJECT on the wire, never as coupled-nullable scalars. GET /v1/organizations/{org_id}/usage is the canonical case: `{resource, used_value, limit: null}` for unconstrained vs `{resource, used_value, limit: {limit_value, enforcement_mode, source}}` when a policy is configured — three nullable scalar siblings would force every agent to handle each invalid (limit_value null but enforcement_mode set, etc) permutation. The store struct mirrors the wire: a single `*Limit` (or three pointers projected together with an all-or-nothing guard in the projector). Adding a new field to the nested object later (e.g. a billing period for metered limits) is then forward-compatible with existing readers, whereas adding a new top-level field next to coupled scalars is not.
- Reads that union "configured" + "live" state for the same key set (a quota policy join with quota_usage, a desired-state join with reconciled actual state, ...) use ONE SQL statement with FULL OUTER JOIN + COALESCE(left.key, right.key) for the projection key, never two queries with a Go-side merge. The repository method takes (organizationID, plan) and stays tenant-scoped at every leg of the join — the CTE shape `WITH policies AS (... WHERE org=$1 OR plan=$2), usage AS (... WHERE org=$1) FULL OUTER JOIN ... ORDER BY resource` is the reference; two queries would force the handler to re-implement deterministic ordering AND would leak observability (the two queries can see different snapshots).
- HTTP reads of an "already-redacted at persistence" surface (audit log, job error_summary, redacted reservation reasons, ...) project the persisted values onto the wire VERBATIM — the handler must not silently re-scrub or un-scrub sentinel values. The contract is "redaction is the writer's job, the reader trusts the chokepoint": audit.Auditor redacts metadata + IP + UA before AuditRepository.Append persists the row (the store treats them as clean), and the HTTP handler renders them as-is. Pin this structurally with a "feed the fake reader a row whose values are output.Sentinel, assert the wire renders Sentinel" test — a future regression that "cleans up" sentinels in the projection fails this test, not only review.
- Endpoints that expose N coupled scalar fields that are EITHER all-present OR all-absent project as ONE nullable nested OBJECT on the wire, not N coupled-nullable scalars. Canonical cases: usage.limit ({limit_value, enforcement_mode, source} or null), audit_event.actor ({id, kind} or null for an unauthenticated denied event), audit_event.resource ({id, kind} or null for an organization-root action). An agent reads one boolean (`actor != null`) instead of validating N invalid combinations. The store struct mirrors the wire (a single `*Foo` pointer or N pointers projected together with an all-or-nothing guard). Adding a new field to the nested object later is forward-compatible.
- audit.read is the load-bearing example of a "read action that is NOT CapRead" in the policy catalog (catalog.go: ActionAuditRead -> CapAdmin). The audit log cannot be listed by a viewer or a developer — only by an owner, admin, or a support principal performing a cross-tenant read. A boilerplate "every in-tenant role allowed" assumption is wrong for audit.read; the policy matrix and a contract-test "viewer denied in own tenant" assertion together pin the property (the contract test makes a future accidental drop to CapRead a contract failure, not only a matrix failure).
- Organization-scoped variables (0012_organization_variables, store/organization_variable.go, httpapi/variables.go): the lowest-precedence layer of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service variable hierarchy. The table mirrors the established conventions — composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target for future scope-narrowing override tables; UNIQUE (organization_id, key) for per-tenant key uniqueness (an env var named DATABASE_URL in two orgs is two rows); ON DELETE CASCADE from organizations; bigint version + bump_version() trigger for optimistic concurrency. The repository follows the AuditRepository read pattern: ListByOrganization is tenant-scoped at the SQL predicate (cross-tenant id matches no rows, empty list, never another tenant's data), ordered deterministically by (key ASC, id ASC). The wire redaction contract is wire-side at the projection chokepoint, NOT store-side: store.OrganizationVariable.Value carries the literal verbatim (a future write path needs lossless round-trip), but httpapi/variables.go's organizationVariableOf replaces is_secret=true values with output.Sentinel on every projection — agents can never read a secret value back through this endpoint. Defence-in-depth: OrganizationVariable.LogValue redacts every value (regardless of is_secret) at the slog boundary, so a stray log record that captures the struct cannot leak the literal. Non-secret values pass through verbatim so customers can audit their own organization-wide defaults.
- The env.write policy matrix (BE-0111, internal/controlplane/httpapi/variables_put_policy_test.go) is the structural twin of the CapWrite/no-cross-tenant-support family (project/environment/service create/update/delete) and the load-bearing distinction from the env.read matrix (BE-0108): same {org_id} resolver, same organizationIDResolver, but Developer JOINS the allow row (RoleDeveloper holds CapWrite), Viewer/CI/Support all deny via ReasonDeniedNoCapability, and the engine's cross-tenant clause (`required == CapRead || required == CapSupport`) excludes CapWrite — so a Support principal cannot cross tenants and cannot mutate its OWN home tenant either. The grant-containment row mirrors the env.read suite verbatim except that the org-level allow row tests a Developer grant (CapWrite) instead of a Viewer grant (CapRead); the Viewer grant at the same org scope is asserted to DENY, locking the CapWrite requirement against the grant path so a future catalog drift in either direction fails here. Wire-level redaction (organizationVariableOf -> output.Sentinel) is pinned on the allow-by-role AND allow-by-grant branches, and deny-path leak checks use a distinct seededPostWriteOrgVariables fixture (POST_WRITE_REGION/POST_WRITE_SECRET with the recognisable "post-write-plaintext-correcthorsebatterystaple" needle) so the matrix's request body (MATRIX_KEY/matrix_value) and the env.read suite's seededOrgVariables (REGION/DATABASE_URL) cannot false-positive a leak check. Cross-tenant assertions on the wire pin BOTH the submitted-payload non-echo (MATRIX_KEY, matrix_value) and the post-write-data non-echo — the policy boundary is the only place an env mutation is gated, so a denied caller seeing either would itself be a contract violation.
- The CapWrite-on-nil-resolver policy matrix (BE-0123, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_create_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the env.write matrix above (BE-0111) but for endpoints that use a NIL ResourceResolver instead of organizationIDResolver — POST /v1/projects is the reference. The wire surface offers NO path/body field that could name another tenant (the strict JSON decoder rejects organization_id in the body, the contract suite TestCreateProjectIgnoresOrganizationIDInBody pins that), so tenant isolation on the wire is STRUCTURAL: the creator is always invoked with principal.OrganizationID and there is no caller-controlled value the policy boundary could fail open on. The cross-tenant probe therefore moves OFF the wire and ONTO a sub-test loop (cross_tenant_owner/admin/developer/viewer/ci/support, each t.Parallel()) that authorizes the action against a foreign-org Resource directly through policy.NewEngine().Decide; EVERY role — including Support — must deny via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant, because CapWrite is outside the engine's `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)` exception. The role matrix proper still allows Owner/Admin/Developer (ReasonAllowedByRole) and denies Viewer/CI/Support (ReasonDeniedNoCapability) at the principal's own home org root. Grant-containment for a nil-resolver write authorizes against the org root, so project/env/service-scoped admin grants all deny with ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (covers() one-way); an org-level VIEWER grant denies with ReasonDeniedNoCapability (the load-bearing distinction from a CapRead matrix where the same grant ALLOWS), an org-level DEVELOPER grant allows via ReasonAllowedByGrant. Deny-path leak needles cover BOTH the caller-supplied request-body fields AND the canned-creator-output fields (two recognisable values per field, e.g. prj_matrix_alpha vs the request projectID) — a denied response leaking either is a separate regression direction, kept in one guard. Future CapWrite-on-nil-resolver endpoints (none yet planned; this is currently the only one) can copy this shape verbatim.
- Hard-delete vs soft-delete decision rule: a customer-facing resource takes a SOFT delete (deletion_scheduled_at on organizations + 202 Accepted, revoked_at on api_keys + 200 OK with revocation snapshot) ONLY when the row carries a continuing tie that must outlive the resource — an audit-log FK that must keep the row reachable, a child cascade that must be ordered carefully, or a "lifecycle stale" property the customer's audit trail depends on. A resource with no continuing tie (the audit log records the deletion independently, no child tables, no FK ties) takes a HARD delete with a 200 OK response carrying the row's snapshot exactly as it stood at the moment of removal (DELETE /v1/organizations/{org_id}/variables/{key}, BE-0115). The store repository method uses `DELETE FROM ... WHERE org=$1 AND key=$2 RETURNING ...` so one round trip removes the row AND yields the snapshot the orchestrator returns to the wire; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound (a cross-tenant key is indistinguishable from a missing row). The service files an audit event in the SAME tx as the delete, with Metadata={variable_id: row.ID} — customer-supplied key + the deleted value are never recorded, so the audit row is auditable without ever observing the variable name or its value. An agent can confirm what was deleted (id, key, version, timestamps) without a separate read; 204 No Content would have been less useful here.
- Audit-event listing in tests: store.AuditRepository.ListByOrganization is ORDER BY occurred_at DESC, id DESC — events[0] is the NEWEST event. A test that reads events[len-1] gets the OLDEST event, which in a "seed then mutate" fixture is the seed event (with seed metadata), NOT the mutation under test. A test that probes the post-mutation state through ANOTHER mutation (e.g. a second Replace to verify the deleted row is gone) files yet another audit event and pushes the mutation-under-test to events[1] — so audit assertions must run BEFORE any further state-changing call, and post-state probes must read directly through the repository (NewOrganizationVariableRepository().ListByOrganization), NOT through another service-level mutation.
- Deep-resource resolver pattern (BE-0124, GET /v1/projects/{project_id}, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects.go projectIDResolver): when an endpoint targets a resource named in the path but the path has NO org_id segment (sub-organization endpoints like /v1/projects/{project_id}, future /v1/.../environments/{environment_id}, /v1/.../services/{service_id}), the ResourceResolver pulls the principal's home OrganizationID from the request context with policy.PrincipalFromContext(r.Context()) — RequireAuth attaches the principal BEFORE invoking the resolver, so the context already carries it even though the ResourceResolver signature is func(r *http.Request) policy.Resource. The resolver returns Scope{OrganizationID: principal.HomeOrg, ProjectID: r.PathValue("project_id"), Kind: domain.KindProject}; do NOT look up the resource's real org from the database inside the resolver (keep the resolver pure path+context, no I/O). The policy engine then authorizes against the principal's own home org root combined with the path id, so a project/environment/service-scoped grant for THAT resource (containment match) authorizes via ReasonAllowedByGrant while a grant for a SIBLING resource fails the engine's covers() check; cross-tenant project_ids reach the tenant-scoped repository query with the principal's home org and surface as a deterministic 404 (E_NOT_FOUND) from the persistence layer, NOT a 403 from policy — which is acceptable per the acceptance criteria ("cross-tenant IDs must behave as not found or forbidden"). The Support principal's cross-tenant read capability does NOT apply through this endpoint because the resource scope is pinned to the principal's home org, not the path's tenant; support cross-tenant reads remain available through endpoints whose path DOES carry an {org_id} (organizationIDResolver). PATCH/DELETE on the same resource adopt the same resolver — handlers should call PathValue and use principal.OrganizationID for the store call, never trust a caller-supplied org id. The canonical regression check is TestGetProjectCrossTenantIDBehavesAsNotFound (the response body must not echo a foreign organization id either).
- Reader-port extension pattern (BE-0124): when a new endpoint on the same resource family lands (e.g. GET /v1/projects/{project_id} alongside GET /v1/projects), extend the existing reader port in place rather than declaring a parallel sibling interface — ProjectReader now has BOTH ListProjects and GetProject; OrganizationReader has GetOrganization and is used by both list and by-id endpoints. The fakeFooReader in the *_test.go is similarly extended in place (added project/getErr/gotGetOrgID/gotGetProjectID fields on fakeProjectReader for the by-id tests); the zero value of the extended fake still works for every prior call site (the other test files / suites that never reach the handler) so no cross-test churn is needed. The store-backed adapter pattern stays the same: *Reader composes *Repository (never re-issues SQL), each new method opens its own short-lived Store.Read tx and delegates to an existing Querier-accepting repository method, inheriting that method's tenant-scoping guarantees for free. Auth-error sentinel naming gotcha: the auth package exports the PLURAL form (auth.ErrInvalidCredentials, auth.ErrNoCredentials) — singular forms do not exist; tests that exercise the invalid-credential path must use the plural names to match the writeAuthError errors.Is dispatch.
- The CapWrite-on-deep-resource policy matrix (BE-0129, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_update_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the CapWrite-on-nil-resolver matrix (BE-0123, projects_create) but for endpoints that use projectIDResolver (or any deep-resource resolver: future environmentIDResolver, serviceIDResolver). The role matrix is the same CapWrite split (Owner/Admin/Developer allow via ReasonAllowedByRole; Viewer/CI/Support deny via ReasonDeniedNoCapability) because the resource org id comes from the principal's home org and falls same-tenant at the engine. The cross-tenant probe stays at the engine layer (the wire cannot name another tenant — a foreign project_id surfaces as 404 from the tenant-scoped repository query, with the body never echoing the foreign org), and EVERY role — including Support — must deny via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant on a foreign-org Resource. Grant-containment for a deep-resource write authorizes against the resource (project / env / service) scope, NOT the org root, so a project-level grant naming THIS resource ALLOWS via ReasonAllowedByGrant and a project-level grant naming a SIBLING resource denies via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (covers() one-way); a project-level VIEWER grant on the SAME resource denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability (the grant covers the resource but does NOT confer CapWrite — this is the NEW assertion vs CapRead matrices, where a Viewer grant covers + allows); env-/service-scoped grants on the parent project deny via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope; an org-level Admin grant allows via ReasonAllowedByGrant; an org-level Viewer grant denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability (mirror-image of the org-level Viewer ALLOW on CapRead). The engine-twin "scoped key in a foreign home org" deny is also pinned (a scoped key cannot be smuggled across tenants — the cross-tenant guard fires before the grant is even considered). Deny-path leak guards needle for the canonical row identifiers AND the caller-supplied request-body fields (display_name patch payload), because either echo is a separate regression direction. Future CapWrite-on-deep-resource endpoints (project DELETE/restore, environment update/delete, service update/delete) copy this shape verbatim.
- Deep-resource PATCH pattern (BE-0127, PATCH /v1/projects/{project_id}, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects.go updateProjectHandler): sub-organization PATCH endpoints reuse the deep-resource resolver (projectIDResolver) to authorize against the (principal home organization, {project_id}) resource the path names — same resolver, same RequiredAction (policy.ActionProjectUpdate), and the store call is built from principal.OrganizationID and r.PathValue("project_id"), NEVER from the request body. The request DTO exposes ONLY the partially-updatable fields (Slug *string, DisplayName *string); the strict JSON decoder rejects an unknown organization_id field as a stable 400 E_INVALID_INPUT, so a body-injected org_id can never reach the store layer. Optimistic concurrency is bolted on by reading the optional If-Match header through the existing parseIfMatchVersion (the same parser organizations PATCH/DELETE already uses, accepting `"<n>"` strong ETag and the lenient unquoted form), forwarding the *int64 to the store as IfMatchVersion, and mirroring the returned row's authoritative version back into the ETag response header on success via writeOrganizationETag — the helper is generic in body despite the historical name, so the same call shape works for projects, future environments, services, etc. The handler stays thin: read principal, parse If-Match, validate.DecodeJSON, call port, render. The orchestrator (ProjectService.Update) follows the OrganizationService.Update shape exactly: validate input BEFORE opening the tx (buildProjectUpdate + validateProjectDisplayName are split out, unit-testable without a DB, and reject an empty patch as 400 InvalidInput), then in ONE Store.Write tx Get current, version pre-check (current.Version != *IfMatchVersion -> apierr.ConflictStale(current.Version)), apply non-nil fields, repo.Update(tx, desired, IfMatchVersion), audit.Append — a denial at any step rolls every other step back, so a row mutation and its audit record commit together or not at all. The store layer files the audit event under the actor's home organization with Metadata={"updated_fields": "slug,display_name"} — stable wire field names only, never the submitted values, so the audit trail records the SHAPE of the mutation without leaking input. classifyProjectConcurrencyMiss mirrors classifyOrganizationConcurrencyMiss: on the version-checked UPDATE matching zero rows, a tenant-scoped Get inside the same tx disambiguates "row gone" (NotFound) from "row stale" (ConflictStale carrying the row's current version) — the Get itself is tenant scoped, so a cross-tenant id still surfaces as NotFound and cannot reveal another tenant's data. Pre-existing test failures gotcha: TestAPIKeyServiceCreate* and TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch* fail on HEAD due to unrelated pre-existing issues with the testutil.Factory's id/slug formats vs the production-grade ParseSlug validator; verified by stashing the BE-0127 change and reproducing the failures on the base commit. These are NOT caused by BE-0127 and are out of scope.

- New customer-data resource = full vertical (BE-0136 pattern, project_grants migration 0014, store/project_grant.go, httpapi/project_grants.go): one story is allowed to land the whole vertical when the resource has no foundation yet (no migration, no repo, no port, no handler, no wiring). The migration declares the table tenant-scoped at the database (composite (organization_id, project_id) FK -> projects with ON DELETE CASCADE, composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target for future scope-narrowing override tables, bigint version + bump_version trigger from 0011, set_updated_at trigger), CHECKs the enum-shaped columns to a closed set (principal_kind IN ('usr','sa'), role IN (six built-in roles)), and uses a UNIQUE index with COALESCE(env_id,'') / COALESCE(svc_id,'') so per-principal scope-tuple dedup works through NULLs (Postgres unique constraints treat NULLs as distinct by default until v15's NULLS NOT DISTINCT). The repository (ProjectGrantRepository) and reader adapter (ProjectGrantReader) follow OrganizationVariableReader exactly — repo accepts a Querier for reads, mutations need *Tx, reader composes the repo through Store.Read; cross-tenant queries return empty (no rows) at the SQL leg, but the LIST-FOR-PARENT shape (list grants OF a project) requires the reader to Get the project first inside the same transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound — never as an empty list, which would invite an agent to believe the project exists with no grants. The HTTP layer adds a new ProjectGrantReader port + handler + projection (projectGrantResource), and the route plugs into the existing projectIDResolver and policy.ActionProjectGrantsRead in routes.go's newRouteTable. EnvironmentID / ServiceID stay nullable pointers all the way from the schema to the wire so the (project | environment | service)-scoped distinction reaches an agent intact; the principal projects as a nested object Principal(id, kind) for the same reason the audit_event.actor and usage.limit endpoints do — one boolean (principal.kind == 'usr') instead of branching on coupled scalars. NewHandler signature gets ONE more positional port, which forces a sweep of every test file that constructs the production handler (a Python regex over 28 test files anchored on `fakeProjectRestorer{}, X)` plus manual edits to projects_restore_test.go and projects_delete_test.go whose end-of-call shape didn't match the dominant anchor; six single-line newRouteTable calls with all-nil args need a separate sweep to add one trailing nil). cmd/yalla-api wires *store.ProjectGrantReader at startup. The story implicitly delivers most of BE-0439's repository acceptance criteria (CRUD comes with the PUT story BE-0139 later) and BE-0440's tenant-isolation criteria for the list path.
- Bare top-level lookup-by-id endpoint for a deep resource (BE-0154 pattern, GET /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go GetByID + GetEnvironment, httpapi/environments.go): a customer-facing route that addresses a deep resource (env, service, deployment) by its global id WITHOUT carrying its parent ids in the path. Two load-bearing constraints: (1) the resolver builds policy.Resource{Scope: {OrganizationID = principal.home, <ResourceLevel>ID = path.id}} — pinning OrgID to the principal's home (NEVER the caller-supplied id) is the structural anti-spoof, and pinning the resource-level id is the only scope leg the path actually carries; (2) the resolver CANNOT pin the parent ids without a DB lookup, and the policy engine's Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) requires every level the grant pins to match the resource — a grant with pinned ProjectID against a resource with empty ProjectID is structurally denied. Accepted design: middleware admits org-wide read roles + org-wide grants + (for CapRead actions) the support cross-tenant exception is suppressed because the resolver pins OrgID to principal.home; project-/env-/service-scoped grants are denied at the boundary by design, and grant-only principals must use the parent-scoped /v1/projects/{project_id}/environments instead. Document this in the route description, the resolver doc, and the handler doc — readers will misinterpret the structural deny as a bug otherwise. The store layer adds a tenant-scoped GetByID(ctx, q, organizationID, resourceID) on the existing repository (composite (organization_id, id) predicate so cross-tenant returns apierr.NotFound, never another tenant's row, never a 500 leaking the cause), and the existing reader struct grows a Get<Resource>(ctx, organizationID, resourceID) method that wraps GetByID inside Store.Read. The HTTP layer adds a NEW narrow port (distinct from the existing parent-scoped port — e.g. EnvironmentReader vs ProjectEnvironmentReader — so a handler change to one doesn't ripple through the other), a NEW resolver function (e.g. environmentIDResolver), and a NEW handler that REUSES the existing wire-shape projection (projectEnvironmentOf for environments) so the GET-by-id response is byte-identical to one element of the LIST response (modulo the wrapping object name). cmd/yalla-api/main.go passes the same *store.<Resource>Reader value twice — once for the parent-scoped port, once for the new bare-lookup port — because both methods live on the same store struct; no second startup wiring is needed. The contract tests cover the standard envelope: happy path, NotFound (cross-tenant + unknown both render as the same 404 — no oracle), 401 unauthenticated + invalid credentials (no token echo), 503 store outage (no raw cause leak), 500 nil-port wiring guard, envelope shape lock (asserts the documented field set against a generic decode so a future struct column added without updating the projection is caught), bearer-token never-logged guard (slog over a buffer), OpenAPI route registration, and TWO resolver unit tests (pins home org + path id; no panic with no principal on context). The matching policy matrix story (BE-0156 for environments, the structural template for service/deployment lookups) tests the deny — the deny matrix on the bare endpoint is part of the contract, not a gap.
- environment.grants.write-on-bare-{environment_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0171, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_grants_put_policy_test.go) is the structural HYBRID of the project.grants.write matrix (BE-0141, projects_grants_put_policy_test.go) and the environment.grants.read matrix (BE-0168, environment_grants_policy_test.go): take BE-0141's CapAdmin role table + cross-tenant + request-body-leak-guard skeleton, swap action+resolver for the write env-grants endpoint, and re-pin every wire tie-in with BE-0168's no-ProjectID covers() denial pattern (project/env/service scoped principals ALL denied OutOfScope at this route — even on a grant naming the SAME env id — because the env-id resolver pins NO ProjectID leg and covers() is one-way). Load-bearing engine distinction from BE-0168: CapAdmin is OUTSIDE the support cross-tenant exception, so the engine_cross_tenant_deny_* parallel subtests MUST include the Support row for write actions and exclude it for read actions. Net-new fixtures in the put_policy file are only a route-specific replaceEnvironmentGrantsMatrixBody (no environment_id field per grant — the env is named by the path) + replaceEnvironmentGrantsRequestBodyLeak with distinctive needles; the canonical*EnvGrants* fixtures, fake*EnvironmentGrant* fakes, replaceEnvironmentGrantsHandlerFor / putEnvironmentGrants / decodeReplaceEnvironmentGrants, and orgPrincipal / decodeError are all reused verbatim from BE-0168 and BE-0169.
- Inbound HTTP rate-limit pattern (BE-0035, internal/controlplane/ratelimit/limiter.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/ratelimit.go + apierr.RateLimited + config.RateLimit): the limiter is an in-process multi-dimensional token-bucket gate (per-org, per-API-key, per-IP) with read/write-class specs derived from the HTTP method (GET/HEAD/OPTIONS = read, everything else = write). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the gate wraps every route INSIDE RequireAuth in server.go's per-route loop (`h = rateLimit(h); if RequiresAuth: h = RequireAuth(...)(h)`) so the resolved principal (org id, key id, auth method) is on the request context when the limiter decides — public endpoints fall back to the IP bucket alone; (2) the internal-worker exemption is wired through a new package-private context helper `withAuthMethod` / `AuthMethodFromContext` in middleware.go that BOTH RequireAuth and RequireInternalWorker stamp with id.Method, so the rate-limit gate can recognise auth.MethodInternalWorker and pass `Exempt=true` to the limiter without re-running the credential store; (3) the "first exhausted bucket wins" rule short-circuits downstream debits (org denied -> key & ip buckets untouched) so a single throttled tenant cannot drain a shared NAT'd IP bucket of every neighbour. Bucket map is sync.Mutex'd with lazy eviction on the request path (no background goroutine; pruning sweeps every IdleTTL); the freshly-created bucket MUST set lastUsed=now BEFORE the sweep runs so the sweep cannot reap the bucket it just inserted (initial zero-value lastUsed is older than any cutoff and was a real bug caught by tests). The 429 wire shape is the standard yalla.error.v1 envelope with apierr.RateLimited(scope, retry) attaching `scope` (bucket dimension name only — "organization" / "api_key" / "ip", NEVER the tenant id or key id or IP) and `retry_after` (integer seconds, clamped to >=1 because the wire Retry-After header cannot carry sub-second); the response also sets the matching `Retry-After: N` header. NewHandler grew ONE new trailing parameter (RateLimiter) — sweep all 64 NewHandler call sites in tests via a Node script that walks the source with depth-tracking parens and appends `, nil` before each closing `)`. Config plumbing: new YALLA_RATE_LIMIT_* env vars (ORG/KEY/IP × READ/WRITE × RPS/BURST + IDLE_TTL + DISABLED master switch) layered over per-profile defaults; ProfileTest defaults to Disabled=true so contract tests across the whole repo never accidentally trip the limiter. The limiter constructor takes a `func() time.Time` clock injection point so deterministic tests (`fixedClock.Advance(d)`) can drive refill behaviour without sleeping. Forward-applicable to any future throughput cap (per-resource, per-tenant-tier, per-route-specific) — extend Specs/Specs.For pairs rather than adding a new bucket dimension. Redaction backstops: the 429 envelope, the Retry-After header, the WARN log record on denial, and every rate-limit identifier flow through telemetry.LogAttrs / apienvelope's regex-based scrubber so a bearer-token in the Authorization header on a throttled request NEVER reaches a log file (TestRateLimitDenialLogsNoSecretValue is the regression pin).
- Pagination contract package (BE-0037, internal/controlplane/pagination): every customer-facing list endpoint composes the shared pagination.Params + pagination.Cursor + pagination.Page[T] envelope rather than reimplementing limit/cursor/sort/filter parsing. ParseParams(url.Values, ParseOptions) parses ?limit= ?cursor= ?sort= ?direction= ?filter.<k>= with stable apierr.InvalidInput (E_INVALID_INPUT) violations whose Reason text NEVER echoes the submitted value (only classification + accepted range — protects against reflection-style content channels), accumulates multiple violations into one envelope, enforces the package MaxLimit=200 ceiling even when an endpoint misconfigures its own, and rejects a cursor whose embedded Sort/Direction disagrees with the request as a stable 400 "fetch the first page again" instead of silently serving overlapping pages. Cursor is base64url-encoded JSON {v: "yalla.cursor.v1", p: Position, s: Sort, d: Direction} — opaque to clients, deterministic (same input -> same encoding so HTTP fixtures diff byte-for-byte), URL-safe (RawURLEncoding, no '+/='), and DOES NOT carry an organization_id / tenant / principal field by contract (a TestDecodeCursorTenantIsolationCannotBeBypassed test guards this so adding one is impossible without breaking the suite — tenant isolation stays the persistence layer's responsibility, cursor only encodes a within-set position). Page[T any] is the wire shape: Items always non-nil (BuildPage guarantees []T{} for empty), NextCursor omitempty (empty string == end of stream — agents do NOT compare len(Items) to limit), TotalEstimate *int64 omitempty (nil = "not cheap to compute", endpoints must NOT fabricate a value). BuildPage uses the over-fetch strategy: store fetches Limit+1 rows, BuildPage trims to Limit and encodes next_cursor from the last retained row's position via a PositionFunc[T] adapter. Cursor stability under inserts is proven end-to-end by TestCursorIsStableUnderInserts: seed N rows, page once, insert NEWER rows after the cursor boundary, prove page 2 still returns exactly the remaining originals (no duplicates, no skips, no newly-inserted rows leaking onto page 2 because the cursor is a strict upper bound on (sort_key DESC, id DESC)). Future list endpoints replace their bespoke parseXxxLimit / list payload struct with one ParseParams call + a BuildPage projection — the existing GET /v1/organizations/{org_id}/audit-events (BE-0103) and break_glass list are pre-pagination-contract and should migrate in their own stories.
- Bare-id route on a deployment (BE-0211+0212+0213 pattern, GET /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}, store/deployment.go DeploymentReader.GetServiceDeployment, httpapi/deployments.go DeploymentGetter port + deploymentIDResolver + getServiceDeploymentHandler + getServiceDeploymentPayload, routes.go GET /v1/deployments/{deployment_id} entry, server.go NewHandler DeploymentGetter parameter, cmd/yalla-api/main.go wires the SAME *store.DeploymentReader as BOTH DeploymentLister AND DeploymentGetter): canonical shape for ALL bare-id read endpoints on a Deployment-level deep resource. Two LOAD-BEARING structural differences from the environment / service bare-id routes (BE-0154 / its services sibling): (1) policy.Scope has NO DeploymentID leg — the Scope hierarchy stops at ServiceID (Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service), so the resolver pins ONLY OrganizationID, NOT (org + DeploymentID) the way environmentIDResolver pins (org + EnvironmentID). The resource scope carries no inner leg at all, which makes the engine's covers() rule deny EVERY scoped grant (project/env/service) at the boundary via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope — even a service-scoped grant naming the deployment's actual parent service is denied, because covers() is one-way (a grant scope that pins ServiceID cannot cover a resource scope that does not). Principals whose only access is a scoped grant must use a parent-scoped route family to address a deployment. (2) The store-layer adapter intentionally skips the parent-prefetch the list adapter performs: DeploymentReader.GetServiceDeployment composes only DeploymentRepository.GetByID inside a short-lived Store.Read tx (no service prefetch), because the schema's composite foreign keys on the deployments table already guarantee (organization_id, project_id), (organization_id, environment_id), and (organization_id, service_id) cannot diverge across the deployment row — a tenant-scoped GetByID is itself a complete tenant-isolation check, and a prefetch would only duplicate the work of the schema. This is the load-bearing distinction from the list adapter ListServiceDeployments (which DOES prefetch the service to disambiguate "service missing" from "service has no deployments"). The wire shape (serviceDeployment + serviceDeploymentOf in service_deployments.go) is REUSED verbatim — an agent that listed deployments for a service does not re-parse a different shape to fetch one of them by id. Production wiring passes the same *store.DeploymentReader pointer twice (deploymentService, deploymentReader, deploymentReader, breakGlassService, ...): one concrete adapter satisfies BOTH the DeploymentLister AND DeploymentGetter port interfaces, so adding a second read port costs zero new constructors / dependencies. The contract test suite (deployments_get_test.go, 12 tests) and policy matrix (deployments_get_policy_test.go, 4 parent tests) follow the BE-0210 list-side template structure verbatim, with one structural extension in the grant-containment matrix: a service-scoped grant naming THIS deployment's actual parent service is also denied at the wire, because the bare-id resource scope has no ServiceID leg to match. Future bare-id read endpoints on deep resources whose Scope leg does not exist (deployments today; jobs / runs in later stories) should adopt this exact pattern.


- Bare-id read endpoint on a deep resource with a worker-deferred upstream (BE-0229+0230+0231 pattern, GET /v1/services/{service_id}/logs, store/service_log_reader.go ServiceLogReader.ListLogs, httpapi/services_logs.go ServiceLogReader port + serviceLogLine/listServiceLogsPayload wire DTOs + parseServiceLogsLimit + listServiceLogsHandler, routes.go GET /v1/services/{service_id}/logs entry, server.go NewHandler + newRouteTable + cmd/yalla-api/main.go wiring, services_logs_test.go contract suite + services_logs_policy_test.go matrix): canonical shape for ANY observability read endpoint whose real upstream is the Dokploy worker (logs, metrics — BE-0232..0234 next; future events/traces) and whose true content adapter is deferred to a later worker story. Three load-bearing rules: (1) the store-level adapter (store.ServiceLogReader, the second instance of the typed-input-typed-output read adapter shape after store.ServiceReader.GetService) performs the tenant-scoped parent existence check inside a Store.Read transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown service_id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound at the persistence boundary — never disguised as an empty success and never disguised as a 500 — and then returns an empty Lines slice with the ServiceID populated; the real Dokploy log fetcher is wired in BE-0301+ alongside the provisioning worker, matching the noopJobEnqueuer pattern for placeholder upstreams; (2) the wire DTO is a closed tuple `(service_id, lines[]{occurred_at, stream, message})` with NO pointer-typed fields, NO embedded interfaces, and an empty lines slice that marshals as `[]` not `null` so an agent does not need to special-case the absent-vs-empty distinction; the occurred_at field is RFC3339-nanosecond UTC; the stream enum is the closed set {stdout, stderr, system} so an agent can branch on the value without escape-decoding it; (3) the ?limit= query parameter is clamped at the boundary via parseServiceLogsLimit (the second instance of the bounded-limit-query-parser shape after parseAuditEventLimit, range [1, 1000] default 100), and the error path never echoes the submitted value — only the classification and the accepted range — so a typo can never become a reflection-style content channel. The CapRead policy matrix shape is the structural sibling of services_get_policy_test.go (BE-0184) — all six built-in roles allow same-tenant via ReasonAllowedByRole, support is allowed cross-tenant via ReasonAllowedBySupport at the engine level (defence-in-depth — the customer-facing route cannot reach this branch because serviceIDResolver pins the resource to the principal's home org), every project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied OutOfScope on the bare-service-id path via the engine's one-way covers() rule, and an org-level Viewer grant is ALLOWED end-to-end (the load-bearing distinction from services_start_policy_test.go where the same grant is denied because service.start is CapDeploy, not CapRead). Limit assertion subtlety pinned by tests: the no-echo check on the invalid-limit deny path must restrict to non-numeric submitted values because numeric out-of-range inputs may legitimately share digits with the accepted-range message ("0" is a substring of "1000" in the "must be between 1 and 1000" string) — only the non-numeric case (where caller-controlled byte content could realistically reach the response body) is asserted. Test-only nil-port test: a typed-nil ServiceLogReader is needed (a bare `nil` literal does not satisfy the interface positional argument), so `var nilReader ServiceLogReader` is the pattern. Port-arity sweep: dominant `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` -> `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceLogReader{}, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` across 63 test files (108 sites) plus a minor variant `fakeServiceStopper{}, reader, fakeServiceVariableReplacer{}` -> `fakeServiceStopper{}, fakeServiceLogReader{}, reader, fakeServiceVariableReplacer{}` in service_variables_test.go (the test injects a local fakeServiceVariableReader as `reader`, so the LogReader slot is the new positional argument); the `stopper, fakeServiceVariableReader{}` variant in services_stop_test.go is one of the 108 dominant sites. Forward-applicable to BE-0232+0233+0234 (GET /v1/services/{service_id}/metrics, metrics.read action — the OTHER CapRead observability endpoint on the same resolver), and to any future read endpoint whose true content adapter is the Dokploy worker (events, traces) — same vertical, same placeholder shape, same wire-contract guarantees while the real fetcher lands later. The OpenAPI `?limit=` query parameter today is documented only via the Description prose because openapi.Endpoint has no QueryParams field; a future BE story can add structured QueryParam metadata and the existing route entries (audit-events, logs) will switch over together. CONFIRMED two-instance pattern after BE-0232+0233+0234: GET /v1/services/{service_id}/metrics mirrors the logs vertical one-for-one (store.ServiceMetricsReader with tenant-scoped GetByID, httpapi.ServiceMetricsReader port + listServiceMetricsHandler + parseServiceMetricsLimit + listServiceMetricsPayload, serviceMetricSample{name, occurred_at, value, unit} wire DTO with empty samples slice marshalling as [] not null, route on serviceIDResolver with action metrics.read, contract suite in services_metrics_test.go, policy matrix in services_metrics_policy_test.go); the metric-name closed set is the usage-metric ladder (http_requests, http_response_bytes, http_rps_peak_1m, latency_p95_ms, container_cpu_millicore_seconds, container_memory_mb_hours, storage_gb_month, build_minutes, …); the type-level doc explicitly disclaims billing authority (usage_counters + BE-0569/BE-0580 remain authoritative for invoiceable totals — this surface is a customer-visible read-through, not the billing oracle). New-arg mechanics: a Python ast-walking script (paren-depth-aware comma scanner, REVERSE call-order insertion so earlier offsets stay valid) injects the new ServiceMetricsReader / fakeServiceMetricsReader{} positional arg at slot 55 of newRouteTable and slot 57 of NewHandler across 33 test files / 63 call sites — reproducible recipe for any future newRouteTable / NewHandler signature growth. The third instance (events, traces, profiles, anything else under serviceIDResolver) can follow this template mechanically.


- POST create endpoint for a service-scoped child resource (BE-0250+0251+0252 pattern, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups, mirrors the service_domains POST trio BE-0238+0239+0240; clonable for BE-0264 previews, BE-0289 import previews, and any future bare-id child create): the store-layer composition is the canonical create-unit-of-work shape — ServiceXxxRepository.Insert (single-statement INSERT...RETURNING + mapWriteError mapping driver errors to typed apierr.Conflict whose message is value-free) + ServiceXxxService.Create orchestrator that opens a Store.Write Tx, runs the tenant-scoped parent existence check first (so a cross-tenant or unknown parent id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound, NEVER a 500 or silent success), runs an in-tx Authorize (defence-in-depth against grant-change races between the HTTP authorize and the desired-state write — the ONLY operation that runs in-tx Authorize; the in-transaction Authorizer is reserved for Create), runs the Insert, and Appends the immutable audit record — all inside the same *Tx so a row can never be persisted without its audit trail. Validation runs BEFORE the Tx is opened (validateCreateXxxInput against a shared validate.Collector so a request rejecting on more than one field surfaces every violation in one typed apierr.InvalidInput; submitted values NEVER appear in violation reasons). The customer-facing create pins any lifecycle status column to a fixed initial value (e.g. ServiceBackupStatusPending) so a customer cannot forge a 'succeeded' / 'completed' status through the public API surface — the worker is the authority for closed-taxonomy transitions; the status column is NOT in the createXxxRequest decoder shape and the validator overwrites in.Status in the validated struct. The audit Metadata records ONLY fields the application layer can prove redaction-safe by construction: structural identifiers (service_id), closed-taxonomy enums (status, certificate_type), and bounded primitives (retention_count, version, enabled flag rendered as "true"/"false"); caller-supplied free text (display_name, schedule, hostname even — schedule is a cron-style expression but is still caller-controlled bytes) is NEVER recorded verbatim, because a customer pasting a token into one of those fields is exactly the footgun a defensive convention avoids. The httpapi-layer composition is the canonical create-handler shape — createXxxRequest decoder (Enabled / boolean-default-true fields are pointers to preserve the absent-vs-explicit-false distinction at the JSON seam — `*bool` decoded; an omitted field collapses to the default at the handler boundary, an explicit `false` rides through unchanged; integer fields take their schema-side default when zero) + createXxxPayload + createXxxHandler that gates on RequireAuth + the typed-internal nil-creator guard + validate.DecodeJSON (strict, max-body-bytes, unknown-field rejection, no input echo on parse failure) + creator.Create + http.StatusCreated yalla.output.v1 envelope projecting through serviceXxxOf. Action is CapWrite (ActionXxxCreate -> CapWrite in catalog.go): owner / admin / developer allowed by role; CI / viewer / support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — load-bearing distinction: a CapDeploy sibling action (e.g. ActionBackupRun, ActionDeploymentCreate) WOULD admit CI. The wire route uses serviceIDResolver (no parent_project_id leg pinned on the resource scope), so EVERY project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied at the boundary via the engine's one-way covers() rule (ReasonDeniedOutOfScope); only an organization-wide write role or an org-level write grant reaches the handler. Cross-tenant: the resolver pins resource scope to the principal's home org id (never the path service's tenant), so cross-tenant service_ids hit the persistence layer with the home org id and surface as a deterministic apierr.NotFound — engine cross-tenant deny is pinned at the engine level (defence-in-depth: EVERY role including Support is denied cross-tenant for CapWrite — the support cross-tenant exception covers only CapRead / CapSupport). Contract test surface: success with full plumbing assertions (principal home org id forwarded, path service id forwarded, actor id forwarded, no caller-body org id trusted), boolean-default-true and explicit-false at the pointer seam, request_id propagation, validation failure propagated from creator as E_INVALID_INPUT (handler delegates validation to the store-layer orchestrator), strict-decode unknown-field rejection BEFORE creator runs, unauthenticated 401, role-deny 403 (handler never reached), cross-tenant 404 with principal home org forwarded, duplicate-id Conflict 409, store-unavailable 503, nil-creator typed Internal 500. Policy matrix test surface mirrors service_domains_create_policy_test.go one-for-one. Wiring footprint: adds ONE new positional arg (ServiceXxxCreator) to newRouteTable (~slot 57) and NewHandler (~slot 60); ~70 test files / ~120 call sites need a `fakeServiceXxxCreator{}` insertion via bulk substitution `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` -> `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` (where Yyy is the sibling Reader and Zzz is the next port after the new slot in the signature); the special cases are the in-package contract-test helper for the new resource (`reader` -> `..., reader, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, ...`) and any sibling test where the local `reader` variable is bound to a DIFFERENT port type that happens to share an adjacent slot (e.g. service_variables_test.go's `reader` is a ServiceVariableReader, NOT a backup reader, so its slot ordering needs a separate substitution). Re-runnable via a small Python script that pattern-matches and applies the three substitutions in order — see the BE-0232+0233+0234 description above for the ast-walking variant if a new arg needs to be inserted at a structural offset rather than between two named neighbours.

- Verb-on-child-resource endpoint pattern (BE-0253+0254+0255, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups/{backup_id}/run; sibling of the services_restart verb-on-parent shape, but adds a tenant-scoped child existence check; clonable for any future "trigger an action on a worker-driven child resource" endpoint — backup PATCH/DELETE, future preview-run, future deployment-retry triggers): the store-layer composition is the canonical run-unit-of-work shape — XxxRepository.GetByID (composite (org, parent, child_id) read; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound(kind, child_id) — the kind/id NEVER contains the org or parent so the customer-facing message stays free of identifiers the principal cannot already prove they own) + XxxRepository.MarkPending (UPDATE...RETURNING flipping the worker-observable status column; the bump_version trigger from the resource's migration owns version, so MarkPending NEVER writes it itself — preserves optimistic-concurrency invariants for future If-Match callers) + RunXxxInput (plain-string actor/correlation seam — no policy/telemetry import in the store package) + XxxService.Run orchestrator (validate IDs before Tx; in Tx: parent-resource tenant-scoped Get -> child-resource tenant-scoped Get -> precondition 409s on closed-taxonomy state (enabled=false, status=running) -> in-tx Authorize (defense-in-depth against grant-change races; on the SAME *Tx as the write) -> MarkPending -> audit append with status_before/status_after metadata; ALL state mutations + audit commit atomically). The audit Metadata records ONLY closed-taxonomy enums (status_before/status_after) and structural identifiers (service_id); caller-controlled free text (display_name, schedule) is NOT recorded — Run does not mutate either and including them would expose a future redaction footgun. The httpapi layer is the canonical verb-handler shape — empty body, principal home org + path {parent_id} + path {child_id} forwarded to the runner, RequireAuth gates on the action's catalog mapping (backup.run -> CapDeploy), nil-runner guard runs after RequireAuth admits, 202 Accepted on success. Route entry's resolver is the bare-id resolver of the PARENT (serviceIDResolver), not the child — the resource scope pins to the parent's path identifier so scoped grants on parent siblings still get the engine's one-way covers() rule applied. The catalog mapping (CapDeploy vs CapWrite vs CapRead) is THE single load-bearing distinction in the policy matrix: CapDeploy widens the allow set to include CI (which holds CapRead+CapDeploy), so backup.run is allowed for CI keys even though backup.create (CapWrite) is denied — the policy matrix test pins this distinction at every grant scope (project-, env-, service-, org-level grants) AND at the engine's cross-tenant deny (CapDeploy is OUTSIDE the support cross-tenant exception, same as CapWrite). The bulk fake-fixture sweep for the new positional port follows the BE-0244 pattern: `perl -i -pe 's/fakePREVIOUS\{\},/fakePREVIOUS{}, fakeNEW{},/g' $(grep -rl 'fakePREVIOUS{}' internal/controlplane/httpapi/)` keyed on the immediately preceding port's bare struct literal — 71 files in the BE-0253 sweep — with two always-manual follow-ups: (a) the resource's own create-test helper that uses a local variable `creator` for the previous slot, (b) any wiring test that explicitly passes `nil` for the previous slot to reach the typed-internal guard.

- BE-0309 postgres is the FIRST database-engine clone of the BE-0301/BE-0307 per-service-type renderer-subtype pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_database_postgres_test.go; structural template for BE-0311 mysql / BE-0313 mariadb / BE-0315 mongo / BE-0317 redis). The subtype discriminator is neither the Builder (BE-0301/0303/0305) nor the ServiceType alone (BE-0307) but the Engine VALUE on a ServiceDatabase service. Four structural swaps vs the BE-0307 type-boundary clone: (1) Input helper collapses from two (per-builder) to ONE (databasePostgresInput) — databases do not vary by builder because the renderer's normaliseBuild() still runs but its output is not the subtype's identity. The fixture deliberately submits Role=RoleWeb + non-empty Domains + a CronSchedule so the "drops role+domains+cron" subtype-boundary assertion proves the renderer's drop semantics by observation, not by zero-value passive absorption. (2) The Drops* test becomes a single DropsRoleDomainsAndCron pin that asserts each of the three application/compose-only fields is dropped AND that none of their submitted values appear in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String() (the customer-submitted hostname "db.acme.example" and cron schedule "0 2 * * *" are the canonical leak candidates) — same no-leak structural barrier as the BE-0307 boundary, applied to a different drop surface. (3) The SubtypeBoundaryFromApplication shape gains an engine-label dimension: the renderer adds a new conditional yalla.engine label only for ServiceDatabase services (mirroring how yalla.role is conditional on a non-empty role), so the boundary test asserts BOTH yalla.service-type (must be "database") AND yalla.engine (must be "postgres") on the postgres case AND that yalla.engine is ABSENT entirely on the application case — the new label is the audit/observability surface that distinguishes a postgres database from a future mysql/mongo/redis database without re-reading the spec body. (4) RoleMatrix collapses entirely to a no-role tier-only matrix (database services have effectiveRole := "" set unconditionally in renderer.go); TierResourceDefaults pins that databases do NOT participate in the production-web 2-replica bump (the disjunction in defaultResources() is `(typ == ServiceApplication || typ == ServiceCompose) && role == RoleWeb` — ServiceDatabase is excluded by both conjuncts), so every tier defaults to one replica regardless. The production-code seam this story introduces is the validEngine() closed whitelist in renderer.go: BE-0309 ships only EnginePostgres = "postgres"; BE-0311..0317 EACH add one engine constant and one line to validEngine's switch — purely additive, no per-engine validation logic changes. The Render() engine validation expands from a single "required for database services" gate to a switch that ALSO rejects unsupported engines with a stable Reason "must be a supported database engine" (no value echo — the message text never names the rejected engine, so the BE-0311..0317 stories that add engines do NOT need to change the message). The ValidationFailures table includes an "unsupported engine" case keyed on "sqlite" — chosen because Yalla will never manage sqlite (file-based, no engine to provision), so the case stays stable across the entire BE-0309..0317 family even as the whitelist grows. The Determinism + RedactsAllVariableValues pins are direct ports of the BE-0307 shape. database.golden in testdata/renderer/ needs the +yalla.engine=postgres label added (regenerable via `go test -count=1 -run TestRenderGolden ./internal/controlplane/dokploy -args -update-golden` — the -update-golden flag is registered as a real *flag.Bool via init() in internal/testutil/golden.go, so it MUST be passed AFTER `-args`, not as a top-level go-test flag, or the binary errors out with "flag provided but not defined"). The worker-side acceptance criteria (dokploy_refs atomic write, quota reservation-to-conversion, fake Dokploy create/update/deploy/poll/failure/retry/replay) all defer to the later database worker story per the BE-0301 deferral pattern. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0317 redis (BE-0311 mysql, BE-0313 mariadb, BE-0315 mongo all landed: validEngine extended to `case EnginePostgres, EngineMysql, EngineMariadb, EngineMongo`; renderer_database_{mysql,mariadb,mongo}_test.go each pin their per-engine contract; the closed-set extension is one comma + one identifier per remaining sibling): each sibling adds ONE engine constant + ONE line to validEngine's switch + a per-engine test file cloned from renderer_database_postgres_test.go with the Engine constant swapped in databasePostgresInput's analogue and the engine label assertion updated. NOT applicable to non-database service types or to subtypes whose discriminator is the Builder or the ServiceType.

- Per-subtype quota dimension on the services Create unit of work (BE-0326 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go ServiceService.Create + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0327 compose_stacks, BE-0328 databases — every per-subtype dimension whose denominator is a SUBSET of the services table filtered by Kind): the create unit of work calls svc.quota.Reserve TWICE in the same *Tx for a service whose kind consumes a per-subtype dimension — first the existing QuotaResourceServices reservation (counts every service), then a conditional `if service.Kind == ServiceKindXxx { Reserve(QuotaResourceXxx) }` AFTER it and BEFORE the services.Insert. Order matters: the services reservation locks first so a tenant who exhausts the services dimension cannot acquire the per-subtype counter at all; the per-subtype reservation locks second so a tenant who exhausts ONLY the per-subtype dimension still gets a typed apierr.QuotaExceeded whose ExceededDetail.Resource names the per-subtype dimension specifically. Both reservations roll back together on any later failure (Insert, Enqueue, audit Append). Test pattern (clone of BE-0325): TWO quota_usage rows + TWO quota_policies seeded (per-subtype TIGHT at 5, services LOOSE at attempts*2 so the per-subtype dimension is the boundary that bites), assert detail.Resource == QuotaResourceXxx, assert BOTH active reservation tables == success count (because every winning Create reserved one of each). A second non-concurrent test exhausts the per-subtype dimension then proves a sibling-kind Create (compose / database for BE-0326) still succeeds — pins the per-subtype boundary as a regression barrier against a future refactor that drops the Kind guard. BE-0326 already lands the kind-guard inside ServiceService.Create at the same line where Reserve(services) returns nil; BE-0327 and BE-0328 each add ONE more conditional Reserve call right below the BE-0326 block (Kind == ServiceKindCompose / ServiceKindDatabase) and ONE more test file cloned from service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go with the dimension constant + Kind constant + sibling-kind sanity-success cases swapped. NOT applicable to dimensions that span MULTIPLE service kinds with a non-trivial denominator (cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb — those count a numeric column on every kind and belong to a different per-numeric-resource pattern still to be designed).
- Per-Kind quota facet on environments (BE-0330 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environmentservice.go + migrate/migrations/0025_environment_kind.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/environment_quota_preview_environments_concurrent_test.go): structural template for ANY future per-Kind environment quota dimension (analog of the BE-0326/0327/0328 services per-Kind dimensions, now ported to the environments table). The FIRST per-Kind environment story lands the seam: a `kind` text column on environments with NOT NULL DEFAULT 'standard' and a CHECK constraint mirroring the closed Go enum one-to-one; a partial index on (organization_id, project_id) WHERE kind = '<minority-kind>' for the future sweeper; a `Kind` field on Environment, CreateEnvironmentInput, and CloneEnvironmentInput; a validateEnvironmentKind helper that normalises blank to the default member so legacy callers stay green; a closed `EnvironmentKind*` const set; and a conditional Reserve(QuotaResource<KindFacet>) on BOTH EnvironmentService.Create AND EnvironmentService.Clone (environments are the only resource with two create-shape entrypoints, both must be gated together). Subsequent per-Kind environment quota stories ship test-only — the seam is in place after BE-0330. The Reserve gates run in the SAME *Tx as Reserve(QuotaResourceEnvironments) and the desired-state write, so a tenant that exhausts either dimension cannot half-write an environment row whose other dimension passed. The default-on-blank Go validator MUST match the SQL DEFAULT so the migration is forward-compatible with in-process callers that predate the Kind field.

- Dimensional (magnitude-axis) quota dimension on a Create unit of work (BE-0331 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/projectservice.go QuotaReserver port + internal/controlplane/quota/quota.go Checker + internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go serviceCPUMillicoresByKind + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_cpu_millicores_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0332 memory_mb / BE-0333 storage_gb / and every future quota dimension whose allocation is a numeric magnitude per request rather than a +1 count). The FIRST dimensional story widens the load-bearing seam ONCE for the entire dimensional-quota family: the store-layer QuotaReserver interface gains a second method `ReserveAmount(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string, amount int64) error`, and the real adapter quota.Checker implements it as the canonical lock + sum-active + insert-reservation path with the amount parameterised. The count-axis convenience `Reserve(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string) error` is refactored to a one-line delegate `return c.ReserveAmount(...,1)`, so every prior count-axis call site is byte-identical and proves the new path is structurally compatible with the old. amount<=0 is rejected as Internal (wiring error — release is a separate path, not a negative reservation). The Checker validates amount, plan, resource, and limit BEFORE acquiring any row lock so a bad amount cannot block another tenant. The unit-of-work call site (ServiceService.Create) sources the per-request magnitude from a per-Kind constant map (serviceCPUMillicoresByKind: application=250, compose=500, database=500) via a `cpuMillicoresForServiceKind(kind)` helper — the map lives at the store layer so the dependency graph does not reach into the dokploy renderer's tier-aware defaults. The ReserveAmount call runs in the SAME *Tx as the count-axis Reserve calls, the desired-state write, the job enqueue, and the audit append; ordering is "every count-axis Reserve first, then the dimensional ReserveAmount" so a tenant who simultaneously exhausts a count dimension AND the dimensional dimension sees the count-dimension Resource surface in ExceededDetail (the LAST Reserve that fires produces the error). Three fakes (noopQuotaReserver in cmd/yalla-api/main.go, nopQuotaReserver in store/internal_test.go, recordingQuota in store/projectservice_test.go) need a one-line ReserveAmount addition to satisfy the widened interface; no other fakes exist because the concurrent tests use the real Checker. Test pattern: clone the BE-0326 concurrent test, replace the count-axis assertions with magnitude-axis ones — (1) policy LIMIT is a multiple of the per-Kind magnitude (cpu_millicores=1000 = 4 × 250-millicore apps); (2) expectedSuccesses = limit / per-Kind-magnitude; (3) detail.Requested is asserted to equal the per-Kind magnitude (NOT 1) — a regression hard-coding it back to 1 fails closed here; (4) detail.Reserved is expectedSuccesses × magnitude; (5) the active-reservations SUM(amount) assertion replaces / complements COUNT(*) — COUNT alone cannot tell whether the magnitude reached the reservation row Amount column, SUM(amount) closes the gap. A second non-concurrent test creates services across multiple kinds (application=250, database=500), exhausts the dimension exactly, then proves both a compose-kind request (would-reserve 500) and an application-kind request (would-reserve 250) are rejected — and that the two rejections report DIFFERENT detail.Requested values. The single equality check `composeDetail.Requested != appDetail.Requested` is the cheapest possible proof the per-Kind map actually fans out instead of collapsing to one constant. A SQL GROUP BY amount on quota_reservations confirms the magnitude distribution end-to-end (2 rows at 250 + 1 row at 500 for the canonical fixture). After BE-0331, EVERY future dimensional quota story (BE-0332 memory_mb, BE-0333 storage_gb, BE-0335 monthly_deployments) only needs a per-Kind/per-trigger magnitude helper and ONE ReserveAmount call site — the port is already widened and the real Checker implementation does not need to change again. The Reserve(...) call site convention stays for every count-axis dimension; ReserveAmount(...) is reserved for dimensions whose amount actually varies per request.
- BE-0332 memory_mb is the SECOND instance of the dimensional-quota template (BE-0331) and confirms the template clones without any port widening, Checker change, or fake update — only (a) a per-Kind magnitude map + helper in serviceservice.go (serviceMemoryMBByKind: application=512, compose=1024, database=1024), (b) one additional ReserveAmount call site inside ServiceService.Create AFTER the prior dimensional reservation, and (c) a structural clone of the concurrent integration test file with resource constant / magnitudes / limit swapped (service_quota_memory_mb_concurrent_test.go). Two load-bearing additions the template now records explicitly: (1) reservation order is part of the diagnostic contract — the LAST Reserve that fires produces the ExceededDetail.Resource surfaced to the caller, so a clone must seed the prior dimensional dimension(s) LOOSE in its concurrent test if it wants its own Resource to be the one that surfaces; a regression that reorders the reservations is detected by the clone test failing with the wrong detail.Resource (cpu_millicores instead of memory_mb, or vice versa). (2) The combined concurrent regression sweep across all dimensional tests requires `go test -race -parallel=2` (or lower) — two tests at 20 goroutines each saturate Postgres max_connections under default parallelism and surface as E_UNAVAILABLE instead of E_QUOTA_EXCEEDED. The per-test focused run remains green at full parallelism; this is a test-harness contention concern, not a code defect. Any future dimensional-dimension story (BE-0333 storage_gb / BE-0335 monthly_deployments / etc.) adds another concurrent test to the family and inherits both rules.
- Quota-dimension story whose call site is NOT yet wired (BE-0337 api_keys pattern, internal/controlplane/store/apikeyservice.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0027_quota_resource_api_keys.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/apikey_quota_api_keys_concurrent_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go; structural template for BE-0338 members and ANY future dimension story whose owning Service.Create predates the QuotaReserver port — applies whenever a dimension story is the FIRST to wire its service-layer Reserve, distinct from the BE-0331..BE-0336 'call site already there' siblings whose owning unit-of-work already reserves the dimension). The minimum atomic delivery is FOUR change axes: (1) closed-set expansion — a new `NNNN_quota_resource_<name>.{up,down}.sql` migration that follows the BE-0326 backup_schedules template verbatim (DO block looking up the inline DOMAIN CHECK by `contypid = 'quota_resource'::regtype AND contype = 'c'` so the migration is robust to any Postgres-assigned constraint name, then `ALTER DOMAIN quota_resource ADD CONSTRAINT quota_resource_check CHECK (VALUE IN (...))` with the prior set + the new label; the .down.sql is structurally identical and narrows back to the prior set, accepting that any row carrying the new label fails the restored CHECK — that is the correct behaviour, silently dropping rows would lose audit-relevant state); plus the matching `QuotaResource<Name>` constant + membership-set entry in internal/controlplane/store/quota.go; plus updating the `valid` slice in internal/controlplane/store/quota_internal_test.go::TestQuotaResourceValid which pins `len(valid) == len(quotaResources)` — the test is a sealing-trap that catches a forgotten membership-set entry but ALSO fires on a forgotten valid-slice entry; both must change together. (2) Constructor port widening — the existing service struct grows a `quota QuotaReserver` field, the constructor parameter list grows the same port, the nil-guard switch grows a `case quota == nil` arm placed BETWEEN the last existing repo arm and the audit arm (the convention is store → parent repos → own repo → authz? → quota → jobs? → audit), and the Create unit of work grows a `svc.quota.Reserve(ctx, tx, key.OrganizationID, string(QuotaResource<Name>))` call placed AFTER every existence/parent-lookup check and BEFORE the desired-state Insert so a 404 on the parent is never disguised as a quota rejection AND a row-level conflict (slug, prefix, unique constraint) rolls the reservation back with the rest of the unit of work. (3) Constructor call-site fanout — three call sites change: cmd/yalla-api/main.go grows a `<name>Quota := noopQuotaReserver{}` placeholder (the real quota.Checker adapter is wired separately by a cross-cutting plan-resolver story; placeholder mirrors the projectQuota / environmentQuota / serviceQuota convention exactly), the in-package `package store` constructor-nil test grows a `nopQuotaReserver{}` local + a new `{nil quota, ...}` case wedged BETWEEN the last existing nil case and the audit case (positional ordering must mirror the constructor's nil-guard switch), and the `package store_test` integration-test helper grows the shared `&recordingQuota{}` (defined in projectservice_test.go — not a local `noopQuotaReserver{}`, which is only visible to cmd/yalla-api; the store_test package has its OWN allow-all fake hierarchy and the convention is to reuse `recordingQuota` for happy-path constructors). (4) Concurrent integration test — clone of the BE-0336 concurrent_deployments test with five structural simplifications when the owning service has no Authorizer / JobEnqueuer ports (api_keys is pure Yalla identity with no Dokploy mirror, so APIKeyService takes neither): no `atomicAuthorizer` / `atomicJobs` fakes need to be defined or counted; no `authz.calls == attempts` and no `jobs.calls == successes` assertions; the per-goroutine input loop calls `auth.Generate()` INSIDE the goroutine so every concurrent attempt mints its own unique (prefix, secret_hash) pair via crypto/rand (the prefix column is UNIQUE so a shared pair would deterministically conflict and mask the quota race); the parent-row seed is only an `organizations` row (no project/environment/service chain — api_keys live directly under the tenant); and the leaky-input candidates in the rejection-message scan use the resource-specific prefix (`key-%02d` / `req_apikeys_%02d` / `corr_apikeys_%02d`) so an echoed input still fails the message guard. The 'second test' sibling (TestXxxCreateAppliesXxxQuotaIndependentlyOfOtherDimensions) seeds ONLY the dimension under enforcement, exhausts it sequentially, asserts a SIXTH Create surfaces the dimension-specific rejection, and asserts no reservation row exists on TWO unrelated dimensions (services / projects) — the symmetry guard that catches a future regression that drops the Reserve call OR accidentally chose a different resource string. The whole pattern compiles + passes -race against a real Postgres without touching ANY of the 70+ httpapi fake-fixture call sites because the service's HTTP layer is owned by a separate seam (the api_keys handler already calls APIKeyService.Create with the same input shape; only the store-layer wiring changes). Pre-existing test failures gotcha: TestAPIKeyService* and TestEnvironmentService* / TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch* fail on HEAD due to testutil.Factory id-prefix vs ParseSlug drift — confirmed by stashing the BE-0337 changes and reproducing the identical 23-test set on the base commit. NOT introduced by this pattern, owed to a focused triage story before BE-0500+ verification stories run.

- Pure N-scope inheritance resolver (BE-0343 pattern, internal/controlplane/variables/{doc.go, resolver.go, resolver_test.go, AGENTS.md}; structural template for any future hierarchy-aware merge engine over org/project/env/service — feature-flag inheritance, domain-config inheritance, rate-limit inheritance). Five load-bearing rules: (1) The resolver package owns NO I/O — it defines a scope-agnostic ScopedVariable input shape and a Resolver that operates purely on in-memory tuples. The caller fetches inputs through the per-scope *Reader adapters (internal/controlplane/store) and converts store types into the resolver's input shape. This decouples the merge contract from persistence and lets unit tests use hand-built fixtures without a fake DB. (2) Collect ALL field violations across ALL scopes in one pass and return them as one apierr.InvalidInput; do NOT short-circuit on the first violation — that is hostile to the agent contract. Field paths anchor on <scope>.variables[<index>].<field> and NEVER echo the submitted value. (3) Customer-input failures (invalid name, duplicate within scope, secret downgrade without opt-in) -> apierr.FieldViolation -> apierr.InvalidInput (HTTP 400). Server-side failures (Open ErrUnknownKey / wrapped ErrInvalidCiphertext / provider id mismatch) -> apierr.Internal with a wrapped cause for server-side logging (HTTP 500). The classification is load-bearing: customer-input means "fix your request"; Internal means "retry or escalate". (4) Three redaction views: (a) Resolved/Rendered are internal-only and carry verbatim plaintext for renderer/worker consumption; (b) LogValue redacts value and ciphertext at the slog boundary as defence-in-depth against stray slog captures; (c) Explained projects every value to output.Sentinel for customer-facing JSON envelopes, audit metadata, and operator diagnostics. Tests prove no error message, JSON marshal, or slog line contains source plaintext or ciphertext. (5) Closed-set Options.AllowSecretDowngrade opt-in: secret -> non-secret at a higher scope is REJECTED by default (apierr.InvalidInput); the opt-in path MUST be backed by a caller-emitted audit event recording the override. The asymmetry between upgrade (always allowed) and downgrade (rejected by default) is the safe direction — upgrading the classification strengthens the posture, downgrading weakens it. Two test-fixture gotchas the pattern documents: (a) whole-error redaction tests must choose fixture values that cannot collide with stable reason phrases (the first BE-0343 iteration used "duplicate" as a fixture value AND as part of the rejection reason — false positive); use distinctive suffixes like "fixture-value-one-zzy" to anchor the redaction invariant. (b) Tests against the Plaintext provider must use different strings for the plaintext-leak assertion and the ciphertext-leak assertion — Plaintext.Seal returns the input verbatim, so using the same string for both would tautology-pass under Plaintext but fail to catch a real leak under AESGCM. NOT applicable to the dokploy renderer's mergeVariables (renderer.go) — that path is intentionally separate because the renderer's Variable input is the deterministic spec, not the at-rest seal tuple, and it ALSO validates tier/role invariants. A future refactor could unify the two but is a distinct story (it changes the renderer's input contract).

- Security-verification two-test pattern, httpapi port (BE-0345 pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/{body_size_static_test.go, body_size_test.go, AGENTS.md}; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template — confirms the template ports cleanly across packages and surfaces the httpapi-specific knobs). Four httpapi-flavoured rules the store-flavoured BE-0344 entry does NOT capture: (a) the static analyzer's receiver-name allowlist (`r`, `req`, `request`) is what makes `<*http.Request>.Body` distinguishable from unrelated `<*http.Response>.Body` / `<mail.Message>.Body` selectors in the same package — the convention is uniform (`r` everywhere in httpapi today) so the `req`/`request` aliases are defensive against future rename; document the convention in the analyzer's package doc. (b) the allowed-shape set is a small closed set: validate.DecodeJSON arg, io.LimitReader arg, `BinaryExpr` nil compare (either side), `AssignStmt` LHS, and `CallExpr` with zero args whose method is `.Close()` (the `len(call.Args) == 0` check on Close is what keeps `.Close(someArg)` from accidentally passing — io.Closer.Close takes no args by definition). Each shape lives in its own `markAllowedIn<Kind>` helper so adding a new allowed shape is a single function, not a branching edit on a monolith. (c) the regression self-check covers BOTH known-bad AND known-good synthetic snippets — without the positive cases a future change that over-tightens the analyzer would slip past silently. (d) the runtime test reuses the existing `createOrganizationHandlerFor` / `postOrganizations` / `decodeError` helpers from `organizations_create_test.go`; the entire 4-case end-to-end test is ~120 lines because the handler-wiring helpers already exist for every mutating endpoint. New security-verification runtime tests in httpapi should look for an existing handler-builder in the same package before adding a new one. Acceptance criteria "Unit tests cover success, validation failure, authorization failure, and not-found behavior" maps onto body-size as: success = valid in-cap body decodes (201 yalla.output.v1), validation failure = over-cap body returns 400 E_INVALID_INPUT with "exceeds the maximum allowed size" message, authorization failure = oversized body from an unauthenticated caller is 401 E_AUTH (auth middleware runs before body decode), not-found-style = empty body is 400 E_INVALID_INPUT "must not be empty" (nothing to decode is the symbolic lower edge). All four assertions also pin the no-body-reflection invariant (the error message must NOT echo the submitted body — a 64-byte slice of the padding is the regression probe). Sibling BE-0346..BE-0363 security-verification stories continue to follow the BE-0344+BE-0345 template; the package that owns the invariant determines whether the analyzer scans store SQL or httpapi handlers or worker job inputs etc., but the two-test pair shape, the regression self-check shape, and the AGENTS.md section shape are identical.

- Security-verification two-test pattern (BE-0344 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/{sql_injection_static_test.go, sql_injection_test.go, AGENTS.md}; structural template for BE-0345..BE-0363 sibling security-verification stories and ANY future "this invariant must hold for every call site of a typed surface" verification — request body size, JSON parser hardening, log-redaction fuzzing, environment-variable redaction, audit tamper resistance, etc.). The shape is two complementary tests landing in the package that owns the invariant: (1) a STATIC AST analyzer test that walks every non-test .go file in the package and rejects any source-level shape that re-opens the invariant — runs without a database, fails the build for any regression, and includes a self-check sub-test that parses synthetic bad source snippets and asserts the analyzer reports the expected violations (the self-check satisfies the "tests fail when the control is removed" criterion independently of whether production code happens to be clean today; a future weakening of the analyzer that lets a real regression slip past would itself fail this test). (2) a RUNTIME integration test that runs against an isolated Postgres (testutil.RequireMigratedDB), feeds a canonical attack/probe corpus through every relevant input position, asserts each call returns through the typed error surface (NotFound, InvalidInput, Conflict — never E_UNAVAILABLE, which is the store's mapping for raw driver errors and the load-bearing negative assertion because a control bypass would surface there), AND asserts a post-loop integrity probe (row counts byte-identical to baseline, seeded fixtures readable byte-identical) — plus a verbatim round-trip test that proves the invariant boundary actually IS the pgx parameter binder by inserting each payload as a free-text column value and reading it back byte-identical. Four load-bearing rules: (a) the static analyzer accepts only compile-time-constant expressions (string literals, const-bound identifiers including function-local consts, parenthesised wrappers, +-concatenations of those) — var declarations are rejected because var is mutable and a safe value today could be reassigned to caller input later; the const/var distinction is the language guarantee that makes the rule load-bearing; (b) the analyzer encodes structural exemptions ONLY by receiver type + method name (e.g. *Tx Querier wrappers), never by file path or line number, so the allowlist cannot drift; (c) the runtime payload corpus deliberately omits inputs that fail at a lower protocol layer (NUL bytes for SQL injection — pgx rejects NUL in a text parameter before it reaches the server, surfacing as E_UNAVAILABLE for reasons unrelated to the invariant) so the load-bearing E_UNAVAILABLE negative assertion stays clean; document the exclusion explicitly in the test file's package-doc block; (d) the SQL-keyword scan in the analyzer is case-SENSITIVE and anchors on uppercase keywords — the codebase writes SQL uppercase, so case sensitivity costs nothing and eliminates prose false positives like "transition from %s to %s" or "returning the value"; the regex requires \S (not \w) after UPDATE/FROM so Sprintf templates like "UPDATE %s SET …" still match (% is not a word char). The package's AGENTS.md gets a new section above the existing patterns documenting the threat model, the two-test invariant, the structural exemption, and the deliberate corpus exclusions — so a future developer can find the rule without re-reading the test files. Pre-existing -race failures on the full package run (recordingAuthorizer races, Postgres max_connections saturation at 20×N goroutines) are not regressions; -race -run filtered to the new tests is clean. NOT applicable to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract with NO static analog (e.g. log redaction fuzzing, idempotency replay, chaos tests for timeouts) — those follow a single-test runtime-only shape; the two-test pattern applies when the invariant is also a source-code rule.

- Write-endpoint contract-test quadruple (BE-0110 pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/variables_put_contract_test.go; WRITE-endpoint variant of the BE-0095/BE-0101/BE-0107 read-endpoint contract-test triple — structural template for every P=2 "Add contract tests for {PUT,PATCH,DELETE,POST} .../{org_id}/X" story whose endpoint mutates state and whose request body MAY carry is_secret=true / API-key / token / cookie values). FOUR named tests close the contract gaps the base *_test.go + *_policy_test.go pair leaves open: (1) "server writes response data only through ResponseWriter" — wraps the served request in captureProcessOutput, asserts os.Stdout AND os.Stderr are empty, AND that the structured logger sink is non-empty, AND that the captured replacer/updater/patcher input carries the {org_id} path parameter (writes don't have a stable "no-data" envelope to spot-check, so the captured-input assertion replaces the read-endpoint's "data == seeded entry" assertion; this is what makes the WRITE quadruple different from the READ triple's first test). (2) "request log redacts the bearer credential" — a single test function with a `tests := []struct{...}` table over (success path, authorization failure path), each subtest builds a separate logger and asserts logBuf.String() and rec.Body.String() are both free of the contract secret. The deny leg uses a replacer/updater whose write-path errors if invoked, so a leaked "store error" cannot be the leak source — the deny must short-circuit at policy. (3) "request log redacts a submitted secret value" — variables-flavoured EXTRA pin the limits sibling (BE-0098) does NOT have. IDENTICAL table shape to (2), but the marker is a customer-submitted is_secret=true value embedded in the request body (yallasubmittedsecretMARKER0123456789FACEFEED). Headers-not-logged / body-not-logged contract applies symmetrically to the request body for write endpoints; a regression that starts dumping the decoded body into the log would silently spill every secret variable through this endpoint, so the contract pin guards against that regression on BOTH legs. The deny-leg leak detection is even more important than the success leg here: a leaked submitted secret in the deny log surface means the operator log holds a value the store layer never wrote — a leak the wire-level redaction CANNOT catch (no row exists to render with output.Sentinel). DROP pin (3) for write endpoints whose body has no submitted-secret to leak (DELETE variables, POST projects unless projects carry secrets in the create body, PATCH limits, etc.); KEEP for every variables/environment-variables/project-variables/service-variables PUT and PATCH. (4) "error envelope doesn't leak dependency cause" — apierr.StoreUnavailable wrapped around stderrors.New("connection refused dialing 10.0.0.5:5432"), asserts 503 / E_UNAVAILABLE / non-empty stable Message / cause string absent / host "10.0.0.5" absent from the body. Probe bytes are intentionally identical across sibling contract tests (read triple AND write quadruple) so the invariant is a single closed regression set, not a per-endpoint variant. The new helper this pattern requires is a "WithLogger" clone of the existing handler-builder that swaps the second-to-last NewHandler nil slot for the caller-supplied *slog.Logger — identical edit across every write endpoint, because the logger seam lives at the same position in NewHandler's parameter list regardless of which writer port the route depends on. Forward-applicable to BE-0113 (PATCH variables — keep all four pins; the PATCH body also carries is_secret=true), BE-0116 (DELETE variables — drop pin (3), DELETE body has no submitted secret), BE-0122 (POST projects — drop pin (3) unless the project create body carries secrets), and every future write-endpoint contract story. The only per-story knobs are the writer port type, the fake writer's field names (`vars`/`err`/`got` for replacers, `limits`/`err`/`got` for limits updaters, etc.), the canonical contract body, and the canonical contract-submitted-secret marker (use a distinct suffix per story so a regression that swaps two surfaces fails loudly — BE-0110 uses CAFEBABE, BE-0098 uses FEEDFACE for the bearer, future stories should pick distinct suffixes for BOTH the bearer credential and the submitted-secret marker).
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, on-every-PR required-step-INSERTION instance (BE-0401 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_release_build_static_test.go + internal/release/release_build_test.go's new canonical pair TestReleaseBuildCoversCallSites + TestReleaseBuildPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Release Build Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table (with When = "Every push and PR") + CONTRIBUTING.md entry 25 + Step 25 paragraph + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: release build tests (BE-0401)" subsection; TWENTY-THIRD worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0400 verification-suite mega-pattern AND THE FIRST WORKED INSERTION OF A NEW REQUIRED VERIFY.SH STEP INTO THE MIDDLE OF THE SEQUENCE (every prior BE-0379..BE-0399 instance appended its required step at the trailing end of the required block; BE-0401 inserts at #25 BETWEEN BE-0399's #24 and the existing optional block). Canonical command `go test -run TestReleaseBuild ./...`. The TWO BE-0401-specific shifts from the on-every-PR template are forward-applicable to every future required-step insertion (not just verification stories): (1) `INSERTING A REQUIRED VERIFY.SH STEP AT POSITION #N FORCES A RENUMBER OF EVERY OPTIONAL STEP #N..end IN scripts/verify.sh AND A SYNCHRONISED CONSTANT UPDATE IN EVERY PRIOR STATIC TEST THAT PINNED AN AFFECTED STEP HEADER` — for BE-0401, the verify.sh optional steps #25..#29 (govulncheck, staticcheck, golangci-lint, goreleaser check, external smoke) renumbered to #26..#30, AND `verification_suite_external_dokploy_smoke_static_test.go` (BE-0400) had to update `externalDokploySmokeVerifyShStepHeader` from `# 29. Optional: external live-Dokploy smoke tests` to `# 30. Optional…` AND the two synthetic-fixture strings inside the analyser's `TestVerificationSuiteExternalDokploySmokeAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` self-check (the `# 28. Optional: goreleaser check` known-bad fixture to `# 29. Optional…` and the `# 29. Optional: external live-Dokploy smoke tests` known-good fixture to `# 30. Optional…`) in the SAME edit. The renumber cost is bounded — only static tests that pin step headers in the affected range need updates — but a missed update fails the analyser self-check, NOT silently. Forward-applicable to every future required-step insertion: grep all `# N. Optional` and `# N. Required` literals in `internal/release/verification_suite_*_static_test.go` for the renumbered range and update each in the same commit; the AGENTS.md subsection for the inserting story documents the renumber as the shared cost. (2) `PURE PROJECTION HELPERS (NO LIVE INFRASTRUCTURE) STILL KEEP THE CANONICAL PAIR SHAPE` — the release package's `ArchiveExt`/`BinaryName`/`ArchiveName`/`ChecksumsName` are pure functions today, so the contention-burst member of the canonical pair would seem redundant. But the burst is the load-bearing stability check the gate's wire contract requires AND keeping the pair shape consistent across every BE-0379..BE-0401 gate makes the static analyser's canonical-funcs matcher reusable and the operator-facing AGENTS.md narrative consistent. Forward-applicable: every future verification-suite gate (pure-function or stateful) keeps the closed-set-coverage + per-decision-stability pair shape so the `-run TestX` filter binds to a two-function fixture the static gate can name, even when the burst's race tripwire is the only load-bearing reason for the second function to exist.

- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose mutation surface is Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion ONLY (NO UpdateDisplayName, NO Restore, NO ListByOrganization, NO CountByOrganization), with a database-managed version column, a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), a CHECK-constrained closed-set Kind column, and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0442 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0438 project_tenant_isolation_test.go template MINUS the UpdateDisplayName/Restore byte-identical-bystander pairs (the EnvironmentRepository exposes neither method) MINUS the ListByOrganization/CountByOrganization tenant-scope tests (environments are listed PER PROJECT, not per organization — quota counts environments per-project elsewhere, not on this repository). Three structural divergences from the projects template are load-bearing: (1) the ListByProject leg is the per-project analogue of ListByOrganization — the WHERE filter is (organization_id, project_id) instead of (organization_id), so the cross-tenant existence-leak projection is widened to a 3-case table (orgA owned empty project / orgA probing orgB project / orgA probing unknown project) that each yield a deterministic empty slice (NOT nil) AND ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE on the wire; the existing TestEnvironmentRepoListByProjectIsTenantScoped (environment_test.go) proves the cross-tenant projB.ID-under-orgA case alone, so this file's contribution is the symmetry guard across the OTHER two probes. (2) the shared-slug fixture ("production" on BOTH tenants' projects) exercises the per-PROJECT UNIQUE (project_id, slug), not the per-organization UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) of projects — two tenants legitimately share the slug because UNIQUE is anchored on the parent project (which differs per tenant), so the WHERE-on-(organization_id, project_id) filter is the only thing isolating the two rows in ListByProject; a regression that resolved the WHERE by slug alone or by project_id alone would surface here. (3) assertEnvironmentByteIdentical adds a Kind assertion on top of the projects template's column set — environments carries kind in its row body (CHECK-constrained to 'standard' or 'preview' by migration 0025) and the Update path SET clause MUST NOT touch it, so a regression that promoted kind to a SET clause is caught here in addition to the slug/display_name/version/updated_at/deletion_scheduled_at anchors. The deletion_scheduled_at switch/case carries the same three legs (nil->non-nil for ScheduleDeletion, non-nil->nil for a hypothetical Restore that does not exist on this repository — kept anyway as defensive coverage in case Restore is added later — and value-drift across non-nil baselines). The cross-tenant existence-leak probe on GetByID is the standard Code-AND-Hint identity assertion the BE-0438 template provides; this file adds NOTHING new on that surface — the contribution is the byte-identical-bystander projection on every mutation path (Update / ScheduleDeletion) AND the existence-leak symmetry-table on ListByProject AND the per-tenant view of soft-deleted rows. Helper `projATuple(store.Project) -> testutil.Project` is local to this file: the factory's Environment / Service builders take a testutil.Project, but the test seeds projects through the typed repository surface and gets back a store.Project — projATuple bridges the two shapes without a dependency on testutil internals (only ID, OrganizationID, Slug, Name are needed). The environmentFixture / insertEnvironment / getEnvironmentOrFail / assertEnvironmentByteIdentical helpers are file-scope: environmentFixture and insertEnvironment are reused from environment_repository_invariants_test.go (BE-0441 — do NOT re-implement); getEnvironmentOrFail and assertEnvironmentByteIdentical are NEW and named uniquely (no projects-template name collision in the shared store_test package). Pre-existing test failures on HEAD that are NOT introduced by this story: TestEnvironmentRepoGetByIDReturnsRow, TestEnvironmentReaderRejectsCrossTenantProjectID, and TestEnvironmentRepoListByProjectReturnsDeterministicOrdering all fail in isolation on the base commit (testutil.Factory slug-token drift vs hard-coded "production"/"staging" expectations in environment_test.go) — confirmed by stashing the BE-0442 file and reproducing identical failures. Owed to a focused triage story before BE-0500+ verification stories run.

- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, ONE optional nullable scoping pointer (service_id) instead of two (no environment_id pointer because the env IS the parent), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, environment_id) -> environments** (BE-0443 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). This is the BE-0439 project_grants template adapted for environment_grants. Three structural divergences from BE-0439 are load-bearing: (1) the EnvironmentGrant struct has NO ProjectID column and ONE optional pointer (ServiceID) instead of two (EnvironmentID is the mandatory FK target, not an optional scope leg) — the Upsert insert-pointer-roundtrip test collapses from two opt-in pointers to one (service-scoped grant). (2) The ON CONFLICT target is the unique index environment_grants_principal_scope_idx on (organization_id, environment_id, principal_id, COALESCE(service_id, '')) — there is no environment_id leg in the COALESCE because the environment is the parent scope, not an optional narrower; the duplicate-PK Conflict test uses a different principal_id under the same environment to dodge the scope-tuple branch and exercise the PRIMARY KEY (id) violation. (3) The CASCADE-delete-of-parent test deletes the environments row directly via raw SQL (NOT the EnvironmentRepository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a soft delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observes via ListByEnvironment. Helper newEnvironmentGrantID synthesises an `egrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Environment's `env_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.EnvironmentGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindEnvironmentGrant). Sentinel type `environmentGrantTxRollbackSentinel` is distinct from every other *_test.go file's rollback sentinel in store_test. List-helper name `listEnvironmentGrantsOrFail` is distinct from project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go's `listGrantsOrFail` to avoid same-package collision. Test name prefix `TestEnvironmentGrantRepository*` (full Repository) is distinct from environment_grant_test.go's `TestEnvironmentGrantRepo*` and `TestEnvironmentGrantReader*` siblings — no collisions.
- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED short-lived claim/ledger table whose ONLY repository mutation surface is an InsertReservation (one-shot active-row INSERT) plus a single resolver read (SumActiveReservations), with NO per-row Update/Delete/Get, NO version column, a settled_consistent CHECK pairing status<>'active' with NON-NULL settled_at, a CHECK amount>0, a composite-FK out (organization_id, job_id) -> provisioning_jobs added by a LATER migration than the row's own migration, and a database-managed bump-only set_updated_at trigger fired BEFORE UPDATE** (BE-0455 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0453 (quota_usage -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) and BE-0451 (quota_policies -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in six load-bearing ways: (a) the row-shape test must anchor BOTH a nullable-pointer JobID (zero on a no-JobID call, *string round-trip on a non-empty-JobID call) AND a nullable-pointer SettledAt (zero on every active-row insert -- there is no public surface that can mint a settled row); (b) the JobID round-trip leg cannot use a synthetic id alone because migration 0008 added a composite FK quota_reservations_job_fk -> provisioning_jobs (organization_id, id), so the test MUST pre-seed a real provisioning_jobs row (`INSERT INTO provisioning_jobs (id, organization_id, job_type, idempotency_key) VALUES (...)`) before InsertReservation -- a regression that dropped the FK or swapped its column order would surface here; (c) the settled_consistent CHECK violation has NO independent client-input surface (settled_at is not in the INSERT column list of InsertReservation), so the test exploits the OTHER direction: passing Status=ReservationStatusCommitted (non-blank, non-'active') sends settled_at=NULL via the default and the CHECK rejects it, proving InsertReservation can ONLY mint ACTIVE rows; (d) the time-filter leg of SumActiveReservations is a SECOND-AXIS exclusion test alongside the status filter -- a row with status='active' AND expires_at<=now must be excluded by the `expires_at > $3` predicate, so the test pre-seeds an active-but-time-expired row and asserts it is excluded WITHOUT mutating its status (a regression that dropped the time predicate would silently sum already-expired rows); (e) the resource-filter leg seeds TWO active reservations under the SAME tenant with DIFFERENT resources and asserts BOTH `SumActiveReservations(org, A, now)` and `SumActiveReservations(org, B, now)` return their own resource's amount (a regression that dropped the resource predicate would return 11 instead of 3 from either call); (f) the duplicate-ID Conflict test uses an explicit caller-supplied ID instead of relying on the qres_ prefix-mint path, because the PK violation is the constraint and a regression that silently re-minted the explicit id would mask the test (the explicit-ID-preservation test PRECEDES the duplicate-ID test and pins the no-remint contract). Forward-applicable to every future short-lived-claim repository whose public surface is a single one-shot active-row INSERT plus a resolver-read (a hypothetical lease-claim table KindResourceLease with active->held|released|expired, or a future job_attempts row whose public surface is a single INSERT per attempt). Test-helper naming caveat at package store_test: every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same package, so the type name `quotaReservationTxRollbackSentinel` must be distinct from quotaPolicyTxRollbackSentinel / quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel / projectTxRollbackSentinel and friends -- grep `TxRollbackSentinel` before adding a new sentinel. Helper REUSE strategy: this file DEFINES its own `runInsertReservationOrFail` / `loadQuotaReservationRowByID` / `countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg` / `rawQuotaReservationRow` (no shared symbols to reuse), and REUSES the existing `insertReservationRow` / `insertSettledReservationRow` helpers defined in quota_repository_test.go for the SumActiveReservations setup legs because those raw-SQL inserts can stamp the schema's required `settled_at = now()` for non-active rows without going through the repository's active-only mint path.
- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED CLAIM-PROTOCOL TABLE WHOSE FOUR REPOSITORY SURFACES (Claim upsert with steal-expired branch / Complete state-transitioning UPDATE / Release state-conditioned DELETE / Find composite-tuple read) ALL CARRY organization_id AS THE LEADING COLUMN OF A COMPOSITE PREDICATE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES NULLABLE COMPLETION COLUMNS (response_status / response_body / completed_at) DISTINGUISHED BY NIL-NESS UNDER A COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK, AND WHOSE INVARIANTS FILE ALREADY LANDED IN THE IMMEDIATELY-PRIOR PRD STORY** (BE-0470 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0468 (audit_events) cross-tenant template with seven load-bearing deltas: (a) there is NO ListByOrganization read surface — Find is the only read, scoped to the full (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple — so the "cross-tenant list returns only own rows" probe collapses to "cross-tenant Find returns (zero, false, nil)" with a per-tenant count assertion on both sides, AND the "limit clamping is tenant-scoped" probe drops entirely (no list cap exists). The file's package-doc header MUST document the absence so a future reader does not look for a missing test, mirroring the BE-0426 organizations file's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" doc-only delegation; (b) the byte-identity comparator MUST handle three nullable pointer-bearing columns (*int for response_status, []byte for response_body, *time.Time for completed_at) where the pending-vs-completed lifecycle distinction is observable through nil-ness. A simple field-equality loop will silently round-trip a regression that flipped a pending row's NULL completion columns to zero-value scalars (e.g. response_status=0, response_body=[]byte{}, completed_at=epoch). The pattern: switch on (after==nil, baseline==nil) into three branches — both-nil 