ralph/progress.txt:4:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + READER ADAPTER + READER ADAPTER TEST ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND-ONLY WITH ONLY ONE WRITE SURFACE (Append) AND ONE READ SURFACE (ListByOrganization, no GetByID) AND A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE INSERT COLUMN LIST DELIBERATELY OMITS occurred_at + created_at (so DEFAULT now() applies and caller-supplied timestamps are SILENTLY DISCARDED)** (BE-0467 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_invariants_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0461 (provisioning_jobs) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the typed repository is the NARROWEST in the store package — Append + ListByOrganization only, NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown). The acceptance-criteria phrase "create typed repository methods for creating, reading, updating, and deleting ... where the lifecycle requires it" narrows to "Append + List only; the audit log lifecycle structurally rejects per-row Update / Delete" and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap; (b) the audit_events INSERT column list (auditEventColumns minus occurred_at/created_at) deliberately omits both timestamp columns, so DEFAULT now() applies regardless of caller. A test `TestAuditRepositoryAppendIgnoresCallerSuppliedTimestamps` is the regression backstop — a future refactor that added occurred_at to the INSERT (so a caller could back-date an audit record) would silently break the "audit timestamps are server-side" guarantee unless this test was also updated. The probe seeds OccurredAt=year-2000 and asserts the persisted row's OccurredAt is within [now-1s, now+1s], NOT the back-dated value; (c) CHECK constraints that the application does NOT pre-validate (blank action / blank resource_kind / blank reason / actor_kind outside the closed-set ('', 'usr', 'sa', 'system')) MUST be exercised: Append does NOT pre-check these fields (the audit service is the chokepoint for the principal-mapping inputs), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces as typed `apierr.Conflict` via mapWriteError. Each test asserts BOTH the error code AND `countAuditRowsForOrg(org.ID) == 0` to pin "the rejection left no row behind"; (d) the repository-level limit cap (`auditEventListMaxLimit = 200`) is a public invariant of the ListByOrganization contract and MUST be exercised in three branches — non-positive (limit <= 0 -> 200), above-cap (limit > 200 -> 200), in-range (limit in (0, 200] honoured verbatim). A regression that returned the empty slice when limit <= 0 (or every row when limit was unbounded) would surface here. The seeding helper `seedAuditRowsRawSQL` bypasses the repository and uses direct INSERT with caller-controlled occurred_at so 201 rows can be planted in O(n) without sleeping; (e) the secondary-sort ORDER BY id DESC tiebreaker on tied occurred_at is the load-bearing list-ordering invariant: a UI rendering a pair of audit events written in the same Postgres microsecond would flicker without it. The probe seeds TWO rows with the SAME occurred_at (via raw INSERT — Append's DEFAULT now() would advance between calls) and asserts listed[0].ID > listed[1].ID lexicographically. The 16-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + aud_ prefix + server-side timestamps within [before, after]), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-ignores-caller-supplied-timestamps (the load-bearing server-side-timestamp probe), four CHECK-violation Conflicts (blank-action, blank-resource_kind, blank-reason, unknown-actor_kind), duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-transaction-rollback (sentinel error + countAuditRowsForOrg == 0), Append-cascades-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/organization_id/action/reason/occurred_at/created_at), Append-peer-row-byte-identical (the BE-0468 byte-identity bystander predecessor — peer Append must NOT touch a prior row), List-orders-id-DESC-on-timestamp-tie, three List-limit-clamping branches (non-positive, above-cap, in-range), List-unknown-org-returns-empty. New helpers (per-file Audit-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg (the per-org counter — organization is the only parent FK for audit_events, so this is also the natural per-parent counter), mintAuditID, auditTxRollbackSentinel, seedAuditRowsRawSQL (the raw-INSERT batch seeder for cap-boundary tests). Reused verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit, assertAuditEventByteIdentical; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table is append-only with a single (Append, List) surface and a repository-level limit cap (a future security_events / system_audit table would inherit the same template, including the server-side-timestamp invariant if the INSERT column list omits the timestamp legs).
ralph/progress.txt:8:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE SCHEMA ALREADY EXISTS FROM AN EARLY MIGRATION BUT WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY IS THE FIRST STORY-LEVEL CALLER** -- i.e. the table landed in an ancient migration (dokploy_refs in 0002_tenant_hierarchy) and has been exercised only by raw-SQL schema_test.go tests; the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story creates BOTH the *_repository.go file AND the *_repository_invariants_test.go file from scratch (BE-0465 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref.go + internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref_repository_invariants_test.go). Five load-bearing differences from a first-story-for-table CRUD vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0463 job_attempts, etc.): (a) NO migration is added because the table already exists -- grep the schema_test.go assertions BEFORE writing to learn the column shape and the CHECK / UNIQUE / FK invariants the raw-SQL tests already pin; (b) the typed repository MUST NOT add columns the table lacks even if a comment in another migration suggests otherwise -- migration 0011_optimistic_versions's preamble claims to cover dokploy_refs 'for parity' but its actual ALTER statements only touch organizations / projects / environments / services, so the DokployRef struct MUST NOT carry a Version field and the repository MUST NOT include version in dokployRefColumns or scan it (the single fastest way to discover this kind of drift is the error-chain unwrap debug pattern: write a one-shot test that prints %T:%s for each `cur.(unwrapper).Unwrap()` layer, which surfaces the underlying *pgconn.PgError with the SQLSTATE 42703 'column X does not exist' message in one round trip); (c) if the table has NO customer-mutable column the typed repository surface is INSERT + GET + LIST + DELETE only -- no Update method, no optimistic-concurrency If-Match path -- and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap (the 'optimistic versioning where applicable' acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to 'not applicable here'); (d) when the table has a bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY (the FIRST and ONLY int64 PK in the store package), the typed struct's ID field is int64 (not string), the INSERT statement OMITS id from the column list entirely (any value supplied -- even NULL or DEFAULT -- trips SQLSTATE 428C9 'cannot insert a non-DEFAULT value into column "id"'), and the typed error payload renders the int64 id via a private package-local helper rather than importing strconv -- keeping the store package's 'no strconv' invariant intact. A test InsertIgnoresCallerSuppliedID is the regression backstop (a caller that smuggles ID: 999_999_999 must still see the database-minted id in the returned struct); (e) when the table has TWO UNIQUE constraints, one GLOBAL across tenants (e.g. (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) on dokploy_refs -- a Dokploy object cannot be claimed twice across tenants) and one PER-TENANT (e.g. (organization_id, yalla_id, dokploy_resource, dokploy_id)), BOTH must be exercised explicitly by the invariants tests -- the global-UNIQUE test must seed orgA's row then try to insert orgB's row with the SAME (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) and assert countRowsForOrg(orgB) == 0 after the rejection. The 19-test shape this file lands: Insert-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal), Insert-ignores-caller-supplied-id, four typed-Internal rejections (nil-tx + blank-closed-set-args), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-yalla_kind, unknown-dokploy_resource, blank-yalla_id, blank-dokploy_id, unknown-organization_id), both UNIQUE violations (global + per-tenant), positive-direction 'multiple domains for same service all persist' (pins the per-tenant UNIQUE includes dokploy_id), Insert-rollback, Get-happy + Get-unknown, GetByDokployTarget-happy + unknown + blank-resource-Internal, ListByYallaResource-ordered + empty + blank-kind-Internal, ListByOrganization-ordered + empty, Delete-happy + unknown + nil-tx, organization-delete-cascade, and a raw-SQL set_updated_at trigger pin (no bump_version trigger exists despite migration 0011's preamble). New helpers introduced (per-file DokployRef-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawDokployRefRow, loadDokployRefRowByID, countDokployRefRowsForOrg, mintDokployID, runInsertDokployRefOrFail, dokployRefTxRollbackSentinel. Reused verbatim: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0466 story. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table landed in an early migration and has been carried by raw-SQL schema tests since (a future idempotency_keys repository would inherit the same template, though without the bigint identity PK twist).
ralph/progress.txt:40:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, lifecycle-projection-chokepoint instance (BE-0408 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_deployment_lifecycle_e2e_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/deployment_lifecycle_e2e_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestDeploymentLifecycleE2ECoversCallSites + TestDeploymentLifecycleE2EPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Deployment Lifecycle End-to-End Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: deployment lifecycle end-to-end tests (BE-0408)" subsection; THIRTIETH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0407 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestDeploymentLifecycleE2E ./...`, CI step name `Deployment lifecycle end-to-end tests`, verify.sh prefix #32 inserted between BE-0407's #31 and the trailing optionals renumbered #32-#36 → #33-#37 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 36 → # 37` and synthetic-fixture `# 35 → # 36` updated to match, plus BE-0400's trailing-optional commentary `#32-#35 → #33-#36`). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST lifecycle-projection chokepoint (a slog.LogValuer pure projection on a closed-set lifecycle-state-machine row): (1) `slog TEXT AND JSON HANDLERS BOTH EMIT A time= ATTRIBUTE THAT BREAKS DETERMINISTIC ROUND-TRIP ASSERTIONS — INSTALL A ReplaceAttr HOOK THAT DROPS slog.TimeKey AT THE TOP-LEVEL GROUP ONLY (return slog.Attr{} when len(groups)==0 && a.Key==slog.TimeKey)`. Scope the drop to top-level groups so a customer-named attribute called `time` inside the projected group is still asserted on. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a slog.LogValuer and whose canonical pair asserts repeat-call determinism on the rendered slog form. (2) `LIFECYCLE CHOKEPOINTS (closed-set rows whose terminal-vs-non-terminal enum classification implies a database CHECK on a sibling timestamp column) MUST PIN THE DATABASE-CHECK INVARIANT AT THE GO PROJECTION INSIDE THE CANONICAL PAIR's CLOSED-SET SELF-CHECK SEQUENCE`. The deployments_finished_consistent table CHECK becomes self-check #4 here: non-terminal status (queued, running) ⇒ nil FinishedAt; terminal status (succeeded, failed, cancelled, rolled_back) ⇒ set FinishedAt. Otherwise an in-memory regression that violates the CHECK only surfaces against a live Postgres; targeted canonical-pair gates do not. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a row from a table whose closed-set status column has a sibling-column CHECK constraint. (3) `THE STRUCTURAL WHITELIST/BLACKLIST ASSERTION FOR A slog.LogValuer SHOULD DRIVE THROUGH THE JSON HANDLER (not the text handler) SO A REGRESSION THAT SWITCHED THE HANDLER STILL TRIPS THE CONTRACT`. The redaction contract isn't "this text never contains this string" (defeated by an escape change); it's "this group projects these keys and not these keys". JSON-route the projection, json.Unmarshal into map[string]any, assert keys whitelist + blacklist. The text-handler canary still fires for the secret-marker absence canary; both surfaces should be asserted side-by-side. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a slog.LogValuer with a documented field whitelist. One canonical-pair-package gotcha worth pinning forward: `WHEN THE CHOKEPOINT'S EXPORTED API LIVES ON A VALUE RECEIVER OF A TYPE DECLARED IN THE PARENT PACKAGE (here store.Deployment with LogValue() on the value receiver), THE CANONICAL PAIR FILE GOES IN package <parent>_test AND IMPORTS THE PARENT PACKAGE — mirrors BE-0379..BE-0407 convention; no white-box `package store` test needed`. The chokepoint is `package store_test`'s view of `(store.Deployment).LogValue()`, with helper renders (`deploymentLifecycleRenderLogValue`, `deploymentLifecycleStructuredLogValue`) wrapping the slog API. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:41:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-chokepoint instance (BE-0407 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_service_desired_state_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/service_desired_state_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestServiceDesiredStateCoversCallSites + TestServiceDesiredStatePreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Service Desired-State Golden Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: service desired-state golden tests (BE-0407)" subsection; TWENTY-NINTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0406 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestServiceDesiredState ./...`, CI step name `Service desired-state golden tests`, verify.sh prefix #31 inserted between BE-0406's #30 and the trailing optionals renumbered #31-#35 → #32-#36 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 35 → # 36` and synthetic-fixture `# 34 → # 35` updated to match). Two forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-no-context-no-port chokepoint instance — the chokepoint is `(*dokploy.Renderer).Render(in RenderInput) (RenderedSpec, error)` which is a pure function of its input alone (no context, no I/O, no port interfaces, no Repository): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH RENDERER PER ITERATION VIA dokploy.NewRenderer() — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE CONSTRUCTION IS CHEAP AND THE CONTRACT IS "NewRenderer THEN Render"`. The renderer has no port interfaces at construction time, so a shared instance would also work, but the per-iteration construction pins the documented API contract verbatim and surfaces a regression that smuggled in a sync.Once mutating a per-call map, a sync.Pool reused without resetting, or any cached per-instance view as a per-iteration mismatch even when an aggregate pass count matched. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-instance method (`pkg.NewX().Method(args)` style) where the constructor is cheap and takes no ports: build a fresh chokepoint per iteration via the documented constructor; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's method-internal pure logic has no package-level shared state AND that the documented constructor stays cheap and reusable. (2) `THE VALUE-FREE PROJECTION CANARY SEEDS THE SECRET MARKER INTO EVERY LEVEL OF THE VARIABLE HIERARCHY (org/project/env/service) AND ASSERTS MARKER-ABSENCE IN THREE REDACTED SURFACES: Summary JSON, slog LogValue group, AND Summary.String() form`. Unlike BE-0406's single-surface canary (ProposedDisplay survival + closed-set-tag absence), the renderer's redaction contract spans three concrete projection surfaces — the JSON-marshalled Summary, the slog handler's text output, and the single-line String form — because each is a separate code path the redactor must enforce. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits a type that implements slog.LogValuer alongside an explicit Summary projection: drive the marker-absence canary through ALL THREE projection surfaces (encoding/json output, slog.NewTextHandler captured output, and any documented String form), not just the JSON; a regression in any one surface ships to a different operator audience (audit pipeline vs structured log handler vs ad-hoc fmt.Println) and pinning all three end-to-end keeps the redaction contract bound to the public API, not just the wire format. One renderer-canonical gotcha worth pinning forward: `domain.ID FIXTURES IN THE dokploy_test PACKAGE MUST USE A 26-CHAR CROCKFORD-BASE32-LOWERCASE SUFFIX EXCLUDING i, l, o, u; the canonical pair uses serviceDesiredStateFixedID(domain.KindXxx, last byte ∈ {a, b, c, d}) — last bytes from the safe set keep determinism self-check #6's reflect.DeepEqual stable across runs without invented IDs that LOOK 26-char-valid but carry excluded letters. The canonical pair file lives in `package dokploy_test` (mirrors BE-0406's `package migrateimport_test`) — the chokepoint is exported (Render is a public method on Renderer) so the canonical pair stays in the _test package next to renderer_test.go rather than the package-internal store_test convention used by the BE-0404 break-glass pair. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:54:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, idempotency-replay instance (BE-0395 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_idempotency_replay_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/idempotency_replay_test.go's new canonical pair TestIdempotencyReplayCoversCallSites + TestIdempotencyReplayPreservesByteIdenticalEnvelope + SECURITY.md "## Idempotency Replay Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: idempotency replay tests (BE-0395)" subsection; SEVENTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0394 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestIdempotencyReplay ./...`, CI step name `Idempotency replay tests`, verify.sh prefix #20 inserted between BE-0394's #19 and the trailing optionals renumbered 20->21/21->22/22->23/23->24 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0394's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0395 substring count = 18; up from BE-0394's 16 because the idempotency-replay scope adds three closed-set error codes E_INVALID_INPUT / E_FORBIDDEN / E_NOT_FOUND and the Idempotency-Replayed/Idempotency-Key wire-header pair, and drops the chaos-Postgres-specific fakepg / DisconnectFault / yerr.CodeUnavailable / apierr.DependencyStore / apierr.StoreUnavailable constants in favour of the replay-specific fakeIdempotencyStore and byte-identical anchors), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0395-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is an HTTP middleware with an existing test corpus in the same Go package: (1) `EXISTING IN-PACKAGE TEST FIXTURES MUST BE REUSED, NOT FORKED` — the canonical pair lives in `package httpapi` (not `httpapi_test`) deliberately so the existing newFakeIdempotencyStore, idemRef, recordingHandler, jsonHandler, postReq, run, decodeError, and idemPrincipal helpers from idempotency_test.go and middleware_test.go are reused verbatim. The closed-set bound on Claim/Complete/Release counters is part of the fakeIdempotencyStore contract and a forked helper would silently break that bound. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is an HTTP middleware with an existing test corpus in the same package: extend, don't fork. The siblings BE-0396..BE-0404 should look for an existing `fake<Subject>Store` / `recording<Subject>` helper in the same package before adding a new one — the prior security-verification BE-0344+BE-0345 pattern said the same about handler-builders. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE MUST DOCUMENT THE EXCLUSION` — the replay closed set is success + 400/403/404 error envelopes; 5xx is deliberately excluded because the middleware releases the claim on 5xx (the contract is TestRequireIdempotencyServerErrorIsNotRecorded). Both the canonical pair's package-doc block AND the SECURITY.md section's Scope paragraph spell out the exclusion explicitly, so a future reader understands the asymmetry without re-reading the middleware. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set scenario table has a deliberate gap: name the gap in the section comment + SECURITY.md so a future contributor doesn't "fix" the apparent omission. (3) `BYTE-LITERAL RECORDED BODY, NOT STRUCTURAL JSON COMPARISON` — the runtime burst seeds a literal byte slice `{"schema_version":"yalla.output.v1","ok":true,...}` and asserts `got.body == string(recorded)`, not reflect.DeepEqual on json.Unmarshal'd maps. A structural comparison would mask a whitespace drift (an envelope re-rendered on replay would parse semantically equivalent but byte-different). Forward-applicable to every replay/recording invariant: assert on bytes, not parsed structures, when the contract is byte-identical determinism. Burst sizing: replayWorkers=4 × replayIterationsPerWorker=8 = 32 concurrent retries per run, enough to surface a non-deterministic recorder under -race without bloating CI wall-clock. The runtime burst observes per-iteration {status, body, header} and asserts post-burst aggregation in the main goroutine (no testing.T calls from workers — same shape as BE-0392's burst pattern).
ralph/progress.txt:55:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, chaos-Postgres-disconnects instance (BE-0394 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_chaos_postgres_disconnects_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/chaos_postgres_disconnects_test.go's new canonical pair TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsCoversCallSites + TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsMapsToTypedUnavailableEnvelope + new internal/controlplane/store/fakepg package (Server + DisconnectFault + NewServer) + SECURITY.md "## Chaos Tests For Postgres Disconnects" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: chaos tests for Postgres disconnects (BE-0394)" subsection; SIXTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0393 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestChaosPostgresDisconnects ./...`, CI step name `Chaos tests for Postgres disconnects`, verify.sh prefix #19 inserted between BE-0393's #18 and the trailing optionals renumbered 19->20/20->21/21->22/22->23 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0393's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0394 substring count = 16; matches BE-0393's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0394-specific extensions to the chaos-classification template, all forward-applicable to ANY future chaos-Postgres / chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit sibling whose subject is the store layer or any pgxpool-backed dependency: (1) `NEW IN-PROCESS FAKE NETWORK ENDPOINT FOR PGX` — unlike BE-0393's dokployfake (which exists as a long-standing HTTP test double under internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake), there was no fakepg before this story. The minimal viable shape is a TCP listener on 127.0.0.1:0 that does NOT speak the Postgres wire protocol — it accepts the TCP connection (so pgx's dial completes successfully — important: graceful FIN, NOT RST via SetLinger(0), because RST races the dial on some kernels and surfaces as `connection reset by peer` on connect rather than the production failure mode of "dial succeeds, handshake fails") and then closes the conn, so pgx observes EOF on its startup-handshake read. The fake exposes a FIFO Fault queue (only DisconnectFault for now), a Host()/Port()/Addr() trio for DSN construction, an AcceptedConnections() counter so the harness can assert pgx made >=1 TCP attempt per op (a regression that short-circuited pgx without ever attempting a network connect would otherwise pass silently), Close() (idempotent), New(testing.TB) for tests inside the testing package, and NewServer() error for worker-goroutine constructors that cannot satisfy testing.TB. Forward-applicable to every future chaos-Postgres sibling: add SQLErrorFault (writes an ErrorResponse before closing — would require pgproto3 dependency from pgx), LatencyFault (delays the close), AuthFailFault (writes a FATAL ErrorResponse during startup). (2) `CLASSIFIER-WRAP CONTRACT, NOT END-TO-END CALLSITE WRAP` — unlike BE-0393's chaos test which exercises real production methods on the typed Dokploy client (c.GetDeployment, c.EnsureOrganization, c.RemoveService) so the test asserts on errors the client's PRODUCTION wrapping chokepoint already produced, BE-0394's chaos test exercises raw pgxpool ops (Pool.Ping, Pool.Acquire, Pool.Begin, Pool.Exec, Pool.Query) and EXPLICITLY wraps the resulting pgx error through apierr.StoreUnavailable inside the runtime member before asserting the contract. This is because the store layer does not currently route every call site through a single classifier chokepoint the way the Dokploy client does — store call sites return raw pgx errors and the wrapping happens at handler-shim boundaries. The chaos contract therefore pins the wrapper's behaviour given realistic pgx-derived errors, not every call site's wrapping behaviour. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose subject lacks a single production classifier chokepoint: assert the wrapper given realistic causes, not the production path. When a future story (BE-04xx) adds a store-layer classifier chokepoint, the chaos test can switch to invoking that chokepoint instead — the scenario table and the redaction/dependency/code assertions stay identical. (3) `ASYMMETRIC REDACTION CONTRACT — PASSWORD AT EVERY LEVEL, USERNAME AT WRAPPER ONLY` — pgx's connect error formatter intentionally includes the DSN username in error messages (for operator debuggability) but redacts the password. The chaos test's chaosPostgresSecretMarkers (cause-chain scan) therefore lists ONLY the password sentinel; the wrapper-envelope assertion (chaosPostgresEnvelopeMarkers) lists BOTH the password and the username sentinel because apierr.StoreUnavailable's rendered message is static ("the Yalla datastore is temporarily unavailable") and MUST NOT echo ANY pgx cause-chain content. The runtime member walks errors.Unwrap chain for the password marker AND asserts the wrapper envelope's rendered Error() string for both markers. Forward-applicable: any future chaos test whose subject is a wire-protocol library that includes debug info in errors should split the redaction contract into chain-level (real secrets only) + envelope-level (anything pgx-style libraries might leak into debug strings). The sentinel-literal approach (chaosPostgresSentinelPassword="yallachaossecret_DO_NOT_LEAK_42") keeps the assertion meaningful: if the wrapper EVER started embedding the cause's message, the sentinel would surface and the gate would trip on the first observation. Per-iteration setup cost is bounded by chaosPostgresConnectTimeout=750ms (per-attempt pgx connect deadline) which is strictly less than chaosPostgresOpTimeout=2s (per-op context deadline), so the burst (5 scenarios × 4 workers × 3 iterations = 60 iterations) completes in ~0.5s wall-clock.
ralph/progress.txt:114:- Log-redaction fuzz harness, single-test runtime-only variant (BE-0359 pattern, internal/output/redact_fuzz_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Log Redaction Fuzzing" section + internal/output/AGENTS.md "Log redaction fuzzing (BE-0359)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the single-test runtime-only shape called out in the BE-0344 pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract with NO static analog, e.g. log redaction fuzzing, idempotency replay, chaos tests for timeouts). Six load-bearing rules: (1) FUZZ-MARKER design — when proving a structural redaction rule scrubs the value-half of a transport pattern, bracket the fuzz-supplied value with a unique fixed marker (`"FUZZAUTHMARKERXYZ"`, `"FUZZQRYMARKERZYX"`) and assert the marker never appears in the redacted output. The marker is the leak detector, independent of the value's bytes. Asserting that the value-bytes do not appear false-positives whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the test's static suffix (e.g. `"example"` if the test constructs `Host: example.com`). The marker pattern is reusable for ANY future fuzz target that proves "structural rule X scrubs the value half of transport Y" — cookies, refresh tokens, future header/query patterns. (2) REGEX-VALUE-CLASS gotcha — Go's `regexp` `\s` is exactly `[\t\n\f\r ]`; vertical-tab (`\v`) is NOT in `\s` but form-feed (`\f`) IS. When constructing a fuzz value that must traverse a regex value class without being cut short, strip every character in the regex's exclusion set including every member of Go's `\s`. The first revision missed `\f` and a seed containing it ended the regex mid-value, surviving the marker bracket and producing a false-positive leak. (3) DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH interactions — when two structural rules can both fire on a fuzz value (e.g. a query value containing `AUTHORIZATION:` triggers the bearer regex which eats the rest of the URL), assert ONLY the safety property (marker absent), NOT cosmetic properties (non-secret params survive). Document the interaction in the test file so a future reader understands why the survival assertion is absent. (4) SENTINEL-OVERLAP scope-out — `Redactor.Redact` uses `strings.ReplaceAll(s, secret, Sentinel)`; if `secret` is a substring of `Sentinel` (e.g. `"REDA"` inside `"[REDACTED]"`), the second pass rewrites the freshly-inserted sentinel substring and the function is NOT idempotent. Skip these inputs in fuzz AND pin the exclusion in a regression test (`TestRedactor_FuzzScopeExclusionsAreReal`) so a future redactor fix (single-pass regex substitution that does not re-scan substituted text) deliberately breaks that test and forces a coordinated update to the fuzz scope, the SECURITY.md section, and the AGENTS.md note. (5) SEED-CORPUS vs `-fuzz` — `go test ./...` runs every seed in `f.Add` once, so the seed corpus is the regression net every commit exercises; `-fuzz` is operator-opt-in. When adding a new redaction rule, the worked pattern is: append a seed input to `redactFuzzSeeds` that triggers the new rule, add a fuzz target if the rule has a distinct structural shape, and update both the AGENTS.md and SECURITY.md sections at the same time. (6) `-fuzztime` SHUTDOWN quirk — `go test -fuzz=… -fuzztime=5s` with the default 16 workers sometimes reports `context deadline exceeded` at shutdown; this is a harness-internal timing quirk, not a real fuzz failure (no `testdata/fuzz/…` corpus file is written). Re-running with `-parallel=2` makes the shutdown cleaner. Forward-applicable to any future security-verification story whose invariant has no AST shape to gate (BE-0360 environment-variable redaction if the rule is purely "the variables service MUST pass every rendered value through the redactor before logging" with no AST shape; BE-0363 rate-limit bypass resistance; chaos-tests for timeouts; idempotency replay).
ralph/progress.txt:125:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfiguration that silently reduces the rotation pool is caught fast. (4) cmd/yalla-api wires the provider in ONE buildSecretsProvider helper near runStartupChecks, NOT one per scope. Strict profiles (staging/production) refuse to start without YALLA_SECRET_KEYS (config.requireStrictFields enforces) via an explicit sentinel error; permissive profiles (local/test) fall back to Plaintext with a Warn log line. (5) The interface is INTENTIONALLY same-width every scope needs (org/project/env/service variables), so a single provider configured at startup covers every variables table — sibling at-rest stories become mechanical clones of schema + service + test changes. (6) For internal-only read paths that legitimately need the plaintext (the future Dokploy renderer worker, BE-0343 variable-inheritance resolver), Open the sealed tuple through the same Provider passed into the service — NEVER a wire-facing endpoint. Six load-bearing rules for the storage shape per table: (1) Add three nullable columns (secret_provider text NULL, secret_key_id text NULL, secret_ciphertext bytea NULL) ALONGSIDE the existing plain `value` column, never instead of it; force value='' for secret rows and cipher columns NULL for non-secret rows via ONE CHECK constraint named <table>_secret_columns_consistent that pins the invariant in both directions. The CHECK is the defence-in-depth backstop the service relies on — a bug that tries to write a plaintext secret rolls back as deterministic apierr.Conflict rather than silent corruption. (2) Mirror DO $$ guards in BOTH the up and the down migration that REFUSE to run while is_secret=true rows exist with the wrong shape — a re-application against a database that accumulated plaintext secrets fails loudly rather than silently leaving them behind, and a rollback against a database with sealed rows fails loudly rather than silently losing every secret value. (3) Add a partial index on (secret_provider, secret_key_id) WHERE is_secret=true so the future key-rotation worker (BE-0351) can locate rows referencing a retired key without a full-table scan. (4) The repository's Upsert + UpdateMutable signatures take a closed-set Input tuple (<Table>VariableUpsert with Value/IsSecret/SecretProvider/SecretKeyID/SecretCiphertext) — keeps the signature narrow as new secret fields land and centralises the "nullable columns when is_secret=false" projection in one helper (nullableSecretColumns). (5) The OrganizationVariable struct (and siblings) gain the three encryption-at-rest fields as INTERNAL-only — never on the wire — and the LogValue redaction MUST be updated in the SAME edit to redact BOTH the plaintext AND the new ciphertext to output.Sentinel; an unstale slog record that captures the struct cannot leak either. (6) scanRow projection routes through sql.NullString for the nullable text columns (pgx-scan into *string rejects NULL). Five load-bearing rules for the service-layer seam: (1) Seal at the SERVICE layer, never the repository — Seal is a side-effecting call that depends on a runtime Provider not a *Tx, calling Seal BEFORE opening the transaction shortens the SQL critical section, and the repository stays pure SQL. The cleanest helper is a one-line projector (sealVariableValue) that takes the customer's input shape (<Table>VariableReplace) and returns the repository's tuple shape (<Table>VariableUpsert) — non-secret items pass through untouched, secret items go through Provider.Seal. (2) The Service's constructor (NewOrganizationVariableService) takes Provider as a REQUIRED dependency rejected at construction if nil — a production process cannot accidentally start with the at-rest seam disabled. (3) Patch path for sealed rows has THREE post-state cases the service must enumerate explicitly: (a) caller supplied a new value → use it verbatim; (b) caller supplied only is_secret AND the current row is_secret → Open the current ciphertext via Provider.Open to recover the plaintext, then re-Seal under the active key (the only correct way to re-key a secret without surfacing the plaintext to the caller); (c) otherwise → use the current row's `value` column. Skipping case (b) would either lose the secret bytes on a re-seal or write a "previously-sealed plaintext" string into the value column. (4) The demotion path (is_secret=true→false) reuses case (b) to recover the plaintext, then writes it back to the value column with NULL encryption columns — the ONLY customer path that surfaces a previously-sealed plaintext, and only as a deliberate operator action. (5) Seal failures surface as apierr.Internal with a value-free wrapped cause — a sealing error is a server-side problem, never customer input, and the wrap never echoes the plaintext. Six load-bearing rules for the integration-test surface: (1) The integration test file lives next to the existing repository tests as <table>_secrets_at_rest_test.go, uses testutil.RequireMigratedDB (skips cleanly when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres), and wires a REAL AES-256-GCM provider (mustAESGCM helper that mints a fresh 32-byte key per test so parallel tests cannot bleed key ids across each other). (2) Five pinned tests per scope: SealsSecretValuesAtRest (the load-bearing contract — secret value reaches Postgres ONLY as AES-GCM ciphertext under the active key id, value column is "", on-disk bytea does not contain the plaintext substring, ciphertext round-trips through Provider.Open, audit row never carries any of {plaintext, key name, hex ciphertext, key id, provider id}); PatchRotatesCiphertextOnEveryWrite (a patch that flips is_secret only on a sealed row Opens-then-Seals under a fresh nonce so on-disk bytes change between writes); PatchDemotesSecretToPlaintext (is_secret=true→false with no new value recovers the plaintext via Open and writes to the value column with NULL encryption columns); SchemaRejectsPlaintextSecretWrite (three direct INSERT attempts that would land a plaintext-secret row each surface a constraint violation whose error message names the constraint); LogValueRedactsCiphertextAndPlaintext (a structured log record that captures the variable struct redacts BOTH plaintext AND ciphertext to output.Sentinel while exposing provider id + key id). (3) For the existing tests, fixture-shape changes (seedOrganizationVariable wrote plaintext into the value column for is_secret=true rows) need updating IN PLACE rather than introducing a second helper — the new helper writes the encryption columns under the Plaintext provider's wire identifiers ("plaintext-v1" / "plaintext") for is_secret=true fixtures, so every legacy seedOrganizationVariable(..., true) call site keeps working. (4) Pair the fixture change with a package-level test helper (revealVariableValue) that projects OrganizationVariable.Value back to the plaintext for both shapes — for the Plaintext provider, ciphertext IS the plaintext bytes, so the helper is one branch (if IsSecret → string(SecretCiphertext) else v.Value). Tests that previously asserted v.Value == "expected" become revealVariableValue(v) == "expected" with no further restructuring. (5) ActorKind in audit-fed test inputs MUST be one of {"", "usr", "sa", "system"} — the audit_events.actor_kind CHECK constraint will reject "user" / "human" / any other value as a 23xxx that surfaces as a misleading "an audit event with this id already exists" via mapWriteError's canonical conflict message. The actual diagnostic is the actor_kind CHECK, not duplicate id. (6) When extracting Value/Plaintext assertions out of failing tests against a contract change, FIRST stash the working tree and re-run the failing tests on HEAD to separate "I introduced this failure" from "this was already failing"; the diff of failure name sets is the cleanest signal that the new code is at parity with the baseline. BE-0339 used this to confirm 23 of the 25 store-wide failures and 3 of the 12 org-variable failures were pre-existing. NOT applicable to BE-0343 (variable inheritance resolver) — that story consumes the at-rest seam through Provider.Open against multiple scopes simultaneously and needs its own pattern around the merge order + per-scope Open call site. NOT applicable to BE-0351 (secret encryption key rotation) — that story consumes the partial index organization_variables_secret_routing_idx + the KeyIDs() / ActiveKeyID() surface to enumerate rows referencing a retired key and re-Seal them, but the rotation worker is a separate operational concern from the at-rest write path this pattern owns.
ralph/progress.txt:126:- Per-service-type renderer-subtype contract pin (BE-0301 pattern, internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_application_git_test.go; structural template for BE-0303 application-image, BE-0305 application-drop-artifact, BE-0307 compose, BE-0309..0317 database engines, BE-0319 worker, BE-0321 cron — every `provisioning-service-type` PRD story whose durable provisioning worker / dokploy_refs / quota-conversion seam is deferred to a later worker story but whose declarative-content contract is owed today): single t.Parallel test file in package dokploy_test, anchored on two per-subtype input-helpers (applicationGitDockerfileInput / applicationGitNixpacksInput) that reuse the shared validInput()/fixedID()/mustRender() helpers from renderer_test.go so canonical-id + per-level secret fixtures stay in lockstep with every other renderer pin. Seven load-bearing pins per subtype: (1) subtype-identity happy path per builder variant (asserts Service.Type, Build.Builder, builder-specific fields preserved verbatim, and the subtype boundary — the foreign-builder-only field stays empty); (2) DropsXField subtype-boundary test that submits the foreign-builder field and proves both renderer-drop AND that the submitted value never appears in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String() — the structural barrier against the SIBLING subtype story (BE-0301 vs BE-0303 boundary: Image dropped for dockerfile/nixpacks; BE-0303 will mirror with a DropsGitField test pinning git_branch/commit/dockerfile_path drop for builder=image); (3) RoleMatrix across (web / worker / cron) × (dockerfile / nixpacks builder) — closure-captured builder name in `string(builderInput.Build.Builder)` keeps subtest names readable; (4) ValidationFailures table per subtype using stable wantField + per-row leakyValues — every leaked-value candidate the mutate() introduces is added to a shared leakyValues slice so the no-echo assertion runs against ALL submitted values per case, not just the row's own input; (5) TierResourceDefaults pin across (tier × role × builder) — pins the load-bearing 2-replica production web default + 1-replica everywhere-else default and proves the builder choice does NOT affect resource allocation; (6) RedactsAllVariableValuesAcrossRoles that asserts BOTH (a) the verbatim RenderedSpec carries the secret value the worker needs AND (b) every debug surface (Summary JSON, slog JSON record, Summary.String()) shows output.Sentinel for EVERY variable value — Secret-flagged AND plain — so a future logging path cannot turn the non-secret variable channel into an exfiltration vector; (7) Determinism per builder. The pattern is the renderer-layer counterpart to the engine-pin pattern used for admin-action policy matrices (BE-0285 / BE-0288 / BE-0291 / BE-0294 / BE-0297 / BE-0300) — a state-independent file that pins the contract the future worker-side story must continue to satisfy. The acceptance-criteria coverage map per story: AC1 (renderer supports + validates required fields) is pinned by the success + validation table; AC2 (provisioning worker + dokploy_refs atomic) defers to the worker story but the renderer-side determinism + drop-foreign-field invariants are the structural barrier the worker depends on; AC3 (quota reserved before enqueue) defers — already pinned generically by BE-0323's projectservice + quota.Checker integration; AC4 (fake Dokploy create/update/deploy/poll/failure/retry/replay) defers to the worker story; AC5 (yalla.output.v1 / yalla.error.v1 envelopes) is already pinned globally by apienvelope/apienvelope_test.go — the renderer file just confirms apierr.InvalidInput surfaces; AC6 (unit tests for success/validation/auth/not-found) is pinned at the renderer layer for success+validation, auth+not-found defer to the wire-endpoint story; AC7 (integration tests against migrated Postgres) is the worker story's surface; AC8 (secrets redacted in logs/errors/audit/test output) is pinned per-role per-builder by the RedactsAllVariableValuesAcrossRoles permutation. NOT applicable to subtypes whose subtype-boundary lives outside the dokploy renderer's BuildSettings switch (e.g. BE-0305 application-drop-artifact will need a new builder constant first and a sibling pin that mirrors but also pins the new builder's renderer contract; BE-0309..0317 database engines pin Engine instead of Builder and the role field is dropped — those will follow the database variant of this template).
ralph/progress.txt:127:- BE-0303 application-image confirms the inverted-boundary clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_application_image_test.go): a single-builder subtype (Builder == BuilderImage) collapses the BE-0301 builderCase outer loop to a single role-only loop, swaps DropsImageField for DropsGitFields (submits leaky dockerfile_path + git_branch + git_commit and asserts every one is dropped AND never appears in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String()), and adds the image-builder-missing-image case to the ValidationFailures table (the case BE-0301 cannot pin because git-source builders never require an Image). The leakyValues slice grows to include the rendered Image reference so an echoed image-builder error also fails. The renderer's normaliseBuild() switch already encodes both invariants — Image is required for BuilderImage and every git-source field is dropped — so BE-0303 ships test-only (no production code change) as a structural barrier against accidental weakening. The same single-builder collapse applies to future BE-0319 worker (Role fixed to RoleWorker, drops domains) and BE-0321 cron (Role fixed to RoleCron, drops domains, requires cron_schedule) sibling pins.
ralph/progress.txt:128:- BE-0319 worker is the FIRST role-axis clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_worker_test.go): when the per-PRD-story subtype identity is the Role (Role == RoleWorker) rather than Build.Builder, Service.Type, or Service.Engine, the renderer-subtype pin generalises with four structural swaps from the canonical Builder-axis template. (1) The two input helpers (workerApplicationInput / workerComposeInput) span the two ServiceType variants where the worker role applies (application + compose), mirroring how the BE-0301 helpers span two builder variants — the orthogonal axis becomes ServiceType because the role is now the fixed axis. Both helpers explicitly clear Domains and CronSchedule in their bases, so the "submit a foreign-axis value and prove the drop" assertion stays load-bearing. (2) The DropsXField pin (TestRenderWorkerDropsWebAndCronFields) submits BOTH a leaky domain host and a leaky cron schedule in one test, proving the worker drops TWO foreign-axis fields at once (web's Domains + cron's CronSchedule); the same Summary-JSON / slog-record / Summary.String() redaction sweep used by BE-0303 catches leaks of either value. This is the role-axis analogue of "git fields dropped for image builder" — the structural barrier against any future refactor that would weaken the silent drops at lines `cronSchedule = ""` and the "rendered only for web" Domains contract. (3) The BuilderMatrix subtest takes the place of the BE-0301 RoleMatrix: since Role is now the fixed axis, the orthogonal axes become (ServiceType × Builder) yielding 2×4=8 subtests that prove every supported builder works on every supported service type while preserving the worker shape (no domains, no cron, no replica bump). The check closure per builder stays mechanical — same pattern as BE-0303's single-builder verification but generalised to a four-builder set. (4) The ValidationFailures table deliberately OMITS the cron_schedule legs (missing schedule, bad schedule) because a worker is never a cron service — those legs are owned by the role's own pin (BE-0301/0303/0305/0307 already cover them via web-role inputs flipped to cron, and BE-0321 will own them on the cron-role side). The TierResourceDefaults pin shrinks from a 6-row (tier × {web, worker}) matrix to a 3-row (tier-only) matrix because the worker role is fixed; every row pins replicas=1, the INVERSE of BE-0301/BE-0307's production-web replica bump. Production code change: zero — the renderer already drops Domains and CronSchedule for non-web/non-cron roles. The role-axis pin is the FOURTH structural template in the renderer-subtype family after the Builder axis (BE-0301/0303/0305), the ServiceType axis (BE-0307), and the Engine axis (BE-0309..0317). BE-0321 (cron) will be the FIFTH role-axis variant, cloning this file with a 3-token swap (Worker→Cron in identifiers, RoleWorker→RoleCron, label value) plus one structural addition: a CronScheduleValidation leg in ValidationFailures, since the cron role IS subject to cron_schedule validation while the worker role is not — the inverse omission to the one this file documents.
ralph/progress.txt:129:- BE-0307 compose is the FIRST type-boundary clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_compose_test.go): when the per-PRD-story subtype identity is the ServiceType ITSELF (Service.Type == ServiceCompose) rather than the Build.Builder, the seven-pin template is preserved with three structural swaps. (1) Input helpers (composeDockerfileInput / composeNixpacksInput) carry the SAME two git builders as application-git — the boundary is NOT what the builder is — and the only difference vs applicationGit*Input is `in.Service.Type = dokploy.ServiceCompose`. (2) The Drops* test becomes a SubtypeBoundaryFromApplication test that renders BOTH composeDockerfileInput() AND applicationGitDockerfileInput() side-by-side and asserts each rendered Service.Type matches its input verbatim — neither is silently coerced — AND that the `yalla.service-type` label in spec.Service.Labels reflects the same value, because that label is the audit/observability surface a downstream consumer queries to distinguish a compose stack from a single-container application without re-reading the spec body; an alternate seam-coverage shape preserves the structural barrier when there is no foreign-builder-only field to drop. (3) The ValidationFailures table expands to cover EVERY builder's failure path that the subtype can reach — compose may use any of dockerfile/nixpacks/image/drop-artifact, so BE-0307's table includes BOTH the image-builder-missing-image case (from BE-0303) AND the drop-artifact-builder-missing-artifact-url case (from BE-0305) PLUS the bad-builder case — the leakyValues slice grows to carry the rendered Image AND ArtifactURL references so any echo from those branches fails too. The RoleMatrix / TierResourceDefaults / Redaction / Determinism pins keep the seven-pin shape intact, but RoleMatrix and the per-test invariants assert `Service.Type == ServiceCompose && Image == "" && ArtifactURL == "" && Engine == ""` (engine is database-only — that is the OTHER per-type seam BE-0309..0317 will pin). The load-bearing production-web 2-replica default participates here too: defaultResources() in renderer.go gates the bump on `(typ == ServiceApplication || typ == ServiceCompose)`, so TestRenderComposeTierResourceDefaults pins the compose half of that disjunction directly — a future regression that narrowed the disjunction to ServiceApplication-only would flip this pin. Forward-applicable to ANY future per-service-type subtype whose differentiator is the ServiceType itself (currently only compose vs application; future "function" / "edge" / similar would clone this template); NOT applicable to the BE-0309..0317 database engines, which pin Engine instead of either ServiceType or Builder, drop the role field entirely (effectiveRole := "" for ServiceDatabase in renderer.go:351), and need a database variant whose RoleMatrix collapses to a single (no-role) case and whose Engine-required + Image-dropped invariants supersede the Build.Builder switch's normalisation. The test-only-no-prod-code-change shape from BE-0303 is preserved — BE-0307 ships purely as a structural barrier against accidental weakening of the renderer's existing service-type discrimination.
ralph/progress.txt:139:- Repository tenant-isolation for a tenant-root table (BE-0426 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/organization_tenant_isolation_test.go; structural template for BE-0428/0430/0432/0434/0436 and every future tenant-root-table isolation story): the strongest isolation guarantee in the codebase lives on the row-id-keyed tenant root (organizations) and the proof shape is "byte-identical snapshot of the OTHER tenant across every mutation path the repository exposes". Skeleton: one Go test file per table, one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) — Get returns-only-named-tenant + Get unknown-id-is-NotFound (cross-tenant + missing render the same yerr.CodeNotFound — no oracle); Update only-touches-named-tenant (snapshot orgB, Update orgA, re-read orgB, assert byte-identical across slug, display_name, version, updated_at, deletion_scheduled_at; assert orgA's update DID land so a no-op repository can't pass trivially); Update-with-mismatched-If-Match also-does-not-touch-orgB (catches "fall back to unconditional UPDATE on stale" regressions); ScheduleDeletion only-touches-named-tenant (stamp on orgA, orgB byte-identical incl. DeletionScheduledAt == nil); Get-of-soft-deleted-tenant-does-not-mask-other (soft-delete is per-row, not SQL-level hide; the scheduled row is still readable and orgB unchanged); Insert enforces-global-slug-uniqueness (typed yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty user-facing Message; first row's slug unchanged; second row's id is yerr.CodeNotFound on follow-up Get — the failed Insert lands no row). Three load-bearing rules: (1) anchor on BOTH version (trigger-bumped on every UPDATE so a missing WHERE bumps every row) AND updated_at (trigger-refreshed; same property) — either one catches a WHERE-less UPDATE independently of the other, keep both; (2) the "stale-If-Match" leg is structurally distinct from the "successful update" leg and exercises the conflict-detection path which historically falls back to extra reads/writes that could leak across tenants; (3) Insert conflict-message assertions anchor on yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty Message ONLY, never on a substring (the static "an organization with this slug already exists" message is a stable contract but story-specific prose checks are brittle). Tenant-root tables have NO ListByParent method at this layer (no parent to scope by) — the "list queries don't leak total counts" acceptance criterion is vacuously satisfied for tenant-root tables; the corresponding proof for /v1/me/organizations lives in membership tests. Document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test. CHILD-TABLE variant (BE-0428+ for users/memberships/projects/environments/services/api_keys/etc.) extends this skeleton with one additional leg: a cross-tenant parent_id probe — passing org A's parent_id (project_id, etc.) alongside org B's organization_id must render NotFound, NEVER A's row, NEVER a 500 leaking the cause. Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestXxxRepository' to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset, so CI without Postgres remains green. Known pre-existing -race-with-DB failures in environment-service / api-key-service Create paths and organization_variable_patch audit metadata are NOT introduced by this pattern (confirmed by stashing the new test file and re-running each failing case); a focused triage story should pick them up before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
ralph/progress.txt:152:- Replace-grants writer vertical (BE-0139 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant.go + httpapi/project_grants.go): a PUT-replace endpoint on a (parent, scoped-children) resource family pairs the existing reader-port adapter with a unit-of-work writer (ProjectGrantService) modeled exactly on OrganizationVariableService. The repository grows two new methods: Upsert(tx, id, organizationID, projectID, principalID, principalKind, role, *envID, *svcID) (ProjectGrant, error) using `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, project_id, principal_id, COALESCE(environment_id, ''), COALESCE(service_id, ''))` so the customer-mutable `role` is the only column the UPDATE branch touches (the immutable scope tuple + the bump_version trigger handle the rest); DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(tx, organizationID, projectID, keepIDs) renders the empty-keepIDs case as an unconstrained DELETE (an explicit clear is a meaningful customer operation), the non-empty case as `NOT (id = ANY($3::text[]))` so the bulk delete-by-exclusion is one round trip — same shape as DeleteByOrganizationExceptKeys but anchored on the row id (which Upsert returns from RETURNING) rather than a customer-supplied key, so an upsert that mutates an existing row deterministically keeps the survivor's id. The service Replace runs inside one Store.Write tx: project existence Get (a missing parent is typed NotFound, never a generic FK conflict), per-grant Upsert collecting the persisted ids into keepIDs, DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(keepIDs), audit.Append, post-write ListByProject re-read — every step shares the *Tx so a partial replace and an orphaned audit row are both impossible. Validation lives in buildProjectGrantReplace (split out, unit-testable without a DB): closed-set checks on principal_kind / role mirror the table's CHECK constraints, structural checks reject blank/NUL ids and a service-scope-without-environment, in-memory dedup uses `principalID + "\x00" + COALESCE(envID,'') + "\x00" + COALESCE(svcID,'')` to match the SQL unique index byte-for-byte. Audit metadata records counts only — `grant_count`, `project_scope_count`, `environment_scope_count`, `service_scope_count` — never principal ids, role names, or environment/service identifiers; per-row mutation history is the GET /v1/projects/{project_id}/grants surface's job. The HTTP layer adds a ProjectGrantReplacer port mirroring OrganizationVariableReplacer, a strict-decoded replaceProjectGrantsRequest with Grants *[]projectGrantRequest (pointer-to-slice so missing field is structurally distinguishable from an explicit empty array — a missing field is a stable 400, an empty array reaches the store), and the handler clones caller-supplied *string EnvironmentID/ServiceID pointers before forwarding them so the store cannot observe the caller's backing memory. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectGrantReplacer ProjectGrantReplacer just after projectGrants ProjectGrantReader); a Python regex sweep over 27 test files anchored on `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, nil)` handled the bulk of the NewHandler churn (43 call sites), with three follow-up patterns hand-fixed: `, logger)` in server_test.go and me_contract_test.go (NewHandler with non-nil logger), `, nil)` newRouteTable call (different return type), and the 29-nil all-nil newRouteTable pattern in six test files (added one trailing nil for the new port). The story implicitly delivers BE-0439's CRUD repository acceptance criteria (Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptIDs are the missing half) and the writer side of BE-0440's tenant-isolation criteria.
ralph/progress.txt:171:- Optimistic concurrency on mutable customer-data tables: migration 0011 adds a bigint version column (DEFAULT 1, CHECK > 0) + bump_version() BEFORE UPDATE trigger to organizations/projects/environments/services. The trigger overwrites NEW.version with OLD.version+1 on every UPDATE (including no-op data UPDATEs), so the application cannot freeze the sequence. Repository UPDATE methods accept ifMatchVersion *int64: nil = no precondition, non-nil = WHERE id=$1 AND version=$N; on no rows, a tenant-scoped Get inside the same tx disambiguates "row gone" (NotFound) from "row stale" (apierr.ConflictStale carrying the row's current version). Service-layer Update/ScheduleDeletion also pre-check the version after the read so a no-op data patch still surfaces the conflict against the right row. The HTTP layer parses RFC 7232 strong ETag values (`"<n>"`, lenient unquoted form accepted; weak/wildcard/multi-value rejected as 400 E_INVALID_INPUT) from If-Match, mirrors the row's authoritative version into the ETag response header on every success path (GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE), and surfaces stale writes as 409 E_CONFLICT carrying current_version under the new structured error.details map (apierr.DetailKeyCurrentVersion). yerr.Error.Details (map[string]string, set by WithDetail) is the agent-friendly counterpart to Hint; apienvelope renders it through the same regex redaction backstop used for Message/Hint.
ralph/progress.txt:192:- HTTP idempotency (0009_idempotency_keys, store/idempotency.go, httpapi/idempotency.go): mutating endpoints are made retry-safe by httpapi.RequireIdempotency(IdempotencyStore, ttl) — middleware installed INSIDE RequireAuth (it reads policy.PrincipalFromContext to scope the key to a principal within a tenant). Safe methods and unsafe requests with no Idempotency-Key pass straight through (opt-in). The claim protocol: store.IdempotencyRepository.Claim is a WHERE-guarded upsert — INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE ... WHERE expires_at <= now() — so it freshly claims, transparently steals an expired row (claimed=true), or returns the existing live claim (claimed=false, RETURNING yields no row, follow-up Find reads the row the DO UPDATE attempt locked). On claimed=true the middleware runs the handler against a buffering responseCapture, then Complete records the rendered envelope (status < 500) or Release drops the claim (status >= 500, so a server failure is retried not replayed). On claimed=false: hash mismatch -> apierr.IdempotencyConflict (E_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT, the only use of the new code), completed -> replay the recorded body+status with Idempotency-Replayed: true, still pending -> apierr.Conflict "already in progress". The request hash is sha256(method\npath?query\nbody); the body is buffered then restored as a fresh r.Body so the handler still reads it. Keys are scoped UNIQUE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key); a completion CHECK ties status='completed' to a non-null response. The store-backed IdempotencyStore adapter (httpapi.NewIdempotencyStore) runs each repo call in its own Store.Write — RequireIdempotency is implemented and tested but not yet wired into a route (no mutating endpoints exist yet), same as RequireAuth.
ralph/progress.txt:220:- Deep-resource PATCH pattern (BE-0127, PATCH /v1/projects/{project_id}, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects.go updateProjectHandler): sub-organization PATCH endpoints reuse the deep-resource resolver (projectIDResolver) to authorize against the (principal home organization, {project_id}) resource the path names — same resolver, same RequiredAction (policy.ActionProjectUpdate), and the store call is built from principal.OrganizationID and r.PathValue("project_id"), NEVER from the request body. The request DTO exposes ONLY the partially-updatable fields (Slug *string, DisplayName *string); the strict JSON decoder rejects an unknown organization_id field as a stable 400 E_INVALID_INPUT, so a body-injected org_id can never reach the store layer. Optimistic concurrency is bolted on by reading the optional If-Match header through the existing parseIfMatchVersion (the same parser organizations PATCH/DELETE already uses, accepting `"<n>"` strong ETag and the lenient unquoted form), forwarding the *int64 to the store as IfMatchVersion, and mirroring the returned row's authoritative version back into the ETag response header on success via writeOrganizationETag — the helper is generic in body despite the historical name, so the same call shape works for projects, future environments, services, etc. The handler stays thin: read principal, parse If-Match, validate.DecodeJSON, call port, render. The orchestrator (ProjectService.Update) follows the OrganizationService.Update shape exactly: validate input BEFORE opening the tx (buildProjectUpdate + validateProjectDisplayName are split out, unit-testable without a DB, and reject an empty patch as 400 InvalidInput), then in ONE Store.Write tx Get current, version pre-check (current.Version != *IfMatchVersion -> apierr.ConflictStale(current.Version)), apply non-nil fields, repo.Update(tx, desired, IfMatchVersion), audit.Append — a denial at any step rolls every other step back, so a row mutation and its audit record commit together or not at all. The store layer files the audit event under the actor's home organization with Metadata={"updated_fields": "slug,display_name"} — stable wire field names only, never the submitted values, so the audit trail records the SHAPE of the mutation without leaking input. classifyProjectConcurrencyMiss mirrors classifyOrganizationConcurrencyMiss: on the version-checked UPDATE matching zero rows, a tenant-scoped Get inside the same tx disambiguates "row gone" (NotFound) from "row stale" (ConflictStale carrying the row's current version) — the Get itself is tenant scoped, so a cross-tenant id still surfaces as NotFound and cannot reveal another tenant's data. Pre-existing test failures gotcha: TestAPIKeyServiceCreate* and TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch* fail on HEAD due to unrelated pre-existing issues with the testutil.Factory's id/slug formats vs the production-grade ParseSlug validator; verified by stashing the BE-0127 change and reproducing the failures on the base commit. These are NOT caused by BE-0127 and are out of scope.
ralph/progress.txt:223:- Bare top-level lookup-by-id endpoint for a deep resource (BE-0154 pattern, GET /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go GetByID + GetEnvironment, httpapi/environments.go): a customer-facing route that addresses a deep resource (env, service, deployment) by its global id WITHOUT carrying its parent ids in the path. Two load-bearing constraints: (1) the resolver builds policy.Resource{Scope: {OrganizationID = principal.home, <ResourceLevel>ID = path.id}} — pinning OrgID to the principal's home (NEVER the caller-supplied id) is the structural anti-spoof, and pinning the resource-level id is the only scope leg the path actually carries; (2) the resolver CANNOT pin the parent ids without a DB lookup, and the policy engine's Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) requires every level the grant pins to match the resource — a grant with pinned ProjectID against a resource with empty ProjectID is structurally denied. Accepted design: middleware admits org-wide read roles + org-wide grants + (for CapRead actions) the support cross-tenant exception is suppressed because the resolver pins OrgID to principal.home; project-/env-/service-scoped grants are denied at the boundary by design, and grant-only principals must use the parent-scoped /v1/projects/{project_id}/environments instead. Document this in the route description, the resolver doc, and the handler doc — readers will misinterpret the structural deny as a bug otherwise. The store layer adds a tenant-scoped GetByID(ctx, q, organizationID, resourceID) on the existing repository (composite (organization_id, id) predicate so cross-tenant returns apierr.NotFound, never another tenant's row, never a 500 leaking the cause), and the existing reader struct grows a Get<Resource>(ctx, organizationID, resourceID) method that wraps GetByID inside Store.Read. The HTTP layer adds a NEW narrow port (distinct from the existing parent-scoped port — e.g. EnvironmentReader vs ProjectEnvironmentReader — so a handler change to one doesn't ripple through the other), a NEW resolver function (e.g. environmentIDResolver), and a NEW handler that REUSES the existing wire-shape projection (projectEnvironmentOf for environments) so the GET-by-id response is byte-identical to one element of the LIST response (modulo the wrapping object name). cmd/yalla-api/main.go passes the same *store.<Resource>Reader value twice — once for the parent-scoped port, once for the new bare-lookup port — because both methods live on the same store struct; no second startup wiring is needed. The contract tests cover the standard envelope: happy path, NotFound (cross-tenant + unknown both render as the same 404 — no oracle), 401 unauthenticated + invalid credentials (no token echo), 503 store outage (no raw cause leak), 500 nil-port wiring guard, envelope shape lock (asserts the documented field set against a generic decode so a future struct column added without updating the projection is caught), bearer-token never-logged guard (slog over a buffer), OpenAPI route registration, and TWO resolver unit tests (pins home org + path id; no panic with no principal on context). The matching policy matrix story (BE-0156 for environments, the structural template for service/deployment lookups) tests the deny — the deny matrix on the bare endpoint is part of the contract, not a gap.
ralph/progress.txt:233:- POST create endpoint for a service-scoped child resource (BE-0250+0251+0252 pattern, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups, mirrors the service_domains POST trio BE-0238+0239+0240; clonable for BE-0264 previews, BE-0289 import previews, and any future bare-id child create): the store-layer composition is the canonical create-unit-of-work shape — ServiceXxxRepository.Insert (single-statement INSERT...RETURNING + mapWriteError mapping driver errors to typed apierr.Conflict whose message is value-free) + ServiceXxxService.Create orchestrator that opens a Store.Write Tx, runs the tenant-scoped parent existence check first (so a cross-tenant or unknown parent id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound, NEVER a 500 or silent success), runs an in-tx Authorize (defence-in-depth against grant-change races between the HTTP authorize and the desired-state write — the ONLY operation that runs in-tx Authorize; the in-transaction Authorizer is reserved for Create), runs the Insert, and Appends the immutable audit record — all inside the same *Tx so a row can never be persisted without its audit trail. Validation runs BEFORE the Tx is opened (validateCreateXxxInput against a shared validate.Collector so a request rejecting on more than one field surfaces every violation in one typed apierr.InvalidInput; submitted values NEVER appear in violation reasons). The customer-facing create pins any lifecycle status column to a fixed initial value (e.g. ServiceBackupStatusPending) so a customer cannot forge a 'succeeded' / 'completed' status through the public API surface — the worker is the authority for closed-taxonomy transitions; the status column is NOT in the createXxxRequest decoder shape and the validator overwrites in.Status in the validated struct. The audit Metadata records ONLY fields the application layer can prove redaction-safe by construction: structural identifiers (service_id), closed-taxonomy enums (status, certificate_type), and bounded primitives (retention_count, version, enabled flag rendered as "true"/"false"); caller-supplied free text (display_name, schedule, hostname even — schedule is a cron-style expression but is still caller-controlled bytes) is NEVER recorded verbatim, because a customer pasting a token into one of those fields is exactly the footgun a defensive convention avoids. The httpapi-layer composition is the canonical create-handler shape — createXxxRequest decoder (Enabled / boolean-default-true fields are pointers to preserve the absent-vs-explicit-false distinction at the JSON seam — `*bool` decoded; an omitted field collapses to the default at the handler boundary, an explicit `false` rides through unchanged; integer fields take their schema-side default when zero) + createXxxPayload + createXxxHandler that gates on RequireAuth + the typed-internal nil-creator guard + validate.DecodeJSON (strict, max-body-bytes, unknown-field rejection, no input echo on parse failure) + creator.Create + http.StatusCreated yalla.output.v1 envelope projecting through serviceXxxOf. Action is CapWrite (ActionXxxCreate -> CapWrite in catalog.go): owner / admin / developer allowed by role; CI / viewer / support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — load-bearing distinction: a CapDeploy sibling action (e.g. ActionBackupRun, ActionDeploymentCreate) WOULD admit CI. The wire route uses serviceIDResolver (no parent_project_id leg pinned on the resource scope), so EVERY project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied at the boundary via the engine's one-way covers() rule (ReasonDeniedOutOfScope); only an organization-wide write role or an org-level write grant reaches the handler. Cross-tenant: the resolver pins resource scope to the principal's home org id (never the path service's tenant), so cross-tenant service_ids hit the persistence layer with the home org id and surface as a deterministic apierr.NotFound — engine cross-tenant deny is pinned at the engine level (defence-in-depth: EVERY role including Support is denied cross-tenant for CapWrite — the support cross-tenant exception covers only CapRead / CapSupport). Contract test surface: success with full plumbing assertions (principal home org id forwarded, path service id forwarded, actor id forwarded, no caller-body org id trusted), boolean-default-true and explicit-false at the pointer seam, request_id propagation, validation failure propagated from creator as E_INVALID_INPUT (handler delegates validation to the store-layer orchestrator), strict-decode unknown-field rejection BEFORE creator runs, unauthenticated 401, role-deny 403 (handler never reached), cross-tenant 404 with principal home org forwarded, duplicate-id Conflict 409, store-unavailable 503, nil-creator typed Internal 500. Policy matrix test surface mirrors service_domains_create_policy_test.go one-for-one. Wiring footprint: adds ONE new positional arg (ServiceXxxCreator) to newRouteTable (~slot 57) and NewHandler (~slot 60); ~70 test files / ~120 call sites need a `fakeServiceXxxCreator{}` insertion via bulk substitution `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` -> `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` (where Yyy is the sibling Reader and Zzz is the next port after the new slot in the signature); the special cases are the in-package contract-test helper for the new resource (`reader` -> `..., reader, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, ...`) and any sibling test where the local `reader` variable is bound to a DIFFERENT port type that happens to share an adjacent slot (e.g. service_variables_test.go's `reader` is a ServiceVariableReader, NOT a backup reader, so its slot ordering needs a separate substitution). Re-runnable via a small Python script that pattern-matches and applies the three substitutions in order — see the BE-0232+0233+0234 description above for the ast-walking variant if a new arg needs to be inserted at a structural offset rather than between two named neighbours.
ralph/progress.txt:237:- BE-0309 postgres is the FIRST database-engine clone of the BE-0301/BE-0307 per-service-type renderer-subtype pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_database_postgres_test.go; structural template for BE-0311 mysql / BE-0313 mariadb / BE-0315 mongo / BE-0317 redis). The subtype discriminator is neither the Builder (BE-0301/0303/0305) nor the ServiceType alone (BE-0307) but the Engine VALUE on a ServiceDatabase service. Four structural swaps vs the BE-0307 type-boundary clone: (1) Input helper collapses from two (per-builder) to ONE (databasePostgresInput) — databases do not vary by builder because the renderer's normaliseBuild() still runs but its output is not the subtype's identity. The fixture deliberately submits Role=RoleWeb + non-empty Domains + a CronSchedule so the "drops role+domains+cron" subtype-boundary assertion proves the renderer's drop semantics by observation, not by zero-value passive absorption. (2) The Drops* test becomes a single DropsRoleDomainsAndCron pin that asserts each of the three application/compose-only fields is dropped AND that none of their submitted values appear in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String() (the customer-submitted hostname "db.acme.example" and cron schedule "0 2 * * *" are the canonical leak candidates) — same no-leak structural barrier as the BE-0307 boundary, applied to a different drop surface. (3) The SubtypeBoundaryFromApplication shape gains an engine-label dimension: the renderer adds a new conditional yalla.engine label only for ServiceDatabase services (mirroring how yalla.role is conditional on a non-empty role), so the boundary test asserts BOTH yalla.service-type (must be "database") AND yalla.engine (must be "postgres") on the postgres case AND that yalla.engine is ABSENT entirely on the application case — the new label is the audit/observability surface that distinguishes a postgres database from a future mysql/mongo/redis database without re-reading the spec body. (4) RoleMatrix collapses entirely to a no-role tier-only matrix (database services have effectiveRole := "" set unconditionally in renderer.go); TierResourceDefaults pins that databases do NOT participate in the production-web 2-replica bump (the disjunction in defaultResources() is `(typ == ServiceApplication || typ == ServiceCompose) && role == RoleWeb` — ServiceDatabase is excluded by both conjuncts), so every tier defaults to one replica regardless. The production-code seam this story introduces is the validEngine() closed whitelist in renderer.go: BE-0309 ships only EnginePostgres = "postgres"; BE-0311..0317 EACH add one engine constant and one line to validEngine's switch — purely additive, no per-engine validation logic changes. The Render() engine validation expands from a single "required for database services" gate to a switch that ALSO rejects unsupported engines with a stable Reason "must be a supported database engine" (no value echo — the message text never names the rejected engine, so the BE-0311..0317 stories that add engines do NOT need to change the message). The ValidationFailures table includes an "unsupported engine" case keyed on "sqlite" — chosen because Yalla will never manage sqlite (file-based, no engine to provision), so the case stays stable across the entire BE-0309..0317 family even as the whitelist grows. The Determinism + RedactsAllVariableValues pins are direct ports of the BE-0307 shape. database.golden in testdata/renderer/ needs the +yalla.engine=postgres label added (regenerable via `go test -count=1 -run TestRenderGolden ./internal/controlplane/dokploy -args -update-golden` — the -update-golden flag is registered as a real *flag.Bool via init() in internal/testutil/golden.go, so it MUST be passed AFTER `-args`, not as a top-level go-test flag, or the binary errors out with "flag provided but not defined"). The worker-side acceptance criteria (dokploy_refs atomic write, quota reservation-to-conversion, fake Dokploy create/update/deploy/poll/failure/retry/replay) all defer to the later database worker story per the BE-0301 deferral pattern. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0317 redis (BE-0311 mysql, BE-0313 mariadb, BE-0315 mongo all landed: validEngine extended to `case EnginePostgres, EngineMysql, EngineMariadb, EngineMongo`; renderer_database_{mysql,mariadb,mongo}_test.go each pin their per-engine contract; the closed-set extension is one comma + one identifier per remaining sibling): each sibling adds ONE engine constant + ONE line to validEngine's switch + a per-engine test file cloned from renderer_database_postgres_test.go with the Engine constant swapped in databasePostgresInput's analogue and the engine label assertion updated. NOT applicable to non-database service types or to subtypes whose discriminator is the Builder or the ServiceType.
ralph/progress.txt:242:- Dimensional (magnitude-axis) quota dimension on a Create unit of work (BE-0331 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/projectservice.go QuotaReserver port + internal/controlplane/quota/quota.go Checker + internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go serviceCPUMillicoresByKind + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_cpu_millicores_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0332 memory_mb / BE-0333 storage_gb / and every future quota dimension whose allocation is a numeric magnitude per request rather than a +1 count). The FIRST dimensional story widens the load-bearing seam ONCE for the entire dimensional-quota family: the store-layer QuotaReserver interface gains a second method `ReserveAmount(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string, amount int64) error`, and the real adapter quota.Checker implements it as the canonical lock + sum-active + insert-reservation path with the amount parameterised. The count-axis convenience `Reserve(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string) error` is refactored to a one-line delegate `return c.ReserveAmount(...,1)`, so every prior count-axis call site is byte-identical and proves the new path is structurally compatible with the old. amount<=0 is rejected as Internal (wiring error — release is a separate path, not a negative reservation). The Checker validates amount, plan, resource, and limit BEFORE acquiring any row lock so a bad amount cannot block another tenant. The unit-of-work call site (ServiceService.Create) sources the per-request magnitude from a per-Kind constant map (serviceCPUMillicoresByKind: application=250, compose=500, database=500) via a `cpuMillicoresForServiceKind(kind)` helper — the map lives at the store layer so the dependency graph does not reach into the dokploy renderer's tier-aware defaults. The ReserveAmount call runs in the SAME *Tx as the count-axis Reserve calls, the desired-state write, the job enqueue, and the audit append; ordering is "every count-axis Reserve first, then the dimensional ReserveAmount" so a tenant who simultaneously exhausts a count dimension AND the dimensional dimension sees the count-dimension Resource surface in ExceededDetail (the LAST Reserve that fires produces the error). Three fakes (noopQuotaReserver in cmd/yalla-api/main.go, nopQuotaReserver in store/internal_test.go, recordingQuota in store/projectservice_test.go) need a one-line ReserveAmount addition to satisfy the widened interface; no other fakes exist because the concurrent tests use the real Checker. Test pattern: clone the BE-0326 concurrent test, replace the count-axis assertions with magnitude-axis ones — (1) policy LIMIT is a multiple of the per-Kind magnitude (cpu_millicores=1000 = 4 × 250-millicore apps); (2) expectedSuccesses = limit / per-Kind-magnitude; (3) detail.Requested is asserted to equal the per-Kind magnitude (NOT 1) — a regression hard-coding it back to 1 fails closed here; (4) detail.Reserved is expectedSuccesses × magnitude; (5) the active-reservations SUM(amount) assertion replaces / complements COUNT(*) — COUNT alone cannot tell whether the magnitude reached the reservation row Amount column, SUM(amount) closes the gap. A second non-concurrent test creates services across multiple kinds (application=250, database=500), exhausts the dimension exactly, then proves both a compose-kind request (would-reserve 500) and an application-kind request (would-reserve 250) are rejected — and that the two rejections report DIFFERENT detail.Requested values. The single equality check `composeDetail.Requested != appDetail.Requested` is the cheapest possible proof the per-Kind map actually fans out instead of collapsing to one constant. A SQL GROUP BY amount on quota_reservations confirms the magnitude distribution end-to-end (2 rows at 250 + 1 row at 500 for the canonical fixture). After BE-0331, EVERY future dimensional quota story (BE-0332 memory_mb, BE-0333 storage_gb, BE-0335 monthly_deployments) only needs a per-Kind/per-trigger magnitude helper and ONE ReserveAmount call site — the port is already widened and the real Checker implementation does not need to change again. The Reserve(...) call site convention stays for every count-axis dimension; ReserveAmount(...) is reserved for dimensions whose amount actually varies per request.
ralph/progress.txt:246:- Pure N-scope inheritance resolver (BE-0343 pattern, internal/controlplane/variables/{doc.go, resolver.go, resolver_test.go, AGENTS.md}; structural template for any future hierarchy-aware merge engine over org/project/env/service — feature-flag inheritance, domain-config inheritance, rate-limit inheritance). Five load-bearing rules: (1) The resolver package owns NO I/O — it defines a scope-agnostic ScopedVariable input shape and a Resolver that operates purely on in-memory tuples. The caller fetches inputs through the per-scope *Reader adapters (internal/controlplane/store) and converts store types into the resolver's input shape. This decouples the merge contract from persistence and lets unit tests use hand-built fixtures without a fake DB. (2) Collect ALL field violations across ALL scopes in one pass and return them as one apierr.InvalidInput; do NOT short-circuit on the first violation — that is hostile to the agent contract. Field paths anchor on <scope>.variables[<index>].<field> and NEVER echo the submitted value. (3) Customer-input failures (invalid name, duplicate within scope, secret downgrade without opt-in) -> apierr.FieldViolation -> apierr.InvalidInput (HTTP 400). Server-side failures (Open ErrUnknownKey / wrapped ErrInvalidCiphertext / provider id mismatch) -> apierr.Internal with a wrapped cause for server-side logging (HTTP 500). The classification is load-bearing: customer-input means "fix your request"; Internal means "retry or escalate". (4) Three redaction views: (a) Resolved/Rendered are internal-only and carry verbatim plaintext for renderer/worker consumption; (b) LogValue redacts value and ciphertext at the slog boundary as defence-in-depth against stray slog captures; (c) Explained projects every value to output.Sentinel for customer-facing JSON envelopes, audit metadata, and operator diagnostics. Tests prove no error message, JSON marshal, or slog line contains source plaintext or ciphertext. (5) Closed-set Options.AllowSecretDowngrade opt-in: secret -> non-secret at a higher scope is REJECTED by default (apierr.InvalidInput); the opt-in path MUST be backed by a caller-emitted audit event recording the override. The asymmetry between upgrade (always allowed) and downgrade (rejected by default) is the safe direction — upgrading the classification strengthens the posture, downgrading weakens it. Two test-fixture gotchas the pattern documents: (a) whole-error redaction tests must choose fixture values that cannot collide with stable reason phrases (the first BE-0343 iteration used "duplicate" as a fixture value AND as part of the rejection reason — false positive); use distinctive suffixes like "fixture-value-one-zzy" to anchor the redaction invariant. (b) Tests against the Plaintext provider must use different strings for the plaintext-leak assertion and the ciphertext-leak assertion — Plaintext.Seal returns the input verbatim, so using the same string for both would tautology-pass under Plaintext but fail to catch a real leak under AESGCM. NOT applicable to the dokploy renderer's mergeVariables (renderer.go) — that path is intentionally separate because the renderer's Variable input is the deterministic spec, not the at-rest seal tuple, and it ALSO validates tier/role invariants. A future refactor could unify the two but is a distinct story (it changes the renderer's input contract).
