ralph/progress.txt:13748:- Implemented GET /v1/environments/{environment_id}/variables: an environment-scoped variable reader that closes the gap between project_variables (BE-0142) and a future service_variables surface, as the third-from-lowest precedence layer of the Org -> Project -> Env -> Service variable hierarchy the Dokploy renderer composes.
ralph/progress.txt:13771:  - Reader-only scoped-child vertical on a bare-id resolver (BE-0172, environment-variables): structural sibling of BE-0142 (project_variables) with TWO narrowings worth pinning. (1) The new resource (env-variables) inherits the same migration shape (composite FK to parent, scope-uniqueness index on (org, env, key), composite UNIQUE(org,id) as future-override FK target, version+bump_version+set_updated_at triggers); only the column names change (environment_id replaces project_id, no transitive project_id duplicated — environment_grants is the precedent). (2) The HTTP route uses environmentIDResolver (bare-id, no ProjectID leg) rather than projectIDResolver, so every scoped grant — including a grant on the SAME env_id named in the path — is denied OutOfScope by the engine's one-way covers() rule. The wire chokepoint (environmentVariable struct) still redacts secret values to output.Sentinel exactly as project_variables does; there is no wire-shape difference between the two resources because the agent's job is to enumerate environment-default variables, not to confirm a service's effective value (that's the rendered-service-variables endpoint's job, a separate future story).
ralph/progress.txt:13849:  - Acceptance criterion "Secrets, tokens, API keys, cookies, and rendered environment variable values are redacted in logs, errors, audit metadata, and test output" — pinned at three boundaries: (1) the wire chokepoint on the allow path (post-write re-read renders is_secret=true rows as output.Sentinel, never the canonical or caller-submitted plaintext); (2) envVarsDenyBodyLeak watches the canonical seeded plaintext (canonicalEnvVarsSecretPlaintext) on every deny path; (3) the new replaceEnvironmentVariablesRequestBodyLeak watches the caller-submitted secret needle (replaceEnvironmentVariablesMatrixSecretNeedle) on every deny path. The request-body leak guard is the load-bearing addition unique to env.write because env-grants rows carry no credential material — write endpoints whose request body carries plaintext secrets need a body-direction leak guard, not just a response-direction guard.
