ralph/progress.txt:19011:  - TestListServiceBackupsPolicyMatrixRoles: drives owner/admin/developer/viewer/ci/support through the production request path; pins the engine verdict at ReasonAllowedByRole; verifies the reader receives the principal's home org id and the path service_id; wire renders 200 with canonical row.
ralph/progress.txt:19013:  - TestListServiceBackupsPolicyWrongOrganizationPrincipal: a cross-tenant service_id reaches the reader with the principal's HOME org id (not the foreign target), the persistence layer returns NotFound, the wire renders 404 E_NOT_FOUND, and the error body never echoes the victim org id. Engine defence-in-depth subtests pin ReasonDeniedCrossTenant for every non-support role and ReasonAllowedBySupport for support (CapRead is inside the support cross-tenant exception).
ralph/progress.txt:19182:  - **The "no destructive op on deny" invariant is the load-bearing assertion that distinguishes DELETE policy tests from CREATE/UPDATE/RUN policy tests.** For backup.delete the policy suite asserts callCount == 0 on every deny path — the role-deny path, the disabled-principal path, the project/env/service-scoped grant path. A scoped principal denied on the wire MUST NOT have committed a destructive row removal in the background. This is structurally MORE load-bearing for DELETE than for any other mutation because the row's removal is irreversible at the application layer (the audit trail outlives it, but the desired-state row does not — there is no soft-delete column to clear, no version flip to undo). The body-leak guard (deleteServiceBackupDenyBodyLeak) needles for the canonical backup_id / display_name / schedule / project / environment so an accidental on-deny render of the "deleted" snapshot fails the test even before the deleter-not-reached assertion fires. The malformed-If-Match contract test ALSO asserts the no-run invariant: a malformed precondition that silently degraded into "no precondition" would be a particularly load-bearing footgun for a destructive op, so the assertion is doubly structural.
ralph/progress.txt:19215:  - The picker rule deviation: BE-0264 is P=1 policy on a not-yet-implemented endpoint, the canonical case for the BE-0244/0257/0258 three-story bundle. Previews are uniquely large (no Kind, no store, no migration, no CRUD service, no renderer hook beyond the existing TierPreview enum) so bundling would balloon to a 6+ file greenfield drop spanning domain/store/migration/policy/httpapi/openapi. The engine-only pin here is a deliberate, documented deviation: it lands the BE-0264 acceptance criteria where they are load-bearing (engine matrix) and explicitly defers the wire-level twin to BE-0262's bundle. Future iterations picking BE-0262 should land the full previews vertical (domain Kind, store, migration, CRUD service, handler, contract tests, wire-level policy test) as a multi-story bundle.
