idate (blank action / blank resource_kind / blank reason / actor_kind outside the closed-set ('', 'usr', 'sa', 'system')) MUST be exercised: Append does NOT pre-check these fields (the audit service is the chokepoint for the principal-mapping inputs), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces as typed `apierr.Conflict` via mapWriteError. Each test asserts BOTH the error code AND `countAuditRowsForOrg(org.ID) == 0` to pin "the rejection left no row behind"; (d) the repository-level limit cap (`auditEventListMaxLimit = 200`) is a public invariant of the ListByOrganization contract and MUST be exercised in three branches — non-positive (limit <= 0 -> 200), above-cap (limit > 200 -> 200), in-range (limit in (0, 200] honoured verbatim). A regression that returned the empty slice when limit <= 0 (or every row when limit was unbounded) would surface here. The seeding helper `seedAuditRowsRawSQL` bypasses the repository and uses direct INSERT with caller-controlled occurred_at so 201 rows can be planted in O(n) without sleeping; (e) the secondary-sort ORDER BY id DESC tiebreaker on tied occurred_at is the load-bearing list-ordering invariant: a UI rendering a pair of audit events written in the same Postgres microsecond would flicker without it. The probe seeds TWO rows with the SAME occurred_at (via raw INSERT — Append's DEFAULT now() would advance between calls) and asserts listed[0].ID > listed[1].ID lexicographically. The 16-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + aud_ prefix + server-side timestamps within [before, after]), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-ignores-caller-supplied-timestamps (the load-bearing server-side-timestamp probe), four CHECK-violation Conflicts (blank-action, blank-resource_kind, blank-reason, unknown-actor_kind), duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-transaction-rollback (sentinel error + countAuditRowsForOrg == 0), Append-cascades-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/organization_id/action/reason/occurred_at/created_at), Append-peer-row-byte-identical (the BE-0468 byte-identity bystander predecessor — peer Append must NOT touch a prior row), List-orders-id-DESC-on-timestamp-tie, three List-limit-clamping branches (non-positive, above-cap, in-range), List-unknown-org-returns-empty. New helpers (per-file Audit-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg (the per-org counter — organization is the only parent FK for audit_events, so this is also the natural per-parent counter), mintAuditID, auditTxRollbackSentinel, seedAuditRowsRawSQL (the raw-INSERT batch seeder for cap-boundary tests). Reused verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit, assertAuditEventByteIdentical; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table is append-only with a single (Append, List) surface and a repository-level limit cap (a future security_events / system_audit table would inherit the same template, including the server-side-timestamp invariant if the INSERT column list omits the timestamp legs).
@@ -33526,3 +33527,14 @@ change that widened Reason to caller-supplied data.
   - `break_glass_sessions` tenant-isolation coverage should exercise both active and revoked rows because `MarkRevoked` is the only repository state transition.
   - The strongest no-leak probe for this table is an overlapping actor/request/correlation shape across tenants plus byte-identical raw row reloads for every foreign-tenant bystander.
